      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MODO UCT ALL'S PATHHT SELF-11.AISIN6 FLOUI lIMELTsII:Y THE EX A MINER THURS Y VIA RCI-1 27 I 902 N I [ALL RIGHTS RESERVHD.]</head>
        <p>MODO UCT ALL'S PATHHT SELF-11.AISIN6 FLOUI lIMELTsII:Y THE EX A MINER THURS Y VIA RCI-1 27 I 902 N I [ALL RIGHTS RESERVHD.]</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>81-7 DEAR NELLIII,</p>
        <p>1 have noticed that coiffures get prettier. There are endless varieties in vogue, but a very pretty coiffure in the low style of hairdressing shows the hair lightly waved and drawn down to the back, where it is arranged in a twisted knot just resting on the neck. The front hair is turned back over two side frizettes, and turned in beneath the pads, and in the centre a small pouff curl is rolled on a pad and arranged to form a high centre. A few light curls of hair rest in front, and the whole effect is a decided improvement on previous exam- ples of this style.</p>
        <p>The display of spring hats is especially varied and charming. They range in size from the picture hat, with a crown of roses, and brim draped with cream lace, to the black and white magpie toque, with turban brim and black and white osprey. The latter shape will be much favoured this season. Several of the smartest shops are displaying fine-burnt straw hats. They are in large fancy shaps, with the crown much wrinkled, the brim wavy on one side, and the other turned up in a curved line, very becoming to the face. One of these straws, which especially caught my fancy, was trimmed with black tulle, and one black ostrich feather following the curved line. This hat will prove a useful addition to any woman's wardrobe, as it will stand retrimming again and again. Tulle is now used much more than chiffon, as it will survive the daily risk of rain and rough weather. The hats for weddings and smart occasions are even more of the flower- garden style than those of last season. Many of them are composed entirely of flowers and leaves, just relieved by a knot of velvet repeating the shades of colours.</p>
        <p>For the coming season lace in every variety of shape and form will be more than ever worn. The clinging lace frocks, which set off every line of a beautiful figure to the best advantage, will be still to the fore for evening wear. Exquisite appliques of floral embroidery are being used at present for decorating lace or mousseline de soie evening frocks. The workmanship of these beautiful garnitures is most artistic. The colours of various 'flowers are skilfully carried out in the softest velvet and woollen materials or embroidered in silk with the needle. As soon as the weather permits we shall see a recurrence of the delicate flimsy frocks of last summer, varying from the cheap and pretty imitation lace now within the reach of modest purses to the priceless heirlgoais of the great lady.</p>
        <p>No matter how much dressmakers have tried to banish the familiar blouse, it is still as important a feature as ever in every woman's wardrobe. The changes end possibilities of the blouse are infinite. The clevr girl of the present day, who is limited to a small allowance, can make endless variations _</p>
        <p>in her toilette by means of the blouse if she is possessed of nimble fingers, and keeps her eyes open for pretty patterns. Now that all soft and clinging ratelials are so fashionable, the blouses of this spring will be mainly seen in crepe-de- chine, voile, the finest satin, mousseline de soie, and the new silk-finished linens, though taffetas will be by no means neglected. The trimmings are to be more elaborate this season. Tucks, in- sertions of every kind of lace, and appliques, are all brought into use for smart blouses. Many of the Lest shops are still displaying the delicato silk and satin slips with transparent lace yokes, so much worn last summer.</p>
        <p>For small occasional drawing room tables large mats are more in favour than all over covers, and these can be quickly and easily made of squares of brocaded silk edged with gold or silver galon, or of pieces of plain silk or satin with a spray of flowers worked in silks and spangles in each corner, and a trimming of galon or of little tufts of silk worked along the edge of the mat. Another pretty way of decorating these tiny table covers is with chintz appliqué, sprays of flowers being cut out of a chintz of artistic design, fastened down on a silk or satin ground, and nicely worked up with embroidery silks and spangles. Blotters and 'photograph frames, too, can be effectively covered with linen or silk deco- rated in this way. A charming design for a blouse is made of black taffeta; trimmed with point lace, picked out with gold cord and paillettes—the whole mounted on black velvet. The sleeves are very uncommon and picturesque. They are met by a band above the elbow of the same point, picked out with gold and mounted on black velvet, in the same way as the bodice. The sleeves widen out below the elbow, and are then drawn into cuffs trimmed with the lace and the black velvet. The same scheme can be carried out in pale blue mousseline' tucked. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Christian De Wet, alias John Brown, has been sentenced to seven days' hard labour at Stockport for begging. The Hastings Hydropathic, a large building situate in extensive grounds in Old Town, Has- tings, has been purchased by the Reparatrice, or Nuns of Perpetual Adoration, about 60 of whom are coming from France to reside there. The other afternoon a destructive fire occurred in the calico print combine works of Higgin- botham, Limited, McNeil-street, Glasgow. The fire broke out in the top fiat, stored with goods, which was burned, while the stock in the lower stories was greatly damaged by water. The loss is estimated at £20,000.</p>
        <p>THE HORRORS OF OLD TIME SURGERY.</p>
        <p>Let us imagine an amputation during the tenth century ! While half a dozen aosistants sat upon the patient and subdued his wild struggles, a surgeon, with a great knife shaped like a sickle, cut straight down through the skin and muscles of an injured leg, Nee s icing a sausage or a cucumber. There was no ehloro- form then. Chloroform is a modern luxury and was not rhscovered until the middle of the nineteeenth century. A man in those days had to shut his eyes, dig his nails into the palms of his hands, and bear the torture as best he could. An operating mem was truly a hell of anguish. No flaps of any kind were made in amputating—simply a great, gaping, bleeding wound, with the bone protrud- ing from its centre. And the bleeding was something awful.the blood epurtiug from severed vessels in drench- ing streams. Nothing was known of stopping the circulation by constriction of the limb with a tournivet and it was no uncommon thirg to see a poor, shricking wretch bleed to death upon the operating table. What a memory that mug have been to keep close to one during a dark night ! No wonder surgeons were often afraid to operate. SAWING AND FRIZZLING THE BONE. As soon as the bone had been hastily divided by a saw (how a saw grates on a bone), a red-hot iron was rubbrd again and again across the raw and quivering muscles—think of the agony of it—in order to Bear the open vessels and stop the terrible bleeding ; for the art of tying arteries with ligatures of thread did nct come into general use until the sixteenth century. Instead of using a hot iron, the stump was sometimes plunged into boiling oil, melted sulphur, liquid lead or beiliog water. You could take your choice. The ancient Hindus covered the raw and bleeding part with molten pitch. Such was the fear of bleeding to death that many in- genious expedients were employed to control it. A favourite remedy among learned men was to bold a toad in the hand until the animal became warm. But toads have an unreasenable tendency to remain cold, so the advocates cf this attractive mithod had always an ex- cuse ready in case the patient failed to ref cive that benefit which be bad a right to expect. It was claimed in support of tbie theory that if a toad were hung about the neck of a cock for a day or two the head of that cock could be cut off without the lees of a drop of blood. Cocks are different now. Another method was to thrust the bleeding part into the vitals of a hen.l,But while the surgeon was out in the yard chasing a hen,most patients would bleed to di ath. The Moss that grows within dead men's skulls was a lay- ourite, if somewhat gruesome,apPlication. Various mye- terieus powders and liquids were employed, which were as efficacious as so much sand,but which were neverthe- Irsre bought for oabulous sums, occasionally by kings and princes. Some of these powders were so potent that they would even prevent bleeding when applied to the weapon which produced the wound. All the timid duelist had to do was to get A little powder secretly smeared upon his antegonist's sword, and the results after that might take care of themselves !</p>
        <p>von Medem with tdoue -words of-bitter scorn upon her lips he had made no further attempt to dissuade her from venturing on the ice, and indeed had almost, forgotten his own forebodings, until, as he was stoop- ing to fasten his skates Ulric's harsh voice sounded in his ears and he looked up to see the old forester again standing beside him. "If the Herr Baron values his life," he mitt in low earnest tones, " and the lives of those who are dear to him, he will at least bid his friends beware of skating near the Devil's Spring." "My good friend," replied Conrad with a light laugh, "if that is all, you may rest content: we have none of us the slightest intention of risking an impromptu interview with the water-spirits." ,01.1.11ric muttered something beneath his breath, and turning away without another word disappeared into the forest. _ _</p>
        <p>" What was it he said to you, Baron von Medem?" asked Elsa Lorentz, as the Baron, who had put on her skates before his own, now took her hands in his and they glided swiftly from the shore. " Oh, he was only croaking out some nonsense or other about the Devil's Spring which is supposed to rise at the upper end of the lake behind that jutting rock, do you see,—where the boughs of the trees bend down until they almost touch the ice ?" Elsa followed the direction of his eyes • the lake was nearly circular in shape, and lay like a hollow basin amongst the hills, the dense pine-forest closing it in on every side; but at the spot to which the Baron had pointed a huge rock rose sheer and pre- cipitous from the water's edge, its outline broken into strange, fantastic shapes. Seen in the fading light it might have been the ruins of some robber's stronghold or a watch-tower used in times of war ; here and there upon its scarred and rugged surface a few birch saplings had taken aprecarious foothold, their bare white stems and drooping boughs stand- ing out like pale shadowy ghosts against the dark background of the pines beyond. " It is just possible," continued Conrad, "that there might be some danger there: I remember hear- ing long ago of the existence of a subterranean sul- phur spring which gave to that part of the lake its name. However, we ard none of us likely to venture quite so far." Elsa made no further comment, if indeed his words conveyed any special meaning to her ear, and the two glided on in silence among the rest. All day the young girl had been wrapped in a strange and beautiful dream; try as she would to banish what to her pure mind seemed forbidden thoughts, she could not feel the warm clasp of those strong hands upon her own, nor meet the love-light that shone in the clear blue eyes that followed her every movement without a secret heart-throb of gladness, and her loveliness gained an added charm from the fair sweet dawn of a passion that was just awakening into life. As others watched her pass, the slender gray-robed figure poised with such perfect ease upon her skates, scarcely seeming to touch the smooth surface over which she skimmed with the arrowy swiftness of a bird, her golden hair ruffled by the rapid exercise till it wreathed like an aureole around her lovely face with its flushed cheeks and starlike eyes, there was but one discordant note in the uni- versal harmony of opinion, that the baron would choose wisely if he chose this gentle maiden for his bride, but that one note of discord was the only jarring elerhent in Elsa's buoyant mood ; all through the day Minna von Tiircke avoided her with a strange persistency that the young girl strove in vain to over- come. Her timid overtures •had been ungraciously re- pulsed, and more than once she had been startled to see those black eyes watching her with a curious ex- pression of mingled fear and malevolence in their sombre depths. But the hours sped quickly on, and now for the first time von Medem approached his cousin. "Come, Minna," he said pleasantly, "even you must be satisfied by now; you have had your own way as usual," and he looked up laughing in her face. "The water spirits have left us in peace in spite of old Ulric's threats, and I think it is about time we should think of returning home." He did not notice how the girl shrank back for an instant and her face grew suddenly pale. "Is it so late, Conrad ?" she asked, "must we really start ?" Then, seeing Count Hochstadt draw near, she con- tinued hurriedly, " Well, just ten minutes longer then, I will not ask for more." She turned to Elsa as she spoke : " Friiulein Lorentz, will you take one turn with me, there is something I would say to you before we go ?" Elsa looked up in surprise. It was the first time during the day that Minna had voluntarily addressed her, and even now it was rather a command than a request; still the girl's gentle nature was incapable of harbouring an unkind or resentful thought, and with a quiet "Certainly, Fraulein, if you wish it," she extended her hand, and the two skated off together. For some moments they circled amongst the other skaters, the two beautiful women, one so gloriously dark, the other so delicately fair, and many were the comments of surprise and wonder as the graceful figures swept on hand in hand in ever widening curves ; but still Minna did not speak, and Elsa, glancing timidly at her companion's face, was struck by the set lines around her mouth and the look of despair in her great black eyes. " Friiulein Minna, you are ill ?' she said impul- sively, or you are in trouble—what can Ido to help you—what is it you wish to say?" Then, as there was no reply, she added anxiously: "Come, let us go back; you are tired I am sure after all the excite- ment of the day." She would have turned, but Minna held her hand in a tighter grasp, and almost forcibly drew her on- wards.</p>
        <p>"Tired," she exclaimed at last with a bitter em- phasis upon the word; "yes, tired of my life if you will, tired of falsehood, treachery and deceit. No, we will not turn back yet, Elsa Lorentz ; what I have to say to you must be said out of earshat of that gossiping crowd." There, was such a ring of passionate scorn in this unexpected outburst that Elsa shrank back as if she had received a blow, and her blue eyes dilated with a sudden fear, as a back- ward glance over her shoulder showed that they had left the rest of the party so far behind that even the " shirr " of their skates upon the ice sounded like a :faint, indistinct murmur. "Shall I tell you what you have done?" con- tinued Minna ;von -Tiircke' her proud, cold accents cutting like a knife, " youhave stolen froral me the only thing I cared for in all the world, tie love of Conrad von Medem ;—you, a peasant girl, who would bring disgrace upon his ancient lineage if 14e stooped so low as to marry you." " Frau em n Minna," cried Elsa, a quiver of anger in her sweet voice, "how dare you insult me so? What have lever done to you that you should speak to me in this cruel manner ?" _ _</p>
        <p>She would have snatched her hand away, but her companion, holding it more firmly, looked at her with eyes ablaze with jealousy and hate. "Canyon deny that he loves you, then ?—as he not told you so to-day ?" "It is false !" exclaimed Elsa proudly, "Baron von Idedem has never spoken a single word to me that all the world might not have heard." For a moment Minna von Tiircke paused, and then continued with a contemptuous sneer: "If. he has not already spoken, it is because he knows there is no need for haste, he has but to take what lies so ready to his hand, but accept him at your peril! It is for this that I have brought you here, for this that I have watched and waited through the day: I will not let you go until you premise to refuse him, for I tell you I would rather kill you where you stand thAtt see you live to become Conrad von lledein's w'. As Elsa heard the last words hissed rather tiroin spoken in her ear, and as she :net the burning' look of passion that accompanied them,..the rising tide of in- dignation that in its very force had held her dumb, broke loose, there was an intolerable sense of outrage upon her, as though the inner sanctuary of her h-e had been torn open, the secrets of her very soul 3a,41. bare. She wrenched her hand from her companion's grasp, her fair face dyed with the flood of shame that made every nerve within her body quiver. " How dare you, Fraulein von Tiircke," she repeated, "how dare you insult me thus ! I .will not listen to an- other word and never, if I can helkit, in my whole liie will I speak to you again." For one instant she stood like some beautiful Sibyl, her blue eyes flashiag, her hand outstretched., the next instant she would have turned to skate to- wards the shore but,—what was that harsh cry above their heads like the sound of a mocking laugh ?—and what that black shadow falling suddenly across their feet?</p>
        <p>\\\ - „ --</p>
