      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>BARON'S CHOICE,</head>
        <p>THE BARON'S CHOICE,</p>
        <p>ALICE ISAA.CSON "Author of " After Ten Years," 4.c.)</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI.</p>
        <p>The sledges were all drawn up on one side of the clearing, the horses had been unharnessed and were sheltered in some outhouses at the rear, and their late occupants were gathered on the margin of the lake, an animated group surrounding two central figures, one of whom was Fraulein von Tiircke. Conrad pulled up in front of the open door of the hunting- lodge with a sudden jerk that 3cattered the snow in a white cloud from beneath the horses' feet, and, as- t sisting his companion to alight, bade her hurry within and warm herself before the great fire of blazing pine-logs that was burning on the open hearth ; then, thrusting his hands into the deep pockets of his fur-trimmed coat, he sauntered leisurely towards foe shore. As Minna caught sight of his approaching figure, she detached herself from the rest and met him with heightened colour, an angry light sparkling in her black eyes: "You have come then at last, Conrad," and there was a ring of petulant vexation in her tone. "Perhaps now you will speak to this old fel- low and put an end to his absurd complaints." As she turned Von Medem saw the forester Ulric close behind ; he was a tall, broad-shouldered man, vigorous still in spite of his sixty odd years, with grizzled hair and long gray beard flowing down to his breast. Over his forester's dress he wore a thick, shaggy sheepskin coat, and was standing with head bent in an attitude of humble deference twisting art old fur cap in his hands with a deprecatory gesture. At the girl's scornful words a sudden gleam flashed from his dark eyes, half hidden beneath their bushy over-hanging brows ; he looked up as if to speak, then cast his eyes to the ground again, an expres- sion of sullen indifference spreading over his swarthy face. .</p>
        <p>"Well, Ulric, what is it now ?" asked Conrad when his cousin paused; "have the water-spirits been more than usually obstreperous since you told them we were coming ?" "The Herr Baron would not jest if he knew what I know," said the man somewhat sulkily, fixing his deep set eyes on von Medem's face. "Why, my good fellow, what is it you do know? Let us hear it by all means "—and Conrad, taking out his cigar-case from his breast-pocket, proceeded to light a fresh cigar, and watched the blue rings of smoke curl upwards above his head as he composed himself to listen with a look of half-contemptuous good humour. " It is not wise for the Herr Baron and his friends to skate upon the lake to-day," said Ulric firmly. "I have tried to explain this to the gracious Frau- lein, but she will not heed me. I who have lived here for thirty years, who know every sound in the forest, every sign of the weather, never have I spent such a night as last night; the spirits of evil were abroad, I could hear them whispering and mutter- ing amongst the trees; the thunder growled, and the lightning flashed, and over yonder by the Devil's Spring," and he pointed backward with his thumb across his shoulder, " there was the smell of sul- phur in the air." "Perhaps the devils were having a skate," said von Derendorf with a flippant laugh; he was the tall red-bearded man who had driven the Countess Falkenstein and who had been a fellow student of the Baron's in his University days. " Such nonsense," exclaimed Minna impatiently, "just because ths old man has lived up here alone until he has grown half-crazed and because there chanced to be a thunder storm I suppose last night, we are to be baulked of our pleasure. I tell you, Conrad," she added with an angry stamp of her foot upon the snow, "I will skate over the Brunnen-See whatever happens, even if you choose to return home and leave me here alone."</p>
        <p>"That were impossible," said Count Hochstadt gallantly, "we must needs follow where Friiulein von Tiircke leads the way." "The Count will have to lay down his arms if he wins his bride," remarked von Derendorf in a low aside to Countess Falkenstein ; "there would be no standing upon his rights against Fraulein Minna's will."</p>
        <p>"She carries matters with too high a hand," re- marked the lady addressed in the same tone, " such a display of temper is not becoming even in a beau- tiful woman, and really that old man's words are quite infectious, the Brunnen-See may well be haunted if it always looks as gloomy as it does just now. I for one am perfectly ready to give up our plans; by the time luncheon is over it will soon grow dusk; and then thereia the long drive home again,— that at. any rate will be pleasant, will it not 2" she added looking up archl§ in her companion's face. "So pleasant, Countess, that I would it might last for ever, could I be your charioteer," was the reply, half in jest and half in earnest. Conrad von tledem had opened his lips to reply to Ulric when Minna broke in so impetuously, but at her daring words there arose a quick revulsion in his mind. 'What if the old forester were right after all? He did not credit the popular traditions and would have laughed had any man called him superstitious, but he remembered Elaa's sudden pallor, and the look of fear in her blue eyes ; suppose some harm should threaten her? his own cheek blanched at the thought, and that this were a heaven-sent warn- ing which they in their ignorance might deride. He turned to his cousin with assumed carelessness. "We will have luncheon first, Minna, before we settle this knotty question, but I think myself it would be as well for once to take Ulric's advice, not on account of the water-spirits," he added with a laugh, "though oddly enough Fraulein Elsa seems to have fallen under their spell, for she too wished we were not going to skate on the Brunnen-See, but because we have the long drive back through the forest and the day will soon close in." It is strange at times how slight a thread can hold the crisis of our fate; only a word or look maybe that turns the scale on which a life depends, and only by a hair's breadth difference "what might have been" becomes " what is." As Conrad spoke some kindlier impulse swayed Minna's wayward mood. She was on the point of yielding her will to his when at the mention of Elsa Lorentz's name the old fire of jealousy burned up anew. It was of her wishes then that he was think- ing, and she, Minna, was of no account. It was of the long drive here, the long drive home again through the silent snow-bound forest, under the starlit sky, with that golden-haired blue-eyed girl beside him. What words of love might not already hare been whispered, what tender vows might not ere nightfall be exchanged; and she was powerless to thwart or hinder, nay more, she suddenly remem- bered that during that homeward drive other words would have to be spoken, another promise claimed. The man who loved her so unselfishly was standing by her side, but in her heart there was no feeling of _ .</p>
        <p>pity for his pain, only a surging tide of revolt against her own misery, and a passionate tonging for re- venge. She drew nearer to von Medem, saying in a</p>
        <p>low tone unheard but by him. "Play the fool, if you will, Conrad, for the saks of that peasant girl, but I am no coward to be moved by an old man's senseless fears." Then, turning away without waiting for an an. swer, with heal erect, and haughty contempt ea. pressed in every line and curve of her beautiful fee- tures she walked towards the house.</p>
        <p>~„ The short winter's day was rapidly drawing in, the last rays of the settini,, sun had reached the top- most branches of the pines, and the shadows were deepening into gloom around their feet, while the skaters still glided in and out in graceful evolutionE over the smooth surface of the ice. Minna von Tiircke had had her will. When the merry meal was ended and all were refreshed by the dainty viands and warmed by the generous wines the forethought of their host had provided, it seemed easier to forget old "[Brie's warnings than before. The deep blue dome above their heads was flecked here and there with white clouds touched with palest primrose where their fleecy borders turned towards the sun, and at their feet lay the Brunnen-See locked in its wintry sleep, as still and peaceful as though no wild and ghastly legends had ever woven their chain of mys- tery around it; the snow-weighed trees bent down to enfold it in a close embrace, the ice-bound reeds and rushes whispered soft murmurs as they swayed their brown-tipped heads, and from the silince of the forest depths no note of danger rang to bid the care- less ones beware. There was no prophet to suggest' that beneath this treacherous calm a. hidden foe might lurk, and that ,the spirits of evil might still brood over their ancient haunts around the Devil's Spring.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CaLPTEE VII.</head>
        <p>CaLPTEE VII.</p>
        <p>Never had Minna von Tiircke seemed so gay, so brilliantly bewitching as through that afternoon; to a close observer perhaps there might have sounded a hollow ring in her laughter, a feverish unrest in her mirth, but to Count Hochstadt she was more danger- ously beautiful, more fascinating than she had ever been. The dark green velvet dress she wore with its costly sable-fur trimmings, set off her tall, supple figure to perfection, and from beneath her large picturesque velvet hat, with its one deep crimson feather, her black eyes shone -with a strange fire. Sa.ce_the moment when she turned-from_ Conrad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EVEN BURGEONS STCOD AGHAST.</head>
        <p>EVEN BURGEONS STCOD AGHAST.</p>
        <p>We cannot wonder that a reaction set in, many sur- geons becoming afraid to amputate at all. Limbs were no longer cut off, but were permitted to rot off, during nynths of fever and suffering. O'her operators ampu- tated with red-hot knives, while some past master r in the arts of torture used wooden or ivory knives dipped rereatedly in nitric acid. The more quickly a leg was removed the sooner could bioeding be controlled, hence a pious man invented a machine resembling the guillotine, in which the limb was placed on the edge of a knife-blade, while a second knife, heavily wei2hted, fell upon it from a consider- able height. Bones were, of course, terribly splintered, Ibut no one seemed s pay rwuch attention to a few rip fillers. What a delightful time the victim must have had waiting for that knife to fall ! Another eminent practitioner of the gentle art of surgery tied a cord about the seat of the amputation, ar d, by means of a stick, slowly twisted that cord tighter and tighter during the course of several ahnorm- , ally long days, until the leg was twisted off. GHOULS OF THE CHISEL AND MALLET.</p>
        <p>.............. Those who calmly chopped off legs with a meat-axe were not so cruel after all ,considering the performances of their professional brethren ; but there is no excuse for the villains who used a chisel and mallet, cutting slowly through flesh and bone as thoughdoing a carpen- tering job. As late as the middle of the present century,a Parisian surgeon first fractured the bones of his patient's limbs ' and then twisted off the rest of the tissues with a wire rope. A pity such men must die ! They should live for ever and enrich the world with their valuable die- coveriee. It is strange that surgery made so little progress for enturiea—from the time of the Roman Empire to within two or three hundred years of the present date. Throughout these dark ages of medicine many surgeons feared to amputate through living tissues, but first per- netted injured extremities to become foul and gan- grenous, and then cut them off through the dead parts. This was the only procedure followed during the time of Hippocrates. Bleeding to deat'e was avoided with cer- tainty, even though the patient died from blood poison- Under these old methods it required from six to eighteen months to recover, during which dreary eternity, the end of the bone, which always protruded from the open wound, had to decay and drop off. As an aid to this procesti the bone was repeatedly burned with a red-hot iron. This kept the patient in good heart, ard made him realise that sonlething was being done. Even if the sufferer was unlucky enough to recover. which aeldom happened, his stump was covered with a tt nder and delicate sear which was always ulcerating and giving rise to pain and annoyance. To have a leg amputated, even in the twen- tieth ceetury, is not a luxury, nor a thing to be strongly desired ; but it is not the horrible blooe-curdling experience new that it once was. An amputation is veceesary. Before you fully grasp tee horror of your position, for the mind is haze at such times,chlerotorm is administered. . . . And then you find yotreelf back in bed, gazing at a delight- ful young woman in a white cap and spotless apron, and trying to realise that your wounded leg is no longer at- tached to your body. When the effects of the anmathetic have worn cff,you are quite comlortablewand in two or V-.ree weeks you are well and about on crutches. From the time you entered the hospital there was practically no pain, no fever, and comparatively little discomfort. The art of surgical cleanliness too, which means the exclusion of germs from wounds, has added largely te the sum total of human happine:s and longevity. Much physical suffering, which was fcrmerly endured with what fortitude we could command, is now dissipat&amp;d by a few painless strokes of the hnife, and with a minimum amount cf danger and discomfort. Especially should 1 women be thankful for the blessings which have been conferred upon them in respect of certain complaints which ara beyond other than surgical assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>Absorbed in their own thoughts, forgetful 9f all around, the two had swept heedlessly on, and Elsa now looked up to find with a thrill of terror that they were close beneath the huge jagged rock Baron von Medem had pointed out to her earlier in the day. In the gathering dusk it loomed above them like some gigantic Monster watching for its prey; dimly amid the confusion of her thoughts, the Baron's words recurred: "The Devil's Spring, there might be danger there."—Something cold touched her fag. It was the drooping boughs of a little birch sapli. that grew down from the rock above; instinctively she clutched at them with both hands to steady her- self, and would have raikied her voice in warning to Minna von Tiircke whose impetuous coarse had carried her some yards beyond, but the words died away un- spoken on her lips. It was but for an instant that she saw that tall figure outlined against the pallid gleam of the snow-draned trces-,the next, there was</p>
        <p>a crash- oflpthirerea 1-c-e--•=a-tliWg of black turbid waters above the broken surface—one awful scream as of a human soul in direst agony, and then—the silence of the dead!</p>
        <p>In speechless horror Elsa crouched back against the rock, clinging convulsively to the birch boughs she held, her senses reeling, trembling in every limb. There was something so terrible in this sudden and unlooked for catastrophe that for the moment, stunned and bewildered, she could not realise its truth, and watched with strained eyes half expecting her late companion to re-appear. She was roused by the rattle of falling stones, the trees above her swayed beneath some un- accustomed weight, there was a rush, a movement, and a dark form swung itself rapidly don the sur- face of the rock, and stood beside her in the gloom: to the terrified girl it seemed as though the detnon of the haunted spring had suddenly arisen in human shape, and now the seal upon her lips was loosed, and shriek after shriek of fear and frenzy rang through the frosty air. •" "Nay. Fraulein, be not afraid," said old Ulric, for it was he, laying his hand gently on her shoul- der, " you are safe, and help will soon be here, but what devil's snare tempted you to skate in this direction?"</p>
        <p>_ He carried a coil of rope and was busily engaged as he spoke in knotting one end of it firmly around the stem of the tree to which Elsa was clinging. At the sound of his voice she looked up reassured, and though not daring to stir, she watched with fas- cinated eyes as, holding the coil of rope in his hands, he crept cautiously towards the edge of that yawning chasm.</p>
        <p>_ Meanwhile, Baron von Medem, standing be- neath the rustic wooden porch over the door of the hunting-lodge was speeding his departing guests; a few of the skaters still remained upon the ice, loth tr, relinquish their amusement until the last possible moment, but four or five sledges had already btarted, and their lamps might be seen gleaming here and there like will-o'-the-wisps as they wound in and out along the steep, descending road among.the dusky shadows of the pines. He had been talking and laughing with Countess Falkenstein and Fritz von Dorenclorf, when suddenly, clear and distinct above the laughter and the voices near, above the stamping of the horses' feet, and the jangle of the sledge-bells, above the fainter ring of the skates upon the ice be- low them, came the sound of that wild cry—so agon- ised, so despairing—that it struck a chill of horror to the hearts of those who heard it. . _ _</p>
        <p>" Good Heavens !" exclaimed von Derendorf, "there has been an accident, the ice has given way!" He bent hurriedly towards the little fur-muffled figure standing by his side: Countess, you will forgive me if I leave you, help may be needed—l must go." The lady looked up in his face, her hands out- stretched, her eyes bright with sympathetic tears. "I would not for the world keep you back, my friend," she said softly, " only for my sake take care." _ _</p>
        <p>For one instant the young man pressed hi lips to the fair white hands he held, the next he was stand- ing by his friend upon the margin of the ice. It was the work of a moment to readjust their skates, but in that breathless interval, fainter, more piteous cries were heard, and Conrad's heart seemed to stand still with a horrible sensation of fear, as he fancied he recognised the tones of Elsa's voice. Rapidly he glanced over the group of terrified women huddled near him.</p>
        <p>"Are all here ?" he exclaimed, " is anyone missing from our party ?" Some of the gentlemen had al- ready skated off in -the direction whence the cries had ,come. " Friiulein von Tiircke and Friiulein Lorentz alone are missing," was the trembling answer. A groan of anguish escaped his lips, as he turned to follow those who had hurried on. " I knew it," he muttered hoarsely, " from the first moment Ulric warned us, I felt sure some evil would happen to- day." "Cheer up, old fellow," said von Derendorf, lay- ing his hand consolingly on his friend's arm, "it may not be so bad as you think, just a sprained ankle perhaps, though what possessed those two to skate so far away I can't conceive; if only it were not getting so confoundedly dark. Hullo! what's this ?' Dashing forward at headlong speed they found themselves close upon a knot of dark figures seen dimly in the fading light, but Conrad, blind to all else' had eyes only for that one slight form shrink- ing back beneath the shadow of the rock. Drawing along, low, sobbing breath of utmost relief after the agonising suspense of the past few moments, he gathered her up inhis arms, clasped her to his heart, and his lips closed on hers as he whispered brokenly: "Elsa, my darling, my little love, thank God that you are safe !" . . .</p>
        <p>For one moment the girl lay passive in his em- brace, seeming scarcely conscious of his low passion- ate murmurs of love, then she strove to release her- self, shuddering violently as she said in faltering ac- cents : "Oh, not now, Baron von Medem—l cannot hear you now—your cousin, Fraulein Minna—" The Baron lifted his head—" Ah, yes, by the way, where is Minna ?" Looking round he saw von Derendorf near him, an expression of intense horror and pity on his usually cheerful countenance. " Von Medem," he said, " it is more terrible than we could have imagined.: Fraulein von Tiircke— 'Ulric will tell you all—but my poor friend, I fear there is no hope." /He recoiled suddenly, for hastily releasing Elsa; Conrad von Medem's hand came down on his shoul- der as he twisted him aside, and confronted the old forester, who, bending above that ghastly hole, on the brink of the broken ice that strained and cracked beneath his feet, was gazing down upon the inky surface of the water, which seemed to writhe and shudder with a perpetual snake-like motion as though it were stirred by some mysterious power to a know- ledge of the awful tragedy its depths concealed. " Speak, 'Ulric," he demanded, "where is my cousin? What devil's work is this ?"</p>
        <p>" Ireilige jungfrau !" muttered the forester, as hs rose from his stooping posture and turned his blanche&amp; face and horror-filled eyes upon the young man "it is truly the devil's work ; the Herr Baror knows I Warned him to beware of this cursed spot, but when he troated my words lightly, I knew thai evil would befall, and I came here to watch if helt were neeneds. 1 cried out to the gracious ladies to turn back, but they did not heed my voice, and then, it was but the space of a lightning flash, the dark- haired Fraulein stood just here "—he pointed down- wards— " and sank beneath the ice with that one wild, awful scream. Lieber Gott ! I can still hear it ringing in my ears, and though in the twinkling of an eye I was on the water's edge,, she never rose again." "You should have dived after her, fellow," cried Conrad, hastily tearing off his heavy outer garments "Good God! to starid by gaping like a fool when a human life is at stake, and every second of time is precious." The old man's eyes flashed fire, but he restrained himself and answered calmly :—" My life is of little. value and gladly would I have risked it to save the value, but it would be worse than useless." His voice dropped to an awestruck whisper: "Herr Baron, nothing that sinks below the surface of the Devil's Spring is ever seen again." Conrad shuddered, and paused, still hesitating, still looking fixedly down intothat strangely troubled water from which a faint nauseating smell of sul- phur rose and mingled with the air, when von Der- endorf's voice sounded in his ears.</p>
        <p>" Look here, von Medem," he said, ." those other fellows have taken Count Hochstadt to the lodge; he is half mad; poor fellow, and would have thrown him- self into the water if they hadn't held him back by force, and I've sent for torches and—and—" he lowered his voice, " grappling irons, you know, and that sort of thing. c'tVe can do nothing in this con- founded darkness until the men come, but I think you ought to get Fraulein Elsa away, poor little girl, it has been a fearful shock for her."</p>
        <p>The Baron drew a long breath, and grasped his fr:ead's arm : " Thanks, von Derendorf, the fact is, I feel stunned, it has come so awfully suddenly. Poor Minna !" He dashed aside the tears that filled his eyes. "How can I ever break the news to her mother and mine? Yes, you are right, Elsa shall go home with Countess Falkenstein and you. I must</p>
        <p>siay here," he added, " I cannot leave—until—until" His voice broke down utterly and he turned away without finishing his sentence, but his friend under- stood that silence more eloquent than words. Yes, he would stay until the spirits of the haunted spring should be. driven to give back their prey, until some- thing should be brought up from those treacherous depths, something so pitiful, so ghastly, that it would scarce bear any semblance to the proud, beau- tiful woman who so short a time before had swept on to her fate with cruel words upon her lips, and hatred in her heart.</p>
        <p>Elsa made no resistance when Baron von Medem, raising her tenderly in his arms, half carried, half guided her towards the shore ; only as he placed her in tre sledge, and Countess Falkenstein bent over her with soft, caressing words, the girl's unnatural composure suddenly gave way, and looking up in Conrad's face, her blue eyes full of a wild pathetic pain, she cried out sobbingly: "Oh, do not take me to the Schloss, let me go home to my father's house —he will tell Ille what I ought to do—he will un- derstand." And so it was arranged. There was no thought now of the sweet love-vows that might have been whispered during that homeward drive, but as the sledge glided on swiftly and silently between the tows otstately_ tr.c.:e&amp; with their outstretched, branches</p>
        <p>draped in snow, Elsaio—heart ached bitterly—wit:Fara agony of self-accusation underlying a sense of griev- ous wrong. \ If she could but have foreseen whither Minna's blind passion was hurrying them both, she, who alma knew of the hidden danger lurking there, might heve prevented this terrible disaster, bu then arc a the memory of those cruel words and the look of }owning jealousy with which they had been spoken, and in her inmost soul she trembled as the Baron's words came back: "If any mortal ventures on the Brunnen-See with hatred or anger in his heart, the water-spirits can weave their magic spells around him, and woe betide his efforts to escape; from that moment his fate is sealed." When Conrad von Medem once more reached the scene of the catastrophe, the smoky crimson flare of the pine-torches threw strange gleams of lurid light and weird fantastic shadows across the ice and snow. Could some belated traveller have suddenly looked down from the hillside above, he might have taken that knot of agitated figures, seenindistinctlythrough the flickering glare, for a band of witches holding their unhallowed revels around the Devil's Spring, but as Conrad approached, the men fell back, and ho again stood face to face with Ulric. " Our efforts are all in vain," the latter said, "and the Herr Baron would do well to bid them cease, at least until the morning dawns." But Conrad would not listen ; though all actual hope had died away he could not tear himself from that fatal spot until the body of his hapless cousin had been recovered, and so the hours passed; agein and again the iron drags were used, only to prove that the depth of that part of the lake was unfathomable. Again and again the ice was broken away in wider circles, but all to no purpose, and at last when the men, stricken with a. strange, bewildering, superstitions fear began to mutter rebelliously among themselves, the Baron re- luctantly gave the signal to abandon further search. As he turned, heart sick and weary towards the shore, it seemed to his overwrought brain as though dark phantoms lurked beneath the shadows of the trees, and mocking voices followed him with gibes and jeers, exulting with a fiendish joy over the death- secret that no human power could force them to dis- close.</p>
        <p>(To be Concluded.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Thanks mainly to the efforts of Mr F Sherlock, the practice now obtains in many parishes of presenting the offertories on Easter Day as an offering to the incum- bent. This is a revival of an old custom, and it is one which might be followed generally with advantage, particularly in those perishes where the income from the beeefice is small. In such cases the offering i not only acceptable for its intrinsic value, hut is appreci- ated by the clergyman as a testimony of gratitude for his work. The Baptists, following the example of other denom- inations, are raising a Twentieth Century Fund, and selected Palm Sunday for a special collection throughout their churches. It is intended to help the poorer churches, and to succour poor and aged ministers, as well as to promote the work of Evangelisation. The Baptista claim to have greatly increased in wealth and social position during the past century. In 1801 they numbered 30,000 members, and 400 churches, whereas now they have 400,000 members, and over 4,000 churches. The King will no doubt be reminded during his Easter cruise round the coast, of his first yachting excursion upon which be was taken by Queen Victoria in 184.6. His Majesty was then scarcely 5 years old, and it was in August of that year that the Queen decided to take a quiet cruise in the first Victoria and Albert, which was thee a new vessel. The Royal yacht was accompanied by two other paddle steamers, the Fairy, and the Eagle, and visits were paid to the Channel Islands, Weymouth, Penryn, and other places on the South Coast. The people of Cornwall appear to have taken a great deal more interest in the Duke of Cornwall than in the Queen, at which her late Majesty was somewhat amused, but certainly not displsased, and it is more than pcssible that King Edward VII. remem- bers the fuss that was made over him on that occasion. The Prince and Princess of Wales have become quite at home in fulfilling the many public duties which it is now expected that the Heir-Apparent and his Princess should undertake. Such an occasion was the launching of the new battleship Prince of Wales at Chatham the other day, after which his Royal Highness laid the first keel-plate of the Devonshire, a new first-class cruiser which is to be built at the dockyard. The giving of royal and county names to mar warships is a revival of an oid custom, and the name Prince of Wales has been in use at least two or three times before. After so many public engagements the Prince and Prin- cess of Wales will enjoy their-Eager holidays, and it is quite possible that their Royal Highnesses may after- wards go on a short visit to Denmark, before the Queen returns from her stay at the Danish Court, towards the end of April. The abandonment of the Royal visit to Ireland has not discouraged the association of ladies who are engaged in taking every opportunity of drawing attention to the excellence of Irish manufactured geode. One is glad to see that at a reeent meeting the association was able to congratulate itself upon a considerable measure of em- cee!. Large quantities of goods have been sold in Dub- lin, Manchester, and elsewhere, and the exhibits at Glasgow have evidently drawn the attention of very many people to the 'excellence of Irish lace, homespun tweeds, and embroideries. So many persons especially children, have been burnt to death through wearing "flannelette " clothing, that the wise avoid this material altogether. It is of a highly inflammable nature, and instead of smouldering slowly like ordinary clothing, it at once bursts into flame, if brought within contact of a fire or light. The rick, however, may be considerably le= sealed, if the garments I are rinsed out in a solution of alum and water, which to I a great extent renders them non-inflammable. Another source of danger arises from the use of celluloid fee combs and personal adornments. The inferior celluloid is very liable to take fire in a heated atmosphere, and it is now used in the manufacture of so many articles. In Paris the other day. a funeral corcath rnada of eelluloid suddenly took fire, owing to the presence of a chorister toy carrying a lighted taper, and as nearly as possible involved the tetel destruction of the catafalque, beside seriously burning several people including the priest. Very lovely are the musline, linens, fancy diils, and zephyrs, prepared for summer wear. The flowered muslins are exquisite—resembling closely the painted chiffons so much in request last year—so also are the pin-snotted,and blurred varieties.—Grass lay ns promise I to enjoy a wide vogue, also mercilawn, a fabric which I looks very like a good foulard. The new grass lawns are much thinner and softer than of old, and make up , into charming gowns. It should be remembered hew- I ever, that they suit only fresh bright young faces. The sallow, or faded woman appears at her worst in fawn coloured clothes, but this state of things apparently does not always occur to her, judging by the undesir- able exhibitions one frequently comes across. The dressing gown of the moment is either a copy,or a modified copy ofltherliriniono." Thislie usually built of flannel, but brocade is used for really smart speci- mens, brocade sprayed over with branches of almond or plum blossom, or splaged with chrysanthemums, the flower most associated in British minds with the Japanese nation. In construction these gowns are quite simple and with a good pattern and machine can quickly be run up at home. Evening wraps are now being built somewhat on the same lines, and very smart they look. Moreover they can be slipped over the most airy-fairy toilette, without the customary crushing pro- cess taking place. We have epparently wearied of the large piece of glass designed to represent a diamond, and the huge I earl bead, as the ornamental portions of our hat pine. The most up-tc-date "dagger "shows a big black pearl as a finish, and thie is utilised with every variety of headgear. Smaller pins to match are being used to secure ihe inevitable bunch of violets to the corsage, this sweet smelling bouquet being not infrequently a powder-puff. All leading milliners supply this little accessory, and so skilfully are they arranged that detect,ort is impossible. Many people are in favour of Old Age Pensions, and there is general unanimity that the amount sbould be 54 weekly payable at the age of 65. It is, however, quite certain that this problem will never be dealt with as a whole, and it is suggested that a stert might be made by giving pensions to women only. In many things, except in the hard struggle for life, it is customary to relieve the necessities of women first, and seeing the many &amp;advantages which women, who have to make their own way in the world, are at, as compered with men, there is a very good Case to be merle out for help- ing them first, when they become past work. Something would be gained if pensions ceuici be provided at 70, Or even commencing at 75. It would make a start, and from first helping a small number, it might be possible to gradually work out a general old age pers'on scheme. The new edition of Nr B S Rowntree's important work " Poverty : A Study of Town Life," will be published by Messrs Macmillan immediately • This book has had a very striking success, (lan first edition being sold out within six weeks of publicatien. The general verdict of. the press is that no more important contribution to the literature cf the subj.( cs has bun made for many years Sermons and speeches have been delivered, and confer- ences held on the 6ubj, et of Mr Rowntree'e investigation, and many prominent public men have expressed their appreciation of its results. The new edition cenrains additional matter of considerable intereet. MADGE.</p>
        <p>CONTINUAL CURES IN MANCKESTER.—Every week fresh evidence is reported in the newspapers concerning the Wouderful cures effected in Manchester by Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people. The details of the latest—a very remarkable cure—are given elsewhere in this paper. These pills have cured paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; also all diseases aris- ing from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, arremia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, less of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous headache and neuralgia, early de- cay, all forms of female weakness and hysteria. These plus are a tonic not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr Williams's pink pills for pale people, and are sold by chemists, and by Dr Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, Lender), E.C„ at 2s 21 a box, or six boxes for 13s 2d. ' Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars are not Dr Williams' pink pills; accept them only in the pink closed wrappers as above iescribed. it was 'kinder funny—that is, overlookin' the feriouenesi cf it," said the landlord of the Fettyville tavern. "You see, Miss Gabriella Leeks, who has—not meanin' any disrespect to her, you understand—been an old maid so long .that it's generally believed to be chronic, approached the railroad crossin', and a brake- man waved a red flag at her to warn her of the danger. bly thought he was tryin' to flirt with her, and ad- vanced toward him with a smile ; and a caboose that was backin' up struck her good and plenty. Luckily, no bones were broken; and,the first thing she said when ste recrvered consciousness was " • Oh, this is so eudden !'" "smart set.")</p>
