      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHANGES IN MANCHESTER</head>
        <p>CHANGES IN MANCHESTER</p>
        <p>A Painful Story.</p>
        <p>Upper Bro,k-street, Chollton-on-Medlock, which ter- minates in Victoria Park, the seat of some of the wealthiest citizens of Manchester, was, until recetat years, a fashionable rest:antral lucality. "Upper Brook- street" used to be synonymous with " gentility." A tramway new runs through it, and a good deal of its former glory has departed, but it is 6611 an imp:lrtant, if less secluded, artery of the city, and communicates in a direct line with Albert-, (pare and the Town Hall. At the very end of Upper Brook-street, and faciog the park gates is Autumn-street. It is o. ly as it were, half formed, for while it bears tria name " Autumn-street," the ground opposite Ihe ten or a dozen houses ia it is open to fligh-Areet, a thoroughfare connecting Oxford- street with Longeight. Although an ineiguificant street, issues of great moment have sprur g from i'. It ra the centre of an interesting chain of events which led a reporter to make special ioquirir s in Autumn-street itself. No. 9 proved to be the res. Pence of Mrs. Annia Laura Martin, w;fe of Mr Alfred Martin, a builder and con- tractor. Mr Martin was in ye.nrs gone by a wealthy man, with a balance of £30,000 or £40,000 at his b*nkers, but losses en building contracts and a ruinous enterpvse of that kind on the unstable salt grounds of Northwich, reduced him to a position of comparative poverty. . . . .</p>
        <p>'rue story teld by Mr a Martin is as follows :—"About sixteen years ago we lived in Bc rotn-Atreet, City, and I then began tu be very ill. I suffered from indigeation, rh, umatism. and a weak heat. I had pains in the • chest,and pains between the shoulder blade;.,and nothing did me good until, after I had been treated by the best doctors in Manchester. I fortunstely heard of Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people." illneas began with indigestion, as I have men- Wned. Heart disease develoe.ed afterwards. I also suffered greatly from enlargement of the liver." "Dow long were you under the treatment of the medi- cal men ?"</p>
        <p>"Up to about twelve months ay." "And di,l you get el relief in all those fif'een years ? " Not one of the medical gentlemen I toici you of did me any permanent good." " I suppose you took their medicines ?" " I have taken more hot ; les oi medicine than I can count, and pills and powders as well ;in fact, I have taken every kind of medicine, including patent medi- cines also. I used to have a splendid set of teeth, but they have gore, and I attributistheir lcrs to the mer curial potinna I have swallowed. If I bad known of Dr. Williams' pink pills earlier I should not have lost m teetb.",</p>
        <p>A bright. intelligent boy bei•ra present, led to inquiries on the subject of children and elicitedfrom Mrs. Martin that she had lost nearly dozen-children since how-- marriage, the lad in the room being her only son, nine years of age, educated by his mc' her, who is a lady of superior tducation." "You are lookitg very well now, Mrs. Martin. What were the circumstances that led up to this happy change ?" " It is a little over a year ago that a pamphlet witb reference to Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people was thrust under my door. At the same time my attention was also arrested by reading an aricle in the " Man- chester Evening, News " of the sufferings and cure of a person who bad experienced the same dyapepsia and other rheumatic and heart troubles as myseV. I showed the article to my husband. and said I had a good mind to try them. He said, Yes, get a box.' "</p>
        <p>" Now, tell me their'effect;a7nd exactly bow you went on."</p>
        <p>" I had medicine in the house at the time. I have taken none since, however. I at once began to use Dr. Williams' pink pills. I took six or s-ven boxes before I pruld say that I began to be much better. After di teen years' chronic suffering the idibrovement natur- ally was gradual. But it was also are. At the time I started taking the pills I wAs subject to great langour, lassitu4e, swelling of the feet and hands, pains in the &amp;Nome°, and vartord.e veins. Life was a burden. I used t.,3 feel worse when I got up in a morning than when I went tø bed at night. Household work was out of the question.. All that sort of thing used tl be done for me. The docto7 had told me I bad rheumatic gout in my bands. I bad also pain in my side, which I used ta think was pleurisy." "But tell nse the effect of Dr Williams' Did you follow the instructions given in each box ?"</p>
        <p>. " I followed the instructions as far as I could. By degrees I began to feel an improvement in my condi- tion. I slept better, my appetite gradually increased, the varicose veins went down, and the pains and cramp which I had experienced, slowly cased to trouble me. After the fifth box the feeling of numbness in the body left me, ai2i I could feel myself getting stronger every day. , Before I began to take Dr Williams' pink pills I was a confirmed invalid. Since tl.pn I have walked to Ancoats and back, to AU Saints' Church, and even to Withing ton and Didabury." "Dare I ask bow old you are, Mrs Martin ?"</p>
        <p>_ . " Oh, yes; I am fifty-six." " A ci you attribute your recovery entirely to Dr Will, ' pink pills for pale people ?" "I yit is nothing but Dr Williams' pink pills that have brought me to the condition in which you see me to.clay." "Do you know of others who have benefited by taking Dr Williams' pink pills ?" "Yes; I know a lady at Altrincham who suffered greatly from rheumatism; she has been cured by them; my sister, who is now in America, was cured of dyspepsia by them; and I know a young girl who suffered terribly from St Vitus's dance, who has been cured by Dr. Williams' pink pill% after being treated in vain at one of the Manchester hospitals. 'lbis girl need to scream with pain befcre taking Dr Williams' pink pills. When I :old her mother to try them she sneered at the idea, end called it quackery, hut .he is of a different opinion now. I know a lady at Thnperley who used to suffer from seve.re pains in the left side. I recommended Dr Williams' pink pills. She-said. "They will do me no geed," but I urged her to try a box, and gave her three of my pills. These she took, and with such evidently satisfactory results that three days after- wards she wrote to me, asFine, "I have got a box" ; and yet this lady bad cool:Mealy assorted that if physi- cians Could do her no nonfl it was not likely that she would be benefited by Dr. Williams' Pink pills for pale pick pills before she was people. I believe the vie with St Vitues dance only took one box of Dr. Williims.' pi entirely cured. I have recommended them to dozens of &amp;to willing that these particulars shall be people." )7 mat(ft Apr;urboin CI c ? ; Ido not wish to withholl anything that will at an tenefit ”r be of interest to any sufferer."</p>
