        <p>THE UNIVERSITY BOAT RACE: THE CAMBRIDGE 'CREW. 1, T. Drysdale. 2, P. H. Thomas. 3, W. H. Chapman (bow). 4, J. Edwards-Moss. 6, R. H. Nelson (stroke), 6, F. J. Esoombe. 7, C. W. H. Taylor. 8, H. C. S. Wasbrongh (cox). 9, H. B. Grylls.</p>
        <p>of £.200,000 per annum for four years in addition to their entire net profits as a bonus to all retailers selling their goods and refusing to sign the opposition agreement. The Imperial then climbed down and withdrew their boycotting clause, but still refused their bonus to customers who would not agree to keep the American goods out of their show windows. The prospects of the retailer and consumer are, perhaps, rosy enough for the next four years, but after that both may confidently rely upon being squeezed to the utter- most, as whichever combination gains the supremacy is certaAn to put up the price and make as much profit as possible once, a monopoly has been obtained. Perhaps this will be a fittingl opportunity for Sir Michael Hicks-Beach, or pos- sibly by that time a successor, to pile up the 'Customs duty on tobacco, especially the cheaper kinds, so that the country 'may really ,eventually profit, even if the small gutter boy is prevented from stunting his growth and :intelligence, owing to the fabulous rates then in vogue for the articles which he now purchases at five for a penny, with a beautiful photograph and a life insurance poliby thrown in. ,</p>
        <p>Last Saturday the great aquatic event of the year was decided over the usual course from Putney to Mortlake, and resulted in an easy victory for Cambridge, who finished five lengths ahead. The Light Blues took the lead at the start and were not seriously challenged at any point of the course. The official time was 19 min. 9 sec., which ranks among the quickest per- formances on record. There are only four other races in which the time was less than 20 minutes, the fastest time being Oxford's 18 min. 47 sec. in 1893, which was repeated by Cambridge in 1900. Throughout the race the Light Blues showed superior form, and more than fulfilled the ex- pectations of the experts, with the majority of . whom, fasilwith the general public, they were hot' favourites. Both crews boasted four Old Blue' oarsmen, and Oxford had their old coxswain,l G. S. Maclagan, in addition. Oxford were much the heavier crew, and a finer-looking eight have seldom taken the water. The men were big, strong, muscular, and trained to the hour. They had everything in their favour but the skill. Not that they lacked the science of the game, but they were far behind Cambridge in making the most of their weight" and strength.l It was obvious from the start that the leg workj of the Light Blues was better than that of their opponents, and principally to this the experts attribute their success. The difference in style was most marked. Cambridge went swing- jug along with a light, easy rhythmical) motion, the boat answering every stroke like a living thing. Oxford, on the other, hand, were always a dull, heavy, lifeless crew. They seemed to have to drag the boat by sheer physical strength through the water. To watch the Light Blues at their work one felt that they were enjoying themselves. The Dark Blues, however, were always toiling, always in difficulties, ani there never was a clearer, more indisputable case of the best crew winning. Saturday's contest was the fifty-ninth, and of the races which preceded it Oxford have won thirty-three and Cambridge twenty-four, the result in 1877 being a dead heat. That much-abused, if not very much enduring individual, the British working man, has long ' had a grievance against whichever Government happened to be in power, in that they did not do something to check or stop altogether the immi- gration of destitute aliens who during the last ' sixty years have</p>
        <p>The post of Commissioner of Police for the City of London, to which Captain Nott Bower, for twenty-one years Head Constable of Liverpool, bee just been appointed, will be one of exceeding great responsibility during the present year. eontrol the immense crowds which will throng the Capital during Coronation week will require an organising ability almost amount- ing to genius, and not many men would care to undertake th e gargantuan task which lies im- mediately before the new Com- missioner. No- body doubts, however, that Captain, Nott Bower will fully justify the con- fidence which the City Common Council have placed in him, for he has, during his thirty 'years' ex- perience,acquired • a most thorough knowledge of police administra- tion on an extensive scale. Prior to joining the Royal Irish Constabulary as a district inspector in the early seventies, he had had a useful military training, which served him well during the stirring times of the Fenian agitation. After six years' experience of police duty in Ireland, he was appointed Chief Constable of Leeds, and three years later became the head of the Liverpool force of 1,827 men—the largest city police force in the country. During that service he has been constantly responsible for the control of great crowds, and far the regulation of traffic under varying conditions, such as Royal receptions, • embarkations of troops, dock strikes, etc. After the strike of the Dublin Metropolitan Police some years ago he was requested to draw up a scheme for the reorganisation of that force, which was adopted by the Irish Government and has worked satisfactorily ever since. In addition, the Captain has frequently been called to give evidence before Royal, Parliamentary, and Departmental Commis- sions, and in many cases the suggestions he has made with regard to matters of police have been adopted and become law.</p>
