        <p>and report upon (1) The charac- ter and extent ,of LORD JAMES OF HEREFORD the evils which are attributed to CHAIRMAN OF THE ROYAL cox- the unrestricted MISSION ON .4.1.1 Ex IMMIGRATION. immigration of aliens, especially in the metropolis; (2) The measures which have been adopted for the restriction and control of alien immigration in foreign countries and in British Colonies ; and to advise what remedial or. precautionary measures it is desirable to adopt in this country, having regard to the above, matters and to the absence of any statutory power to exclude or expel any individual alien or class of aliens from its borders. Year by year the number of poor persons of foreign birth who settle in this country is increasing, and there are now a quarter-of- a-million foreigners amongst us, or six in e 'try thousand of the population, many of whom are poverty-stricken Russians and Poles with little or no aptitude for skilled labour. There can be no doubt about the undesirability of this class of immigrant, and with our workhouses and lunatic asylums' full to overflowing it is time that we began to look to the matter. The battle of the big tobacco trusts has now commenced in real earnest, and Great Britain is for the first time the venue of one of these huge commercial conflicts which have been waged so fiercely in the United States during the last decade. The Imperial Trust (a combination of most of the largest British firms) fired the first important shot by issuing a manifesto to the trade on Wednesday last week, promising a bonus of £50,000 at the end of April, and one-fifth of Ow SIR. J. B. DUKE.</p>
        <p>It is a grave question politically whether the Irish Members are going the right way about things to benefit their country and their con- stituents, and week by week they continue to alienate more and more even the extremest of Liberals, who are with them in theory, but cannot connive at their practices. It is</p>
        <p>only a few days since the cheer- ing of Lord Methuen's disas- ter disgusted the whole House and the whole wt.</p>
        <p>mthtating against the de- sires of the Irish people towards Home Rule and</p>
        <p>other measures more than the most ponderous of verbal argu- ments, and this is followed up by the action of Mr,</p>
        <p>ME. J. DI:</p>
        <p>Dillon in setting every rule of de- corum, so rigidly ipbserved in Par- liament, at defi- ance, by calling the Colonial Secretary a " d----d liar' across the floor of the House, and refusing to apologise. No doubt Mr. Chamberlain is often a very irritating politician to his foes, and, even in the light of the previous disgraceful scene, was not altogether justified in his taunt and sneer at Mr. Dillon and his appreciation of traitors, but it must be admitted that the Irish party by their gross misconduct in the Methuen dispatch incident had laid themselves open to it. Still it would _ _</p>
        <p>SUSPENDED OR Sfl 17N-. PARLIAMENTARY LANGUAGE.</p>
        <p>have been far better i'f the caustic cut had never</p>
        <p>been given, however bitter the impression left by the previous incident, but Mr. Dillon in his turn can in no way be considered justified in such a grave breach of Parliamentary manners as to fling such a pothouse phrase at a Cabinet Minister. His suspension, amusingly enough, came at a time in Parliamentary history when there existed no rule to meet the case—the old one being off and the new one not yet in force! This conjuncture of circumstances, however, was met by a special motion on the part of the Leader of the House, dealing with it on the basis of the old rule. The moral of the whole affair, which really dates back to the reading of Lord Kitchener's despatch on Lord Methuen's disaster, if not further, is that everyone, whether crusted Conservative or Radical, is grieved to see the Irish M.P.'s continually cutting their own throats andl that of their country's best interests by conduct orhich can claim nobody'm oymPatilY.</p>
        <p>SIR W. H. WILLS.</p>
