        <p>Case at Daresbury Petty Sessions. A case of conAderable local interest occupies the anention of the magistrates at Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday Nation M.r John Pemberton, builder, Stockton Heath, was summoned at the instance of Job's Swinton, surveyor and inspector of nuisancee for the Buu worth district of the Run- corn Rural District Council,for not complying with</p>
        <p>byelaw M and 55 of the Rural District Council in the erecunn of buildings in Pike- street, So-Aton Heath. Byelaw 51 provides that the rooms of houses shall not be less than nine feet in height and byelaw 51 has reference to the projection Sz.c of hearthstones.—The ms,gistrates on the Bench were Mr William Inng (in tiao chair), Colonel _Ridgway, and Messrs J Malin gielsten, _ . _ .</p>
        <p>and LI Mel.l. Air Lake, solicitor, Runcorn, con- ducted the case on behalf of the Rural District</p>
        <p>Connell and the defendant was represented by Mr F E Smith, barrister, Liverpool .(instructed by</p>
        <p>Messrs Spencer Wellhead and Sons). In opening the case Mr Lake said the proce,s with regard to plans under the Public health Act was that if a man intended to build prap,qty he should prepare plans and send them to the Council for approval or disapproval within a month. The . _ . . .</p>
        <p>• - - - r ason for this was in order that the Council might know that he intended to build and could inspect the property and alio that the plans might show that the buildings were to be in accordance with the byelaw. The building; in dispute had been under _</p>
        <p>discussion for a considerable period. The first plans were originally deposited about May 23rd, 1899. One plan showed certain property to be built so that the able would come towards Pike-street.</p>
        <p>That property was not built and in October, WOO, a similar plan was sent in as an amended plan and that showed the same properry twisted round a; right angles to the former position as that Ole houses would face into Pike-street. The plan was</p>
        <p>not approved because the clroinage was riot satis- factory. The defeniant commenced to build in 1901 without a plan having been approved. The surveyor was then sent for and pointed out sundry mistakes which were beinz made and reported to</p>
        <p>the committee. The strange position Was this that the defendant was a member of the Parochial Committee which had to deal with the byelaws and plans for his own houses, and ultimately the committee referred the qneEtion to the District Council in order that an outside authority might deal with the matter. The District Coun- cil sAed Mr Pemberton to come tyllre them and show cause why an amendment should not be made. Ho de- clined to come and the Council immediately appointed a ' sub-committee which inscolted the property and met Mr PembErt3n on the 29th November, Defendant</p>
        <p>declined to go over the property with the committee and practically set them at defieeee. The committee report- ed to the Couleeil, who ies-,ruc.:ed proceedings to be taken. With regard to the byel eves, no Parish Council or District Council had power to waive them. The bye- laws were law and were equally binding on the DiAtriet Connell as on the defendant. From July, 1901, every opportunity bed been given to the de- fendant to remedy the defects, and to meet the Council in every possible way, but he seemed to have kept them at arm's length. The first off ,nce was in regard to byelaw 54 which provided that the height of rooms should be 9 feet. In the house in question the height of the rooms on the ground floor varied slightly from 8 feet 6 iletees to 8 feEt 9 inches, and therefore the highest room was three inches be- low the required height- With regard to the bedroo-ns the front and middle rooms were only 8 feet 5i inches high, 'fiv back room was shown on the plan to be a coetinuous height of nine feet whereas it was nine feet lat one sid 3 only and was reduced to 7ft. 9in. at the the outer wall. The second offence was against bye- lawss. which was made to protect property seainst risk from fire. The hearthstones in this case projected in Isome cases only nine and in others 11 inettee from the chimney breast, whereas the byelaw required 18 inches. In addition to that the byelaw stated that from the sur- face of the hearthstone downwards tht re must be seven inches ef incombustible material, but in this case there was only four inches of material. There were five houses concerned in the ceee.</p>
        <p>John Swinton, Stockton Heath. surveror and sanitary inspector for the Bulwoith di4tict of the Runcorn Rural District Council, was then called and gave evidence bearing out this statement. Mr Smith questioned the wifUeSS at considerable length. He asked him if ;a committee had rot been ap- pointed by the Parochial Committee to consider which ot the byalaws of the Council might be omitted as being impracticable and unworkable. Witness : Toe object was to see which byelaws could be usefully worked upon. Mr Smith : Is it not a fact that there were 30 or 40 byelaw which were obviously wholly unworkable iu their existing form ? Witness : Ido not think so. This neighbourhood is r Aid6utial is it not ?—Yes. You do not suggegt that there are any tanneries in the neinhbourhood P—No. Do you know that there are loyelaws dealing with the business of a tannery ?:—Yes. _</p>
        <p>Further questioned witness admitted that there were no hone boilers, leather thessers, or soar) boilers carry- ing on businsss in the neighbourhood. There were bye- laws relating to those businesses. Mr Smith : D ) you agree with me that there are a large number of byelaws which cannot be applied in the neighbourhood ?—I do not agree with you. Can you apple in this neighbourhood byelaws that deal with tannin, leather dressing, and soap boiling ? Yes. Dow can you apply them if there is no one in the district carrying on the business ?—We should not need to. Is it not a fact that the byclews were borrowed from the byelaws of a different neighbourhood, that they are not suitable here, and you have yourself assisted in the formation of a committee whose object was to revise many byelaws which were inapplicable P—A com- mittee has been appointed to go through the byelawe. Perhaps it may make them stricter. We doz,'t know yet. Was the object of the committee to decide which bye- laws should be rejected as not being suitable or appli- cable in the neighbourhood ?—As riot being reqdired. The defender:it was appointed a member of the com- mittee ?—Yea. Can you, with the single exception of four cottages beiouging to Mr Davenport, point to a single cottage in this neighbourhood which does not violate your byelaws in respect of these two r oicets ? —I cannot swear that. Can you name new a single cottage of this class of property with the exception of the cottages of Mr Davenport, which does net violate both the byelaws ? Yes. I can point to a row of houses in Cawdor-street, which comply with the byelaw in respect of the height of rooms. lam not sure whether they do in respect of the hearthstones. Can you give me a single house in the whole of this neighbourhood in whieh the requirements of thebyelaw as to the hearths are complied with ?—No I cannot. Why have'et you taken out summonses against other builders of houses P—l have not been able to follow the matters up really. You have unfortunately had some small quarrel with the Jefendant P—No, I have not. Do you tell the Bench that the real reason that the byelaws have not been put in force against the people, is not that you yourself thoueht they were impracticable and unrcaasnable ?—ef Further cross-examined, witness said that almost every cottage in the neighbourhood failed to carry out the byelaws, but not se glaringly as in the present cese. Complaints had recently been made to builders in res- pect of the height of roams but summonses had not lama issued. Tee builders had appeared before Ike Cduncil. On being pressed witness said there were some things in the Council's byclaws which were unreason- able. Me did not know that the requirement as to height in Warrington was 8 feet 6 inches. Mr Smith then addressed the Bench and said they had not only the power but it was their duty to decide whether the hvelaws were reasonable er not. He quoted from "Knight's annetated model byelaws,"which stated that a byelaw to be in harmony with the laws of Eogland must be certain, determinate, and reasonable: If the Beach cern@ to the conclusion that the byelaws were unreasonable he shmld ask them to say that they were byelaws which they could not enforce to esistently with the. ruling. The Bench havine consulted the' Clerk on the I matter the Chairman said they could not see their way to declare the byelaws unreasonable or to take excep- tion to them. Mr Smith said that if, when tie Bench had heard the case they came to the conclusion that having regard to the circumstances the byelaws were not reasooar,lo,and then decided that they had *not power to go behind them, simply beeause they were byelaws, ha should ask them to state a case. James Wildingsa member cf the Municipal and County EaF.tineere Aseocietion, and surveyor to the Runcorn Urban Dietrict:Council,was called and corroborated with regard to the measurements. He eonsidered the byelaws were reasonable. In h's address for the defence Me Smith said the other side had told the Court that the defendant built the . houses without gaining the Council's approval of the plans. Mr Lake : I said the original plans were approved - not the amended ones. Mr Smith, continuing, said,t hat if that were so is wa perfectly competent for the Council to tak out a summons against the defendant for not conforming to their plans, and assuming the fact was as stated by Mr Leks defendant would hays had no defence to the summons. The Council, however, did not issue a summons for that. Continuing he pointed lout that the original plans were approved subject to the main drains being laid alms; the streets and passages I and he should satisfy the Benda that this work was carried out. This, therefore, involved an approval of</p>
        <p>There is no soap but Fels-Naptha, the money-back soap. 21-cl a bar. They belong to the past; they were good in their time. Fels-Naptha soap Peninsular House Monument London E C</p>
        <p>MR PEMBERI ON IN COURT the plans. The buildings were commenced in May and Mr Swinton ought to have known well enough what was 'AGAIN. going on in the houses unless be had grossly neglected his duty. If Mr Swinton bad gone on the premises up to Octob:r, ea it Wa.S his duty to have gone, he would Fined for Breach of a Building have been able to see that the ceilings required uteri- Byelaw. tic-ins and the delendent could at that time have altered them at the coat of £1 per ileum. There was,:therefore, no economy in the failure to carry out the byelaw. Mr wiaton, however, never made mention of the matter until November, when Mr nernberton received notice to show GAUSS se:ly the houses should not be pulled down. He (Mr Smith) thought it was a most excellent illustra- tion of the wisdom of the law in referring to the decision of magistrates, not govereed by these gusts of prejudice or passion, the question as to whether it was a proper thiog that tee fl,USei should be pulled down in this way, with cot any warning of any action from Mr Swinton or the Council. Mr Painbertonnec dyed a I tree. in July saying that if he altered the drains the plans would be approved and the next letter he received, Mr Swinton leaving visined the buildings several tunes, was mikitag him to show cause why the houses sheine not be pulled down. Eisen on the latter occasion the defendant did not recsive a notice of what the Council complained of in detail. Continue ine Mr Smith said that if the prosecution were a bona- fide attempt Dy a local authority to carry out for public purposes the pewerswhich the law bad given them, he should be sorry to stand there and ask the magistrates 0 exercise their p aver of disallowing them. Bat when he asked them to apply a te it he which they might determine whether the Couocii was making a bona-fide and interested attempt to carry out the powers g giv-n by the Act, he said there was oely oue test to be applied and that was " Are they marryin out these powers all round ; are they summoning other builders ?" He wondered that Mr Swinton was not ashamed toltell them he did se: know whether there was a sinele cottage in the wee e district in which the hearthstones were as required. Mr Swinton practically lead to admit that no attempt what- ever had been no ade in the district to insist upon the requirements of the byelaw in regard to the ceil- ings. Was tee Council justified in picking out this one men and saying they would pessibly cause him to de- ineliah his property ? Out of tee whole of that com- munity with these defects, the Council said " We will pick out this defendaer." Why, he (Mr Smith) did not meow. Whether there was some quarrel between the defendant and the Connsil or Mr Swiet on he (Mr Smith) did not know, but be ventured to suggest that the attitude which the Council had adopted towards the defend tot was only explicanle by the existence of some sort of personal amirnue. It was proved conclusively that the byela WAA net a bye- law which in the (opinion of the GDIVISIi WAS capable of being usefully applied throughout the wnole of the dis- trict. L there was one thing which the law insisted upon it was in the first place that ab; elaw should be reasonable,in the Sec end place it should be:dmerminate, and in the third piece it should be of universal i,pplice- tion. A public holy was nos entitled at the discretion of itself or of its offreee to pick out a mats here and there and say tecy weuld make him eompiy with the byelaw. As to the reasonableness of the byelaws in que.stion they:found they were not iniforce in large and populous districts, whsrs,if ever they were required they would be very much more required than in Stock- ton Elmth. As to their beide determinate notning could be more vague than the system of measurements of the hearths, and as to their general application no ettempt had teen made to enforce them and there had never been a prosecutiou for breaking them. With regard to the height of the remiss the byelaw did not provide what the system of meaeurement should be. As to the hearthstoties he slimel satisfy the bench absolutely that the hearths in their present form .contained every pre- cautien against fire which would have been attained if the byelaw had been most fully carried out, and furher, unless Mr Swinton had most grossly and incompetently neglected hils duty in the paste all these other hearthstones hail been in existence and there hod been this get etal dentem of csrnbuistien dur- ing all the years he had held oifice. Defendant never received a siaele notice caking him to alter the roofs or hearthstenes until the whole erection had beim corn- ple.cted and It was now practically imp ossibei for him to do it without almost pulling the buildings down and re- erecting them. He submitted that on tens ground alone the prosecution must atee utely fall. Jen° Pembsrton, the defendant, was called and in answer t Mr Smith said he had beets summoned once before by Mr S wiaton. He was the mile psrson in Stockton Heath who had ever been summoned and on that ocension he wa. acquitted. Until he received the ! letter of Novernbir 26,11 be was never asked to do any- thing to the property exceia: in respect of the drains. He carried out the alterations to the drains, ad the committeenapproved of the plan op that condition. lie retarned the plan and as he received no intimation of any otj cion he considered that the plan was accepted With roved to the hearthstones there were 5 latches of solid coucrete. He load always laid them in that way for eleven years and had never received any complaint. Everybody laid them that way and it eir es impossible for them t o get on fire. The height of the rooms could not be altered now without alinaist pulling the houses down. The average measurement was 8 feet 7k inches and including the thickness of the floor 9ft 31 ioetaes.At Warrington the measurement was 1 feet 6 ioches. Witnes3 was appointed a member of the committee which sat t 3 revise the beet:ewe because there were so many that could not be camied out or bad ne need to be carried out. In cross-examination defendant admitted that the houses were not quite in accordance with the deposited plans. ' Frederick William Wright, buil:lir and contractor, said be had built houses of the class concerned in the case at Warrington and Smckton Heath for about fifteen years' He had not seen the heerthe in question bet knew what they were like. his hearths were construceed praeticelly en the same priticiple, and he had never received a single complaint from the Surveyor. George3Heary Mason said he was a member of the Parochial Committee for five years, and in all hie experi- ence as a member he never knew of a single case in which a 5U111130113 WAS taken out against:a man either in respect of this roofs or hearths. He had never heard of a complaint, much less a summons. Mr Pemberton's houses were generally considered to be a good class. His property could to picked out by ita appearance. It was superior to any other property of a similar class. The unreasonableness of the byelaws had often been discussed and a committee was appoint- ed to look iato the matter In answer to Mr Lake witness said the bui/ders on the cemnaittee were the most keen on getting the byelaws Irevised. Mr Lake : To put byelaws in force you are bound to begin with someone and you could not begin better than wit, a membir of the committee ? Witness : I don't think that is a question you should put to me. The Bench considered their decision in private and on their return into court Thc Chairman said : The Bench have considered thie case to the best of taeir ability, end are of opinion that the byelaws are made to be observed, but in this case they have not bean of universal application in the dis- trict. Therefore we don't feel at linerty to do anything but pass by the question of the height of the rooms. In the other qmstion, as to the hearths, we consider that the defenclant is in fault, and we inflict a penalty of 203 and costs with the understanding that the matter is set right t 3 the satisfaction of the Council as kOOll as possible. Mr Leke : Do I understand that the summons as to the height of the rooms is dismissed, on the ground that the byelaw is not put in force ? The Chairman : Teat it is not of general application. This is the first ease that has been brought before us, and until we see that the byelaw is enforced generally we cannot think of applying it in that wash</p>
