        <p>11-LE E\ A ‘IINEI\, THURSD A k, MARCH 27 1902.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. EVERY tailor knows a lot of promising young men. "I like spoiled children." " Why ?" "It's. great fun to see them make their parents obey." A cynic is a man who pretends to be tired of the world, but in reality is a man of whom the world is tired. "WHAT do you do in a place like this?" Sandy —" Weel, DIOD, I usually swear like the de'il. I'm verra sorry to say." ROMANTIC MAIDEN "Look, Harry, such A beautiful sunset ! " Unromantic Harry: "Yes, exactly like a fried egg, isn't it? Miss GID: "I really don't know what to do with Charlie Westlake !" Miss Francis "Do without him." "PAPA, if you hadn't married mamma—" "Well?" "What a lot of fun me an' you could have—a doin' as we pleased." THE FLATTERER'S FULCRUM.—"Is Thompson susceptible to flattery ? " "Not unless you preface it by telling him you know he isn't." HE: "Let's go to the theatre to.night." She: "I daren't leave the baby, dear." He: "Well, I'll tell you what we'll do. I'll go." WILLIE—" Mamma, do only good little boys go to heaven?" Mother—" Yes my dear." Willie —" Guess they haven't many little boys up there, then, have they?" "LITTLE JOHNNY opened his drum yesterday to find where the noise came from." "Did he find out?" "Yes. When his father came home the noise came from Johnny."</p>
        <p>TRANSPARENT. 'Neath a parasol hard by the lake They kissed, but they made a mistake. Though both felt quite sure They were hidden secure, The parasol wasn't opaque. "Tms extravagance," said her husband gravely to himself, "requires a check." After thinking the matter over he wrote a chequa, and said nothing to her about it. DUNN : "What do you think of Dauber's latest picture, after Rubens ?" Brown: "1 think Rubens would be after Dauber if he could only see it." "DON'T you believe that what is to be will be?" asked the argumentative man. "I do not," declared the other one, "I do not even believe what has been has been." Semmx : "Vas money der root ohf all al, fader?" Isaac Slim: Yes, Sam; so you must dry and do all de good you can in life by gedding it away from people." Wive: "John, you have a very annoying habit of saying What's that?' whenever you are spoken to. Can't you break yourself of it?" Husbandi (reading) :•" Eh—what's that?" LAMY= (who has lost case) : "I am afraid we are done for. There is no higher court than this." Client: "I should imagine not, judging frem the price of this one." ' " BEASINGTON is the stingiest man I ever saw. Do you know what he's doing now?" "No ; what's his latest scheme for saving money "He's rented a room over a restaurant, so that he can inhale his meals without extra cost." Ma. Cl:mum : "I see that in the recent storm at sea a ship loaded with passengers went ashore." Mrs. Crumley, placidly: "How fortunate ! 1 can imagine how glad those passengers were to get on dry land." all in the dark about how these bills are to be paid," said Mr. Hardup to his wife. "Well, Henry," she said, as she pulled out a yellow one and laid it on the top of the pile, "you will be if you don't pay that one,%for that's the gas bill." Gomm had a sore throat, and the doctor ordered him a very unpleasant medicine. "How horrible it is," exclaimed the little fellow, pushing away the glass. "Look," said the father, "I am going to taste it; and he did so, but very ,gingerly. "Don't taste it, papa; drink it all.' AN old lady who is very much of a bore paid a visit to a family of her acquaintance. She pro- longed her stay, and finally said to one of the children "Pm going away directly, Tommy, and I want you to go part of the way with me." " Can't do it. We are going to have dinner as soon as_you leave," replied Tommy. TEDDY (who has just begun to go to school) : "Papa, do you know what six boys and five girls make?" "Yes," answered his father—" a noise." • THE FATHER " Willie "—whack—" this hurts me"—whack—" almost as much as it "—whack— "does you "—whack. Willie; Then keep it up. I can stand it on those terms." . . .</p>
        <p>.., ~,... "YES, the man is under arrest for conspiring to procure the death of his mother-in-law. He'll be convicted too." "What did he send her? Poisoned candy?" "No. A folding bed."</p>
        <p>Wrra (crossly) : "Well, have your own way, and then you'll be satisfied." Husband: "I'm not so certain about that. I had my own way when I married you, but I'm not satisfied I" A COMFORTER—Young Mr. Isaacs: "Der fact is, fader, I haf had a deesappointraedt in lofe." ' His Father: "Veil, cheer up, my poy—cheer up! Suppose it vos a deesappointmedt in peez- nese !" MIKE: "Well, anyway, I kin flatter rnesilf that I was nivver so droonk that I didn't know what I was doin'." Pat: "An' be the same token, ye war nivver so sober that ye did. POLICEMAN : "Come along now quietly, or it will be the worse for you." Toole: "I'll not. The magistrate told me last time never to be brought before him again, an' I'm going to obey his instructions."</p>
        <p>THE SEASON. Now Cholly surely counts himself A lucky summer man, For first on the engagement ring Appeared the name of "Ann:" When next it served one letter more Made "Anna" very well; And for the third girl 'twas with ease Transformed to "Annabel."</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOLBOY'S VERSION.—" Let me make the legal holidays of the nation and I care not who makes the rest of tho laws." POOR CONIFORT.—Patsy : "An' phy can't Oi go in swimmin' ?" Mrs. Donovan : "'Cause ye don't know how, darlint. But jist's soon's ye can s Nim Oi'll not hov th' laste objiotions to yore pin' into th wather." SHE: "Take your arm from about my waist at once, sir ! I never saw a man act so in all my whole life!" He: "You mistake me dear. l'm not acting, I'm in earnest." VISITOR: "Well, Johnny, I suppose your father thinks the twins are something wondeenl. Johnny: "Yes, but (in a confidential whieper) I could lick 'em both, easy." "THOMAS, how would you correct the sentence, 'He laid down on the floor'? "I wouldn't correct it at all ma'am. It's all righls3' "how do you make that out, Thomas 1" 'He was carrying an armful of down, ma'am, and he laid it on the floor." _ . _</p>
        <p>--• . MR. GRAVES (Ohicagb soap merchant) : "I say, Elfrida, you've got to break off with that Baron Sourcrout." Miss Graves: "Way, p-p•papa ?" Mr. Graves: "Well, a fellow this Morning told me that I won't be any more of a baron than I arm now after your marriage, and if I ain't a-going ter be a noble ye'll 'av' ter marry a man." An angry small boy was pelting stones at a noisy dog when a venerable passer-by stopped and addressed him. "Little boy," the strartger re- monstrated, "don't you know you should be kind to dumb animals?" "lee," replied the angry boy; but what's dumb animals got to do with yelping dogs?" INSANITY IN TILE FAMILY.—Doctor: " Your hus- band appears to be run down, anxious and over- worked, but I see no signs of insanity." Mrs. Da Fashion: "I am sure he is in danger of it. In- sanity runs in the family, you know." Doctor: "Does it ? " Mrs. De Fashion: Yes, indeed. Two of his sisters had chances to marry rich men and then married poor ones." SCENE: Soldiers' liarrack•room at dinner time. Orderly Officer (inspecting men's dinner) asks: "Any complaints, men ? " Voice from the end of the table: "Yes, sir." Officer : " Well, what is it?" Voice "Spuds is bad, sir." Officer; " Sprids is bad 1 Ilaw—er !" (Turning to sergeant —" Spuds is bad I Haw I" What does he mean by spuds Sergeant Murphy ? " Sergeant (glaring at culprit—" The man is higgerunt, sir. He means " taters I"</p>
