      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>I THE REPAIR OF THE TORPEDOBOAT DESTROYER.</head>
        <p>I THE REPAIR OF THE TORPEDO- BOAT DESTROYER.</p>
        <p>I One of the most interesting shio-repair jobs yet I carried out in this country is that now being completed I at Shserness D ockyard, of which Captain Gerald C Langley is Captain Superintendent, This work ig in con- nection with the reconstruction of the torpedo-hoat Idestroyer Salmon, which, as a result of collision with one of the pas ienger steamers of the Great Etetern Rail- way, was almost cut in two pirts transversely, one-half of tier length. the bow par, being driven out of lino to the extent of 15ft. at the stem, listed to starboard to the extent of 510. in 12 it., while the keel was from 6 in. amidships to 21 in. at the stem lower than the stern part, which remained unaffected. Such s contortion in all three pTssilsie diree'.:load is al not uniqus for although there have Nil 1 Cf9P3 of torpedo-boat destroyers having their bows stove in concertina-wise, by running against some obstacle of greater tenacity than the ductile steel of which they have been built, there have berm no ca, es where &amp;maize had been so ex- tensive without complete loss ; and similarly there have been few instances whore the metal has been called upon to stand such severe stress, and has proved so satisfac- ter/. This demonstration of the extent of fatigue which steel of suitable quality will survive is peculiarly oppoituaa at a time vreo discuS don oolthis point ig:rais- ed owing to the unfortunate acoident to the Cobra and ; tho 3 the views of the Salmon, alon?, with the narrative, and particularly the results of the tensile test of the darnaETed ma*erial, which Eogin.ering " putlishes by the kind permission of My Lords Commissioners of the Admiralty, should prove of exceptional interest.</p>
