      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DEATH CF COLONEL WINDUS, OF ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>DEATH CF COLONEL WINDUS, OF ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Lieutenent.Colonel Thomas Windus, who had been connected with the St Helens Volunteer Corps over forty veers, died at his resideuce, Windle—,treet, Sc fislens,en Senday morning, aged seveuty-three. Mr Macias was a native of Orreil, near Wigan, but had beau in Sc Helens nearly sixty years, and had a lifeloug connec- tion with the St Helene Foundri, wnere he held the office of general rnaaager since the year 1853. He WAS most highly respected as a worthy eilezon and a skilful eugiaeer. Mr Windee had been unable to follow his duties for six or St3Van MOnth3, his last visit t) the works being made on nis birthday, November 19:11. He succumbed to an internal malady. Deceastei'e father was connected with the late Mr Daglish at Orrell Collieries. As a boy ho attnsded Upholiand Grammer &amp;sheet, and at the age of fourteen he entered the office of Messrs Daglish at the St Helens Foundry. Ha made great headway in hie profession, and at the early age of twenty-five was pro- moted to toe post of general manager. Among the notable men who have been under Mr Windus's direc- tion is the Hen R J Seddon, the Premier of New Zealand, who was apprenticed witn Messrs Daglish. The skill and ability Mr Windus displayed in designing and constricting engines and pumps of exceptional capacity has resulted in the Sr. Helena Works being renowned far and wide. Immediately on his appointment as general manager he collaborated with Mr T Hawkisey, who afterwards devised the Vyrnwv Waterworks, in designing the pumping engines fer the Ecelesron Hill Waterworks, for supplying St. Helene. The Southport Waterworks followed, with the Wirral, Bristol, Warrington, and Nreterley Waterworks. He else designed the drainage of 5,000 acres of land in the Cambridge Fens, and the splendid pumping engines erected for the St. Helens Water Committee at Kirkby in 1891 In the drainage of mines as well as the construction of huge colliery winding engines Mr Windus was also most successful. He was the pioneer of Cornish pumpiog engines on the Continent, and he designed the engines for draining the mines at Marseilles with a steam cvlio- der 84. iuches diameter by 10 feet stroke ; also for Lord Lonsdale's pits at Whitehaven, Sherelley Collieries, St Helens; WeAphalia Coe! Cempany,Prussia; Pewfail pits, Haydock ; Walthew House, Wigan, exc. In 1876 he invented the Mince reversing motion and the sliding earn lifters for colliery winding enginss, and the firm turned out numerous sets of eegines,a notable set being supplied to the Windsor Pitte, Pontypridd, capable of winding 4 tons of coal from a depth of 700 yards in the brief space of 40 seconds, Railway and Canal bridges, plate-glass machinery, and chemical furnaces of all descriptions were also turned out under his direc- tion. In 1893 Messrs Daglish and their employees, past and present, entertained Mr WilllU9 at dinner, and ha was presented with an illuminated address acid a gold watch and guard is recognition of his fifty years con- nection with the firm. In 1859 Mr Windus was sworn in as a Volunteer, and in 1889 ne was promoted to the henerary rank of lieutenant-colonel, and in February', 1893, was decorated with the Volunteer officers' long- service medal, and on Ms retirement from the corps eves granted permission to retain his rank and to wear the uniform of the corps. He ha I for many years been a prominent member of the St Helens Conservative Club. At St Mark's Churcli, tee Sunday morning, Mr Hey- wood, organist,played the" Dead March," Mr Windus having been A Pirle4Mal there since the church was erected about fifteen years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr., Mrs, and Miss Ward. Mr and Mrs Ward,the retiring master and matron of the Workhouse, were on Saturday the recipients of a handsome silver tea service,subscribed for by the entire staff of the Workhouse as a token of esteem an I regard on their coming retirement. The pre- sentation was made in suitable terms, and Mr and Mrs Ward, who spoke under deep ensotion,returned thanks, informing those present that they would never forget the many friends they wonlfl leave behind them in Wartingtln. Miss Ward (the ussis- tsnt matran). who left Warrington for Southamp- ton the same day,was presented with a very pretty silver present from the officers, A. list of the sub- scribers, and a letter appreciative of the kindly way In which they had always been treated,accompanied the presents. Mr and Mrs Ward left Warrington on Tuesday for Ellesmere, where they will reside for the present. It may be interesting to note that Mr and Mrs Ward have been in charge at the workhouse for 15 years,and that they were ap- pointed during the chairmanship of the late Rev. F Roberts.</p>
        <p>Ragged School and Mission Band of Hope Society. The fourth entertainment of the season was given in the Mission Room on Monday evening, when there was a large audience, The Chairman (Mr R A Naylor), who had come specially from London, gave an encouraging address, after which a capital programme was gone through, consisting of songs, solos, and recitations,. and two cornet solos by Mr Booth. The entertainment concluded with a dialogue entitled "The Flower Qneen " by the sbuior menbers of the Rand of flops. The accom- panist was Mr Owen. A vote of thanks was passed to Mr Naylor for presiding, and to Mr and Miss Sparks,who have the training of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PROFIT SHARING AT WARRINGTON. Messrs. Richmond's Distribute £2,000</head>
        <p>PROFIT SHARING AT WARRINGTON. Messrs. Richmond's Distribute £2,000</p>
        <p>Farther evidence was afforded of the vitality of the profit sharing scheme of the Richmond Gas Stove and Nleter Company ,Limited on Monday evening when the sixth general opeetiag of the employes was held and the fifth distribution of pro- fits made. The meeting was held in one of the moulding shops in Academy-street, Warrington,and some hundreds of workmen, with grimy faces, assembled. , Mr IL W P Rieumond, managing direc- tor and founder of the company, presided and he was accompanted on, the platform by Mr J A Hen- some, Mr Hughes and Mr Davey (officials of the company). Prior to the distribution,the Chairman briefly ad- dressed the employes. He was very pleased,he said,to meet them again ou that the, fifth annual distribu- tion of profits. Hitherto it had been his lot ,on those occesione to report a growing business and increasing profits. This year they had had an increasing business, and they would know how busy they had been at War- rington, but they had had to - experience conditions which bad reduce] prices. As a Matter of fact they bad 'done a .1 arger busidess and made a less profit out of it than hitherto. That wee not a desirable state of affairs. MarlietH and materials went up in prices. They obtained an advance in the price of their stoves but the advance was not continued long enough. The gas companies insisted on the reduction being made before it was watranted. They had to make I the reduction in the price of stoves in March last year and it ought not to have been made until Christmas. The result had been that their profits had gone down considerably. Fortunately other 'branches of the business had done remarkably well and the effect bad been that the company had been well able to pay ten per cent Last year the com- pany paid ten per cent and a bonus of n per cent Now the rules of the profit sharing scheme were that when the profits were sufficient to pay more than six per cent, the emplayem should participnte in the profits, and the company had kept ite word. (Hear, bear.) 'this year, tbe employee were really not entitled to participate in the profits because the profits in the stove trade—which was the in- dustry carried on when the scheme was started bad not realised the whole of the dividend paid. Nevertheless the comptiny was going to pay the bonus (Hear, hear.) The directors felt that the principle of profit sharing was far • too important to be Jeopardised by a question of what was strictly fair cleating. It would have been fair for the directors to have said : "It is true we have paid ten per cent but as it has not been earned in the Stove branch we are not going to give any bonus thia year." The bonus Would cost the company £2,000 and they were going to give it. (Applause.) When the scheme was inaugurate,' it was not foreseen that the profits might be derived _ . .</p>
        <p>from some other source and the rules did not state explicitly that the profits must be made by the stove depertment. It had, therefore, been neces- sary to alter the rules to read " Bonuses shall be Calculated on the net. proflte 'earned by the Rich- mond Stove branch of the company, as certified by the zonapany's auditors, If that net profit is in- sufficient to pay six per cent on tne ordinary share capital employed in tbe Richmond Stove branch of the company, no bonus will be paid." He must compliment Mr Hughes and those associated with him on the expeditions manner in which the amenthid rules had been placed before \the employes at such short notice. Be might tell t hum that before the alteretion was made he went to see Mr Livesey, of tbe South Metropolitan Gas Company, and they talked the matter over very carefully. Mr Livesev expressed the opinion that be thought in was most honourable condue on the part of Messrs Richmond's to pay the employe £2,000 seeing that the stove branch had not earned it Be was glad Co say that Mr Livesey approved of the ae, teratien of the rules. Previously, the rules said that th bonuses would be c ticulated on the eet profits earned bY toe company, Mat at that time they did not anticipate I that they were going to make meters as well as stoves. It was now necessare t 3 differentiate and make the pro- fits clone department stand distinct from those made by another.' When the profits were sufficient to pay six per cent, then a bonus of three per emit would be paid,and for every one per cent of:di- vidend earned above six per ceet, a further one half per cent on wages would , be paid, provid.d always that the bonus to the Riebmend stotte emplot 63 should not ex- need one half per cent•for every one per cent of dividend paid on the ordintry shares of the amalgamated com- pany. There was one other point which he must men- tion. In future the three months' qualifying service must be completed prier to the 1s: ot January in each veer. No one would be able to qualify on the Ist July in future. This year they were hoping t 3 have a more profitable year. The year 1900 was a very good year, and they meanie more profits in that year with one busi- ness than they made in 1901 with two businesses. The present year had opened very promisinely. They had started with tbeir exhibitions, and the results were very satisfactory. He hoped that when he Csms tefortethem nest year, the result of the year's working would have been more remunerative. They were doing an immense business, which was not only extending to every town and village in the United Kingdom but was developing in far away cotters of the earth. Ships were taking out thair gooda to all sorts of places—some of them known by name and others possibly unknown ta them if they heard the names. .He hoped they would remember as they got away from that hall that it had cost the com- pany £2OOO to keep their word. That was a good round sum to pay for giving your wore foe s thing. (Hear, hear.) If they saw anybody doing anything which they thought was not in the interests of the firm he hoped they would remeentar what the firm had dome for its employes. He trusted it would cenvince them that the flrm's determination was to that fairly and gener- ously with them and he had little doubt but that the action of the company would be reciprocated by the employee. (Applause.) Mr. RANSOMS said the action of the firm proved that there was no desire set curtailithi profit shaving scheme. Mr. Richmond was extremely anxious to do all be possibly could for It. The scheme was Mr Richmond's ides, and he was anxious to make it a suecess. It was ' only due to the unfortunate circumstance that the profits in the stove department were a little less that the firm had beea °legged to more strictly deflate', the basis of the bonue. They Were looking forward to a better year in 1902, when he hoped the profits would be larger than ever. (Applause.) He desired them to think of the scheme not merely for one month of the year when the profits were distributed but all the year round, and a 5 partnere in the cor cern, endeavour to serve its intsres's to the beat of their ability. With such an able emeiral at the head of affairs as Mr Rich- mond they were bound to succeed if the rack and file aii their duty. (Hear, hear). In conclusion, Mr Ran- some proposed a vote of thanks to Mr:Rid:mond for his and for presence distributing the profits. (Applause.) Mr HUGHES cordially seconded the vote of thanks. Mr DAvET, in supporting', expres3ed the hope that they might be'able to see Meßichmond a little oftener at the Warrington works. The vote ot thanks was carried with acclamation. Mr Eleanore) ackreaccle.dged the compliment atm said be knew be had a lot cf Wen&amp; at the,Warrin.4ton works The employes at Warrington and Stratford looked upon the directors not merely as employers but as friends— (beer, got employee hear,)—aye ned he believed the firm thought they b who were not mere machines but men, who did not only a day's work but a first-class day's work and the firm was willing to pay for it and bonus them as well. (Hear. bear.) The? desired to have in every department of the-works at Warrington and Stratford, the strictest econotay. They had a magnificent business for the length of time it had been established. It waseandotabtediy the lab gest business of Its kind in the Country for the time it bad been running, It Was a very great question whether at the present time they did not make more gar-cookisg stoves than any cher firm in the United Kingdom, and If they sold more than any one else in this country then they sold more than anyone else ha the world. They believed that as far as cooking stoves were concerned Richmond's stood first. That was very proud position to attain in se short a time. It sh mild be a source of encouragement to them. (Hear, hear). Those who were receiving a less bonus to-day must not forget that the beads of the firm were losing more in the ageregate. This year, the firm was distri- buting 705 primer-shooing shares in the Warrington branch, and in addition 106 went to Dublin, makine 811 from this branch of the business. The sum of £24 $s 7d had been paid in by the men to make odd money into level therm. The directors were very glad to see so many of the workmen taking up ordinary ahareg. Alto- gether, including the staff and officials there were over 5,000 £1 ordinary shares subscribed for and were brine paid far at the rate of a shilling per share per .month. He Considered it a fine record, and it proved that profit-sharing bad shown the employe; the advantages of investing their mcney in the concera in which they worked. It also proved that the men reCoge Dived that the firm was trying to reconcile the conflict- ing interests of capital and labour. He thought it weld be impossible to go to any other concern in War- rington, an offer the men ordinary shares in the com- pany and find theta taken up in any quantity. They would not have had confidence in it if they had not bten trained by means of a profit-sharing scheme to see the advantages of it. The odd money in the envelopes which he was about to distribute was £133 93 sd. In conclusion Mr Richmond expressed the hope that it would give the employes as much plessdre to receive the enmslopes as it gave him to distribute them. *(Applause). , The distribution was then made, and t e interesting proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
