      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>The Government has de- Alien Immigration. cided to meet the cry from the East End of London by the appointment, of a Royal Commis- sion. Its constitution has just been announced, and the interesting statement is added that the members will be empowered "to visit and person- ally inspect such places as may be deemed ex- pedient"; and so we may expect that some of the grim realities of London life will be brought out— as a matter of fact, seriously printed in Blue- books. Mr. HENRY NORMAN is to be a member of the Commission, and his journalistic instinct should serve this roving inquiry in good stead. We are glad to notice, also,that Lord ROTHSCHILD is included on the Commission. This is partly a Jewish question, and the head of the famous financial house which had its origin in a small street in Frankfort-on-the-Maine has shown in many ways his sympathy for those of his race wh° are at the bottom of the ladder. It is well-known, too, that the Jews, by the aid of their Board of Guardians, keep most of the people of their faith off the rates; and that is a consideration which will weigh in this matter. The real complaint about the hordes of destitute aliens who come to our country, and of whom the Polish and other Jews are only a tithe, is that they are prepared to work under conditions which to our people repro. sent acute suffering. So long as they have just enough to keep body and soul together they are content ; and they have no aspirations for cleanli- ness or sanitation. They are the chief offenders in the overcrowding of which such horrible statistics are given,and while the wages they get are so low it seems useless to drive them from place to place. • That is one of the problems which the Commission has to deal with, and, seeing that the smallpox epidemic is putting up the local rates in various parts of London, it is a matter of interest to all the community. The movement to purify the plague spots is not coming too soon. On the other hand, there are two broad considerations which cannot be lightly passed over. This country would have lost its prosperity long ago but for its attraction to foreigners, whose mixture with us,in so far as they have given us of our best, has corrected the defect of insularity. Several of our most important arts and industries have been carried to success by the technical knowlege and enthusiasm of :the persecuted people who have settled among us. The Huguenots strengthened our race, and some of the most violent politicians of other countries become calm and useful citizens when they settle among us, and are ledj to reflect how foolish it is to be violent by the object-lessons of folly which the orators in Hyde Park offer. I Further we have to remember that, through good and evil report,despite all the hatred,jealousy,and uncharitableness which exists among our Con- tinental rivals, we enjoy a Ihigh reputation among many of the poor abroad for the asylum we afford to political refugees, and for our refusal to take the side of the strong against the weak in Continental broils. The people who come here and build up fortunes are also very proud of the country, and claim to be Englishmen by something more than birth—by deliberate adoption, or, in other words, by naturalisation. If we arvoing to draw a hard and fast line and say that only the profitable shall stay within our kingdom, these benefits will go. We may be faced with a worse evil. Instead of our manufacturers employing foreigners at inadequate wages, the time may come when German manufacturers— better educated and sharper than our own—may set up works in England to employ the English as , their servants. It is better to have a fusion of races in all grades than that. There have been many debates in the House o Commons on this subject, and members have been reluctant to take a decided line. They will now have collected for them materials on which to come to a judgment. To anticipate that evidence except by generalities would be unfair; but the question affects the pro- vinces as well as the metropolis, and it is to be hoped that information will be forthcoming of any large alien settlements in the provinces as well as in London, and of the,effect of alien competition on the labour market generally.</p>
        <p>It is stated in a report just issued by Earl Grey's Public House Trust Association that there are only nine counties in England in which no steps have been taken for the adoption of licensing re- form on the lines supported by that body. We are afraid, however, this includes several in which the steps taken have been of a distinctly preliminary character, and some, perhaps, in which there have , been merely a series of meetings for the purpose of ' talking the matter over. Yet that the progress is slow need give little surprise and no anxiety to those who are in favour of the system. Them is a , vast amount of inertia to be overcome before the ' public can be induced to take an interest in any philanthropic movement, even when the philan- thropy is of the kind coupled with interest at 5 per cent. When, however, the thing has once been I set going, inertia has a way of becoming momentum. Still, with the few houses which are actually open under its auspices, the Association claims that it has been successful. A fair margin of profit has been made to devote to public works, without any effort to push the sale of alcoholic liquors; and there has beek no trouble except in one case, where the circumstances were rather peculiar, a very popular manager having been dis- placed in order to make room for the new state of things. Failure is recorded in one or two cases of an attempt to shorten the hours during which the houses are open, the magistrates deciding that this was illegal. However, opinions seem to differ on the subject, for in one district a public-house has adopted early closing without any protest from the guardians of law and order. On the whole, the stage does not seem yet to have been reached for a confident judgment how far this modified Gothenburg system adapts itself to English habits and customs.</p>
        <p>If we are to believe Dr. William Wallace, the Glasgow Exhibition of last year has completely transformed the character of the Scottish people. It had made that characteristic product of Glas- gow, "the man with the square hat," dictator of Scotland. It has turned Scottish enthusiasm for dogmatic religion and Radical politics to art, to music, to athletics, and to sport. Dr Wallace probably proves too much. Still, his article on "The Transformations of the Scottish Tempera- ment" in the April Fortnightly is amusing and interesting, and shows him to have both a keen power of observation and a strong sense of hum- our. It is singular far English writers, as a rule, have erred in their estimate of the Scot in his own land. He is always looked upon as the model of caution pushed to extremes, whereas Dr Wallace shows that rashness of the wildest kind has been the national characteristic. Even Buchanan's allusion to the perfervidum ingentum Scotorunt has been misunderstood, and what was intended for a reproach taken to be a compliment. However, that rapid social changes are going on north of the Tweed, that old habits of thought, forced on the people by the stress of poverty, are being dropped, and a new type being developed,is not to be doubted. As Dr. Wallace points out,the very enthusiasm which Lord Rosebery excites in Scotland may be taken as symptomatic. The Glasgow merchant who used to take his commercial correspondents to sit beneath his favourite minister, and then ask them, triumphantly, " Can you bate that ? " now crowds to hear Lord Rose-</p>
        <p>bery, who, apart altogether from polictics, is an artist and a humorist, the ideal of what the man in the square hat, aware of his own deficiencies, yet longs to be.</p>
        <p>liord Newton is now, of course, independent of the electors of the division he used to represent in Parliament, or he would not, we imagine, so fre- quently force down their throats his own pet I theory of conscription. Though we have a good opinion of his Lordship's outspokenness and candour, we have never yet been able to see rhyme or reason in his proposals on behalf of conscription. At Earlestown this week he expressed the opinion that conscription was the only method Iby which we should be able to avoid the unpreparedness in which we were found at the outbreak of the war. Doubtless his scheme would obviate any such unpreparedness, but conscription is not the only method" by which so desirable a result may be obtaiaed. Surely an efficient administration can solve a problem of such elemen- tary simplicity without resorting to a compulsory call to arms—which would not be obeyed. If the army is enlarged and strengthened in the manner suggested recently and the volunteer force placed on a proper military footing, there will be no such thing as being caught napping when the time for action comes, nor any cause whatever for noble lords to look enviously on the conscript as the man of the hour. The British nation would not tolerate conscription for a moment, and the sooner Lord Newton realises this fact and the better.</p>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A. Provincial .Letter. Entertaining Royalty is proverbially an expensive luxury. It has transpired that the recent Royal visit to Dartmouth cost so much that a " deficit " ot about five hundred pounds has to be made up out of the rates, which will mean an additional sixpence in the pound for the ratepayers to contribute. The expenditure reached 'nearly £9OO. It may be mentioned here, perhaps, that certain Urban District authorities having applied to the Local Government Board for so.nction to the raising of contributions out of the rates towards the cost of the local Coronation Celebra- tions, the reply is that the Board contemplated issuing a general order authorising reasonable ex- penditure for that purpose. Consequently, a num- ber of provincial councils have within the last few days made rates of a penny or more in the pound todefray the cost of the forthcoming festivities.</p>
        <p>By-the-bye, the 'proclamation by King Edward this week to the effect that the 213th of June next, which is on a T.lursdav, and the following Ftiday are to be observed al Bank Holidays, will simplify the work in all parts of the provinces. The declara- tion of the Bank Holidays practically means general holidays, and this will enable local csmmittees to formulate their respective programmes regarding which, in some districts at least, there had until , Monday been a degree of uncertainty. A decided effort may now be made to render the ,actual Coronation Day attractive to the majority of the inhabitants everywhere, and to differentiate be- tween the amusements of that day and those which are available on ordinary bank holidays, when large crowds are usually left to their own devices. The hope that the Coronation may take place under the blessed influence of peace is revived by some of the latest news from South Africa, and even by the guarded statement that was made in the House of Commons on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>There is always a charm in the near approach of Easter; for then we at least know that " The time of the singing of birds has come," and it is pleas- ant to be assured, if only by the almanac and not by the temperature, that the winter is over and gone. The first public holidsy of the year, more- over, appears to come as a reminder that even work and the war and the great tobacco" combines" do not occupy absolutely all our thoughts, but that in the lives of the great masses of the people there is room left for the sympathies and pleasures which these boddays permit them to enjoy. It is not the fault of Nature if Easter Monday, which will be with us a little earlier than ordinarily this year, finds the opening of the cricket season absurdly premature, '-but it is impossible not to admire the courage with which various clubs, having made up their, minds that it is time for summer weather, arbitrarily submit their plans, and expect Nature to conform to them. Toe click of the at will in a day or two be a welcome and seasonable sound, and already footballers have been reminded that, no matter what the weather may be, they are lagging superfluous on the field, That at this period of the year rain is more probable than in summer, if we are to believe the meteorologists, is a delusion ; for these experts have told us that at present we are in the midst of what may be called the dry season —February, March, April and May having smaller rainfall on the average than any of the other months of the year. Of late years, however, the holidays have given us nothing unusual in the way of weather, and millions, probably, will at this moment be living in the hope that Monday next , may not prove exceptional in this respect.</p>
        <p>Bakers and confectioners, if fairly and accurately represented in the "Bakers Times," would fain abolish the Good Friday bun. But, as with other threatened institutions, the evidence is that this one, if it dies at all, will not give up the ghost without a struggle. The organ in question will have the public believe !that the hot cross bun "was two years ago doomed and banished for all time." But this, to say the least, involves some exaggeration. For, turning to last year's files, one finds statements to the effect that "in spite of the protests made by a number of bakers, hot cross buns were probably more largely consumed to-day than in any previous year," and so forth. The young people are, no doubt, the firmest supporters of the custom, but they manage to beguile their elders in to bravely risking the horrors of indigestion and partaking of the fragile morsels. Few persons trouble to remember that " hot cross buns " can be had almost anywhere and on any day in the week all the year round, minus only the cross. But on Good Friday they !lave a special flavour, and are absolutely essential to a fitting commemoration of that day. The bakers really make a considerable profit on the supply,and the spirit of the grumblers among them is not the spirit of this latter day competition in trade, which, whether they like it or not, they must face or be prepared for the lag- gard's merited atternative.</p>
        <p>At the annual conference of Friendly Societies at Manchester, representing a membership of more than four millions, the remarkable statement was made that in a year these societies pay more in sick and funeral benefit than the whole of the out- door relief granted by boards of guardians. Of course, the vexed question of old age pensions was discussed. When the matter was before the House of Commons a day or two previously, it was made to appear that the principal difficulty in the way of any scheme would be that of finding the money for it. The difficulty involved in the resolution arrived at by Friendly Society members is of another kind. The question raised by them relates to the conditions on which old age pensions may or shall be awarded. As at first proposed, the resolution provided that for a person to be entitled to an old age pension of five shillings per week at 65, he must show;that he has been a member of a thrift society for at least twenty years. As ultimately adopted, however, a pension of five shillings or more, was claimed by the resolution, for " all thrifty and deserving persons of 65 and upwards, who are un- able to work and ard in need of the same." This resolution, though, is not to be considered the scheme of the Conference until laid before delegates of the various societies represented. The great difficulty of the resolution as it stands will be to devise some means of deciding who are "thrifty and deserving." The Guardians would appear to be the body most fitted to undertake this work ; but if they do so there will be no great distinction be- tween the old age pension and a little extra out- door relief. If, however, this is to be the plan, it would be a comparatively simple matter to limit expenditure on such pensions. The guardians of each union might be empowered to create a num- ber of old age pensions limited to a specified amount.</p>
        <p>Provincial newspaper readers have no doubt ob- served that the central principle of the new Educa- tion Bill, introduced this week by Mr Balfour on behalf of the Government, is divided into two parts. With the first of these it is possible to concur abso- lutely. That is that there will be one educational</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MARCH 27th 1902.</head>
        <p>MARCH 27th 1902.</p>
        <p>" Ecorzstasriwo. as. The Education Bill. cendancy rather than educational efficiency," that is Dr. Clifford's opinion of the aim of the Bill broadly outlined by Mr BALFOUR in the House of Commons this week. That judgment is passed of course,subject to the text of the measure filling in with its forecast by Mr. BALFOUR,. The speech of the leader of the House altogether was a most remarkable one, and so much that seemed to be contemplated at the beginning was made optional at the end that any final verdict upon it is impos- sible at the present juncture. That it is an insi- dious attempt to support denominationalism out of the ratepayers' money does, however seem. pretty evident. The idea underlying the whole lino of argument was that all schools, both Board and voluntary, should come under the authority of the County Councils and Borough Councils, but this authority shall be deliberately used to retain the denominational character of the voluntary schools, and the ratepayers' money shall be em- ployed to support the instruction given. To those who are curious as to the " locus standi" of the denominationalists in these circumstances, Mr. BaLroua makes reply that they supply the school buildings, and will be required to keep them in order. Indeed, if they are willing to make fresh shells,or in other words to erect fresh buildings,de- nominationalisra shall be encouraged still more by the State. At the present time, under the sheltering care of successive Tory Governments voluntaryism still has a majority of the children of this country, the School Board system being an organisation of comparatively recent growth. The Government proposes to step in, relieve the de- nominationalists of all expense of instruction, and coolly perpetuate at the cost of the nation at large this state of things. And it is to be done in the name of efficiency I A Ministry which was returned to power on the strength áf Imperial en- thusiasm in regard to South Africa proposes to hold a pistol at the head of the Britich . artisan and tell him that he must back the voluntary schools or go behind in the educational race. That is the plain English of Mr BALFOUR'S statement. "Our system compared with those of America Germany, France, and other countries," said he 'IS the most inadequate and ineffectual." There- fore what he practically says is" You bolster up Voluntary schools or be ruined: we won't keep you in any other way." The threat is perfectly obvious, and it is only as the reader draws near to the close of the speech he realises that the Govern- ment, although professing so much earnestness on the subject, is in fact afraid to take re- sponsibility for its own plan, and glad of any possibility of shifting the burden. Mr BAL- OUR' *' s explanation of the permissive clauses re- calls that cartoon of Lord JOHN RUSSELL writing "No Popery "on the wall and then running away. This question is urgent, and there is no other means of solving the problem. But after all it does not matter about London—the metropolis , can wait—and as for the rest of the County Councils and the Countyßoroug,h Councils, there's no hurry, they can adopt it in their own pleasure, at their own time, whenever they are inclined to.' That is what the qualifications to the Bill would suggest. More, it might be assumed that the Bill, if not very useful, was comparatively harmless because many County Couticils will probably refuse to adopt it ; but it is prejndical to good government in several respects. If carried in the Present shape it may turn County Council elec- tions into religious controversies, and so prevent consideration of many other matters which ought to have weight in these elections. Those Councils which are averse to denominationalism will be compelled to reject the proposal, and in those areas the education system will continue unsatis- factory. For quite other reasons, too, those of niggardly economy, Councils which are indifferent about religious points, will refuse to come in, and SO for years our educational system will be uncer- tain and irregular. Instead of system Mr BALFOUR would give us chaos.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>no EXAMINER THURSDAY. MARCFE 27, 1902.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>authority for elementary, secondary, and technical education, which is very excellent as far as it goes. But it may be recalled that a great English preacher said he entirely concurred with the chief principle of Mohammedanism ; the declaration of that faith consisting first, in the statement of there being but one Almighty, and this was admirable. Next it says, however, that Mohammed is His pro- phet, and this, of course, is false, Therefore, while educationists in the provinces agree that there should be one educational authority in every ais- trict, opinions will differ when the next half of the Government's central principle is stated, that this authority shall not be the bchool Board, or even a new body created to deal with the whole of educa- tion, but a committee, of the county or borough council, joined by other nominated and representa- tive persons. The way in which the Government have arrived at this decision is ingenious enough, but the point is one that used not be pressed just now.</p>
        <p>2/6 PRA IMPERIAL QUART SCOTCH ANDEsIRISH WHISKEY.</p>
        <p>ALSO AT 3/-, 3/9, 4/6 AND 5/3 PER QUART, 1/8, 2/-, 2/6, 3/-, AND 3/6 PER BOTTLE, FULTON BROS WINE MERCHANTS, 20, RENSHAW STREEr, LIVERPOOL, (2 Minutes from Central and Lime Street Stations.)</p>
