      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>There is to be no contest for seats in the gilded chamber after all. Presumably the would be candi- dates have considered that discretion is the better part of valour. The Co-operative Society has thus achieved its end and are long the Council will be troubled with questions on private slaughter houses over which the " Stores " were so humili- ated. There can be no doubt that Messrs. Norman, Wood and Douglas acted wisely in withdrawing for it unquestionably would have meant certain defeat. The "stores " interest is rather too strong for an individual to contest. The " Stores " candidate, it is stated, is already selec- ted for next veer, though this may be questioned. The turn affairs has taken this time will certainly =courage them to run candidates until a majority is obtained. When the goal is reached, working- men may reasonably expect, instead of paying rates, the Council to declare a dividend on the public business of the town. Truly what with flower gardens, poplar avenues, prospective baths and a Co-operative Town Council, Runcorn would be pro- gressive enough for the moss. ardent Socialist.</p>
        <p>Thera was little business for the School Board to discuss on Tuesday, The principal discussion arose out of ignorance of the standing orders which govern the Board's procedure. The schools, it appears,close on Thursday for their Easter holidays and in the opinion of the Board it was desirable that the teachers should be paid their salary prior to the " breaking up." But the meeting on Tues- day could not sign the cheques owing to the 25th being the last day of the quarter which would have made thirteen payments in the year. To get out of the difficulty it was proposed to call a special meeting for Wednesday when Mr Imison very properly pointed out that a four day's notice would be required, otherwise a most dangerous and, perhaps, illegal precedent would be established. By the time the notice had expired the teachers would, have started their holidays without their month's cash. It was, of course, apparent that the rule would nave been far more honoured in the breach than the observance.but then , a rule's a rule, and it is worse than criminal,every- one knows,/to violate a standing order. Suggestion after /suggestion was made. and when the members were on the verge of despair, the clerk discovered that a standirlg order allowed that an "extraordin- ary meeting" could be called the very next day. The members breathed more freely after this announcement and so the hard worked teachers will be able to act on .`ihakespeare's. injunction to put money in their;purse,when they retire for their well merited rest.</p>
        <p>The Rural District Council are a very energetic body of gentlemen. Not content with an attempt to stamp out small-pox by gagging the anti-vac- cinator they have determined for the nett two years to make chicken-pox a notifiable disease. This, it is thought, must lead to the recovery of any surreptitious cases of small-pox. The Council evidently are imbued wi'..ll the same spirit which dictated Wentworth's famous po!icy of "thorough."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>FOoTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOoTBALL.</p>
        <p>Very few of Oldham's supporters journeyed to witness the second round of the Northern Union Cup Tie. Whether this was due to sheer indiffer- ence or to a presentiment that the Watersheddiogs team were on the down grade and that to follow t!)e fortunes of a luckless contingent was a waste of time is impossible to say. Still there was a good gate and expectation ran high that a brilliant exposition of the Northern Union code would be witnessed. These anticipations were not, however realised. Both teams were at their full strengt.h. Houghton turning out at the last minute but the game was woefully disappointing. The sun was shining brilliantly when the skipper of the myrtle greens led the way followed by the redoubtable Sammy Lees and his merry men.</p>
        <p>The early stages opened out finely. A well-sustained attack by the Oldham pushers forced Runcorn to defend, and had the slight advantage been; utilised, there is no doubt that Lees or Spottiswoode would have eventually realised his ambition. The fates, however, ruled otherwise. The attack, though well judged. was poorly sustained and by short stages, during which the übiluitous Jolley and the stone-wall:lleughton were here, there, and everywhere, the defenders became the aggressors in turn. And from that point the hopes of the visiting contingent were dashed to the ground. To be sure once or twice afterwards they made incursions into the home territory, but their stay was of a very brief dura- tion, and their attacking power seemed to have expended itself in the first brilliant burst Their defensive qualities, too, were of a very poor order. Had it not been for their 1 custodian, who over and over again displayed I the most meritorious judgment in stopping the dangerous rushes of the Runcorn front rank the score must have been much heavier. So determined were the wearers of the green to carry off the palm of victory that in a burst of enthusiastic play they early registered ten points within as m.ny minutes and then settledldown to toy with their opponents. The first half contained the only brilliant features of the match. Again and again the visiting rear guard tried their famous old time tactics and tried to equalise. But the living cordon of Runcorn's defence rose easily superior to the strain and the proverbial weak link was for once a missing quantity.</p>
        <p>At the change of ends Runcorn's superiority was even more marked than before. The leather was continuously kept within the Oldham quarter and had the advantage been pressed home further dis- aster for the visitors must have been witnessed. The Lancashire forwards were outclassed in both weight and taztics,eome of the dribbles of the home front rauk being beautifully dangerous. The honours of the fray rest with Jolley. Houghton played safely but with little brilliance. Of the three- quarters it were invidious to make distinctions, for each played a sterling game. Richardson played with considerable dash, but nevertheless made two or three mistakes which were inexcassblp, Walker. Reece and Woods being the pick of the forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCOriN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCOriN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting ol the alapve Board met on Tuesday afternoon in the Boardroom, Mr J frit3har4 presiding. There were also prectot Messrs E Janson, 8 Taylor, F J Norman, R H Posnett, W Handley, the Rev Canon Wood together with Mews J W Lightburn (clerk) and A Lightburn (assietant).</p>
