      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>APPOINTMENTS OF TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS OF TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Managemstt COmmittee held on the 18:h February accounts amounting to 12324 16s 2cl wee° passed for payrnerst.—lt was recommended I that the resignation of Miss E Hough assistant mistress at cho Victoria-road Board Schael be accepted. It was further recommended that lkliss Sarah Ellen Britland be appointed in the vacant position at a salary of £45 ner &amp;arum to date from the 'No April ; that Miss flilfa Newhall be appoint d an assistant teacher at a eatery of £4O per annum t 3 data from the let April •, that the Easter vacation shall date from the 27th March to the 7thiApril ; that Miss Harriett Stubbe be appointed assistant tetcher at Granville-rt-eet schools at a salary of £55 per annum ; that Mrs Ambles be requested to report on thes work of the pupil teachers central class mince its inception to cover toe scholarship examination Christmas 1901 and the pupil teachers examination 1902 ; that the following regulations be passed and the geode obtained. Victoria-road girls' school stationary, £4 19s 9d ; ee.wing materials, £2l Is 61. Mr Handles', in moving tee confirmatien of the minutes, said they would have to call a special meeting I for the:purp3se of signing the cheques. They could nos sign them at that meeting or else they world have thirteen months in the year. Miss Hough had resigned I her position to take ap an appointment at Winsforcl,her native place. He hoped her services would be as sue- eessful there as they had been here. Miss Nevshall's appointment was a temporary appeiatment because it was understood that they would very likely have to make a re-arrangement within two or three menthe. Miss Stubbs had bean recommended to Granville-street school. Blase wae a certificated teacher and the appoint- ment was necessitated by the new departure they intended to teke at midsummer with respect to the increasiug attendance at Grauville-street school. Thee had to utilise the upper and lower portion of the build- ing and the education department ccnapelled them to I make the necessary alterations.</p>
        <p>Mr Taylor filicluaed avid the motion was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RECORD XIONTHLY ATTENDANCE.</head>
        <p>A RECORD XIONTHLY ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the average attendance for the past month was the best ou record. The numbers wore 3,149 and exceeded all previous records in the Board's history even when they had a population of 4000 more. This concluded the business. H. M ASHTON'S BUB TONIC Is nut KING OF TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET Warrington.—(Advt. PUBLIC 131INBFIT UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LAW SHlRE.—Acist</p>
        <p>" PREMIER " &amp; r EXCELSIOR" CYCLES at 10 guineas, Free Wheel, 2 Rim brakes,Q.marm, Bridge-et</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>HALTON PARISH COIINCIL.</p>
        <p>The above authority met at the village hall Mr W Morris presiding A precept for £27 was issued on the overseers and accounts amounting to £3l 2s were paesee for payment. The clerk reported that the expenses of the council were a fraction over lid in the .Z. It was resolved that suitable provision be made for the storage at the hall of the property of friendly societies and that the question of the establishment of a library and the celebra- tion of the King's coronation be deferred. ACCIDENT AT THE NATIONAL Scuoors What might have proved a very serioes accident occurred at the National Schools on Tuesday after- noon. A number of young girls were playing up end down a large light of stone steps when a girl named Edith Bazley, aged twelve, residing in Shaw-street, accidentally fell from the tor to the bottom. On being picked up it was found that beyond a severe shaking and a badly ut eye the girl had had a miraculous escape from breaking some of her bones. The head mistress, Miss Sewell, took the girl to Mr Wes.ton's where the wound was dressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN NOVELIST.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN NOVELIST.</p>
        <p>An interesting sketch of Mr Hall Caine appears in the current number of the "Christian Globe." The career of the famous novelist is very graphic- ally depicted and the writer shows that Thomas Henry Hall Caine was born at Runcorn on the 14th May, 1853,the on of a ship's smith. By dint of hard work he managed to attach himself to the staff of the Liverpool Mercury and afterwards developed into a powerful writer, publishing his first book at the ape of thirty-"..w0. Since then he has issued his world-famed novels, and at present resides in the Isle of Man.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL ELECTION. The withdrawals of the nominations for the elec- tion of Councillors for the Runcorn Urban Council took place on Tuesday when the following gentle- men withdrew :—Mr Douglas from Bridgewater Ward ; Messrs Wood and Norman, Mersey Ward; Mr Shaw, Bridgewater Ward.—This leaves the con- stitution of the [six Wards as follows I—Messrs Jones and Shaw, Mersey Ward ; Mr Walton, New town ; Mr Handley, Bridgewater; Mr (Utley, Central; Mr Pritchard, Heath ; Mr Wiswall, Halton. —lt will be seen that there will be no contest in any of the wards and that each nominee has gat a walk over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUN CIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday. Mr W Knowles J.P., presided, and there were present :—Messrs S Woodward (vice-ehair- man),Mclotyri,,J Stok.s.T Wright, Chadwick,Roylacco, J Spruce, E Clarke, G Ptiling,T Lewis, J Edwards, P Gleave, J Whitlow, T Darlington, J Moseley-rrilliams, Mawdsley, J Bolshaw, A Eaton, R Billington, with Messrs G F Ashton (clerk), Dr Adams (Medical Officer), W Diggle (surveyor), J Farrington (sanitary inspector).</p>
        <p>_ . FRODSIIAM F OTPATEr AND TAB COUNTY COUNCIL. Mr Reginald Potts, clerk to the County Counod wrote under date March 15th, with reference to the elevated roadway at Prodsham as follows —ln reply to yours of yesterday' date, it is no doubt true that this souncil's workmen have done some slight repairs to the above. but the same bays been carried out inadvertently. My contention is that this roadway forms no part of the main road which vests in the County Ginned so as ta place apon them the obligation to maintain and repair and that the same is an ordinary highway for which your Council are liable to maintain and repair. The Clerk said he had again written to the County Connell pointing out that MO road was a continuation of a footpath which they now kept in repair. He had not yet received any reply. Mr Moseley Williams aaid that the footpath had always been repaired by the County Council and never by the District Council.</p>
        <p>HATTON SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said they would mnember that by the agreement for the construction oil the mains it was Ipftlwith Mr Smith Burgess to certify as t.) the amount of toe damage and the Council would pay it. Mr T Smith Burgess, in big letter. estimated the damage dose on the farm in the occupation of the executors of the Ism Tobn Black.; haw by the execution of work in con- nection with Hatton Sewage at £4 6s 6J. The offer was considered reasonahle and the aecount was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SMALL POE HOSPITAL AT MOORE.</head>
        <p>THE SMALL POE HOSPITAL AT MOORE.</p>
        <p>--- --- Messrs Dunlop Wallis and Co., Of Manchester, writing on behalf of the Manchester Ship Canal Company in reply to the clerk's letter of the 15th inst with regard to ' the Small Pox Hospital, at Moore, stated that the rent which the Council now paid for toe hospital was a nominal one arranged in 1894 for a period of 12 months. Since that date i had bees renewed by the Rural Coun- cil from year to year, and at the time the rent was arranged it was never intended that it should remain so long at a nominal sum but for the understanding that the tenancy was a temporary one on Vl° Rural authori- ty's parr. As the Council now seemed to contemplate becoming permanent tenants they thought the rent should be placed on a proper toil. They would be prepared to grant the Council a yearly tenancy of the building at the rent of £2O per annum and on hearing from them would send form of agreement for signature They did not think the Canal Company could grant a lease but the yearly tenanev would not be disturbed except tor some unforseen catiee. In reply te a furtlser letter they wrote explainieg that the area occupied by tho above tiuildings was about 365 square yards. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Alter a little diETcussion it was resolved thst Dr Arising and Messrs Mo3eley Williams and G F Ashton try to ob- tain a personal interview with the Ship Canal authority</p>
        <p>NOTICE OF AUDIT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk produced notice of audit of the Council's accounts to commence on the 2Lh April at the Work- house.</p>
        <p>TUE STRETTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr James lie—aa,-;;;;/a-ed sanitary engineer, War- rington. wrote with reference to the Stretton Water sopply, estimating that the cost of carrying a main to opposite the Vicarage: at Stretton would be £730, and the cost of a branch off to the centre of Lower Stretton £260. The Clerk said the question was whether it would pay to have the water carried. They would have to guaran- tee eight per cent. It was resolved that th surveyor get the number of houses on the road and submit them to the clerk so that he could get an estimate of the cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHICEEN•PDX A NOTIFIABLE DISEAPE</head>
        <p>CHICEEN•PDX A NOTIFIABLE DISEAPE</p>
        <p>That the Infectious Diseases Notification Act, 1889. shall for a period to be:fixed, apply, to chicken-pox within the district of the Council. -</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that this resolution was made at the request of the medical officer. The Medical Officer stated that the reason why he wanted the disease to be a notifiable one was that they might not have any undiscovered cases of small-pox. It was only intended to be a temporary measure. It need not be permanently added to the schedule. The only objections to permanently putting it on the notification list was the expense and the non-existence of small-pox under ordinary conditions. It was resolved on the prsposition of Mr Woodward that chicken-pox be made a notifiable disease for a period of two years.</p>
        <p>SPRING JACIERTS. New designs and shapes. latest Novelties, Special purchase cf Moreen Underskirts. New Spring goods, splendid value, see windows. W T WILLIAMS Sankey-street.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ALLEGED CHILD MURDER AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED CHILD MURDER AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, at the Police Court, Mr Brighouse held an inquest on the body of James Whelan, aged eight months, who was found dead in a dolly tub on Thurs. day,and in connection with which his rnother,Catherine Whelan, is in custody on a charge of having caused his death. Superintendent; K -ighley was present on behalf ofothe polfce,and Mr Knowles represented prisoner. alMoses Whelan said he was a colo:r,and resided at 58, William•street. He identified the body as that of his son. He last saw the child alive on Thursday morning between seven and half-pas. A haff-yast ten the same ' der he saw the child dead. In answer to Mr Kno wges witness said he hal been married to prisoner seven years before the child was born. His wife had complained of being unwell and pains in her head. On Monday night she left word that she was not coming back that night. She returned on Wednesday morning, and they had a few words, and ha hit her. Oa Thursday rimming they had more words, and he hit her again. Winifred Caulfield, a little girl, said on Monday after- n.on Mrs Whelan asked her to go for some salts of, letnoe• Witness told her mother, and in coa,equence of what she said witness went and told Milt Whelan that they would uot serve her. On Thursday, about half- past ten witaess went for some steak for prisoner. The baby was then in the mills. When she c ime back at a quarter to elf,ven the cradle W 99 empty. Witn, es west on another message, and when she landed back at 11 15 the cradle was stilt empty, and prisoner Lad gone. In answer to Mr Knowles, witness said the woman had told her that everyone was against her. Emma Roberfs, a nev-door neighbour, said at eleven o'clock on Thurs lay Mrs Wnelan ran into her house and said she had drowned the baby in a dolly tub. She further said she was going down to the police station. Witness had noticed jthat the woman was strange in manner.</p>
        <p>Police-constab!e Rutter deposed to going to the house and finning the chiid dead in a doily tub, over which WU a large mug. Th,ra wero tea incties of water in tho.tub. When charged the woman Yes, I did do</p>
        <p>Inspeck or Sqlckland aim gave evidence,. Dr autchinapn d-pqsed that he had male a pt-nor- tern examination. The cause of death Wbi drowning. The jury returned a vardint of " Wilful murder " against the mother, and the e)raner eparrit:ed her to take her trial at th,i assins.</p>
        <p>ANOrEER REMAND. Oa Monday morning at the kVidnes Patine Court, before the Mayor (kderman W W Gassage) and too ex-Slavcr (Alderman G I N-2il), Catherine Woclan was azain charged on remand with causing the deatia of her child.</p>
        <p>uperinten dent Keighley having stated the facts of the case said he would ask the g! ieh, after hearing the evidence of C:initable Rutter, to remand the prisoner until Monday next. . . . .</p>
        <p>Police-Constable Rutter repeated the evidence he gave at the inquest, reported fully above. Mr J W Knowles, wile again represented the accused, said he had no objection to the remand, te. next Mon- day being Bank Holiday, the case would then be form- ally remanded again until Tuesday, April Ist. The remand was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN BOATING CLUB. Annual Meeting and Dinner.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOATING CLUB. Annual Meeting and Dinner.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner of the above club was held at Wilson's Hotel on Monday evening. Tnere was rather a meagre attendance of members, as there would only be about 3) covers laid. Toe serving and quality of the fare provided by Mr Wildgoose was excellent. During the evening the company was most ably entertained by Messrs Laudenberg and J Taylor, of Widnes, Mr Bankes, Mr Water- worth and also by a very amusing item not on the programme, i.e Profes:ioe Miller, with his " Old Lady "—the latter very ingeniously being composed of an umbrella, au handkerchiet, and a crayon sketch on the back of the hand. The usual toasts of " Bing and Queen" "I-lost and Hostess" were enthusiasically honoured. Toe toast "Runcorn Boating Club" was proposed by Mr A Abel,and replied to by Mr H Tatters. During his;remarks Mr Abel referred to 1894 when the club stated with two boats and 15 members, and said it was a matter for congratulation to the officials of the club that to-day saw them with a packed boat house ;•16 boats, and 90 rnembars.</p>
        <p>, On Tuesday 'evening at 8 o'clock in the Branner Guild Hall, the 801 annual general meeting was held. The attendance was excellent and tally representative, Mr Bennion was in the chair, and his opening re- marks touched upon the laxity exhibited by the members in responding to entertainment organised by the committee at the request of, and tor ttie benefit of the members. Trey had .1J members at dinner out co. 90 on the books. SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The first business was the secretary's (Mr W A Millington's) report, which was as follows :—" It gives me great pleasure to be able to submit to you at this our Bth annual general meeting a report which 1 think you will agree indicates improve- ment and progress on previous yeses workings. Last year I submitted that we had elected during the previous season 39 new members to the privi- leges of the club, while this year 47 new members had been admitted. There have been withdrawals but numerically we are stronger than we have ever been. We have Several applications for member- ship for your approval to-night. While speaking of our members it Will be my duty, and a most painful duty, to mention in this ,1 I report two very serious calamities we have met with as ! I a dub during the past season. The first being the I very lamentable accident on the Weaver which resulted ! in a friend of one of our members losiog his life. I refer , to the late Mr S Rawlins of Widnes. fhe second the very sad death of one who was a well known figure on ,1 the Weaver, and who was most actively interested in 1 the welfare of ths Runcorn Bating Club, namely the late Mrs T Whinyates. lam sure we all deeply sym- pathised with Mrs Rawlins, and Mr Whinyates in their great bereavement. Mention should be made that we were represented at both interments, and that wreaths' were sent from the Club. With regard to the accident the outgoing committee had Isteoderl to provide facili- ties for our members to learn to swim, and this point should have the early attention lof the committee for 1902. Two new boats have been added to our fleet during the year, and tuese boats ap- pear to be greedy liked by our members. Most of you ! will be aware that a scratch crew was sent to Chester just prior to the Chester Regatta last year for a trial spin, with the view of enteriu;z for the " Eaton Plate." F Abel, J G Millington, S Prince. and myself composed the crew, and after going over the COWS° several times, and trying the boat well, we did not consider it wise to enter a race without mare carefully preparing a crew, and intended that next year a bid would be made. Per- serially nothing would give ma greater pleasure and satisfaction than to see a crew of ours competing at Caester, and if we could not win, I leel certain, it our best rowers will only go in for training, that we could do the town credit. Tue draw we held was satisfactory. We have pur- chased tools foe our caretaker at Rocksavage, and the consequent saving on light repairs will be a matter of economy. Our boat house now being completely full, the outgoing committee have drawn up plans and spec- fication for a new boat house, which they propose to build alongside the present one, of exactly the same Biz?. A large portion of the new house will of course be devoted to first class dressing room equipment. Mr Stokes has very kindly allowed us the use of the requi- site land. The regiv,ta and swimming gala held on August 10th was a decided success, especially consider- ing the unfavorable climatic conditions. The profit made was dote on £9, and had the day been fine it might easily have been anything between £l5 and .240. It is hoped by all of us that our gala will be one of the year's most important events in the district, acid there is no doubt it will prove a great attraction, just as there is no doubt that anything the R B C undertakes, if the members will only throw their energies intior it, cannot fail but be successful. In closing this report,l must put on record the entire satifaction given by our caretaker Mr Tnomas in keeping the boats and boat house in such excellent condition. . . .. ..</p>
        <p>Mr J Peacock proposed the adoption of the report, and at the same time:proposed a hearty vote of thanks for the services rendered by the 3.3cretary.—Mr J Beck seconded. Mr 'Peacock enquired if Rule 5 re payment of subscrip- tions by cud of April had breu striocly enforced, and in reply was informed that 1901 being the first year of its existence it bad not been enforced, but that it would be rigidly adhered to during 1902. FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr H G Johnson. treasurer. then submitted a detailed repor tof the club's bnancia !position, and said hat the</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>CREAM</p>
        <p>C R A C:K!E RS</p>
        <p>CRISP AND</p>
        <p>CREAMY</p>
        <p>remark made by the Secretary regarding 1901 being the best year, applied to his accounts. At every point there was improvement, and tie undertakiogs of the season had all been financially successful. The draw added to the club's foods masa en £7, while the gala brought a further a 10. The total turnowr was about £3O more than last year, and alter outlay on boats repairs, tools, accessories, salary for caretaker, he had to carry over £l5 to n2xt year's accounts, It must not be thought however, thst having at) much in band denoted we were well off, the case was the opposite, as 1902 would be a year of heavy expenditure owing to the quite necessary erection of a large boat house, and new coats to meet urgent requirements, so that the incoming Committee wil have to work hard and well to present _a clear balance sheet next year.</p>
        <p>Mr 'l' G Moore moved t ad ip.t.o of the report, and also the best thanks to Mr Joho3-,n, for his work on the Club's behalf. This was seconaed by Mr Green, and carried.</p>
        <p>That purchase of a racing four be left in the hands of the committee, was the proposition of 'Mr A Palma, seconded by Mr W Thomas.</p>
        <p>Mr Millin.gt ni proposed, 8114 Mr Harvey seconded that a n-w rule should be inserted in the hook of rules to the effect that " No member be admitted to tue club under 16 yeara at age," Carried. It was a!so necideci to make a rule to the effect that " any member wishing to become a tile-member could do so on payment of £3 31." Propoaed by Mr a G Johnson, and seconded by Mr Harvey. Carried. Mr &amp;onion suggested that rule 17 be altered to resd that the hour of meeting on Saturdays be 245 p m iristead of 2 30. After discussion it was decide(' that it would suit the bulk of the membera to leave it at 2 30. Oa the introduction of Mr Harvey, it was referred to the committee to make enq Anti; a to the cos', of insur- ing the members against accident and death on the Weaver.uranc t n early nal it the premium allows it to effect such i date. The members passed votes of thank° to the officials and committee, for the satisfactory mann-r in whicn they had conducted the club's business during the past year. _ _ .</p>
        <p>- The election of officers resulted as follows: —President, Sir R M Brooke, Bart. ; vice-presidents, Sir J T 13ranner Bar, M.P., C N Irven, Mrs liaztehuret, J a Biker, J F HaveLurst, R H Posnett,J.t P Taylor, J.P., FE Speak- man, J.P.,Messrs Castner Kellner and Company Limited. T N Smith, and Colonel Sall Walker ; secretary, Mr W A Mirlinger ; treasurer, Mr Li G Johnson ; captain, Ur F Abel ; sub-oaotain, Mr J W Wiswall ;com- naittee,Mersrs B Bsunion,W Harvey,T G Mocre,J Beck,J Peacock, H P Johnson, J G Millington. and R Johnsm ; auditors, Mf'SSE-3 J U Mnugton, and R Bennion. A vote'of thanks to the Otisirruan terminated the pro- ceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.-- Before Messrs Long On the chair). H Nield, Malin Bdelsten, and Colonel Ridgway. DRUNK AND REFtteINIV TO QUIT —Bustace Bennett, Stockton Heath, was summoned for be.ng drunk on the premises of Richard Newell, '• Mulberry 'free " Inn,Stocktort Meth, end refusing to quit when reel oested to do so.— Bvidence was given to the effect that dcendant went into the publie-house torilrink and wee found to be in a drunken condition. He asked for drink and the landlord's sen refused to serve him and requested him to leare.—Defendant . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>refused to go out and bad Co be ejectel.--it fine of 3s 6.1 waS inflicted.</p>
        <p>13nBo1YIES,—I1 ward Berry, a lad, charged with driving a horse and float furiously at Stockton Heath, on the 3rd March, was hued 2s 61.—Peter Simpson, an old man WAS charged with baing at such a distance from his horse and float—isamelv, ti; yards—at Stockton Reath, on the 7th March, as to have no control of the animal. and Was bed 7,6 d—ark Mounfield, riding on ki4 bicycle at 81) p m. on the 2nd March, at Whitley, without a lighted lamp, was ordered to pay the casts, 3164.—rhoin as Taylor, allow- ing ado to lor at large unmuzzled and without a collar, on the 12th Februa:y, at Stockto Heath, was orlereki to pay bei Sri—Walter J Ford, charged with a similar offence in respect of two dogs also at Stockton Heath, was ordered to pay 38 65 tor each dog —Marie Hower was similarly charged with respect to four dogs at Grappenhall, and was hued las 61— For ntakin, use ot obscene and piofane language at Grappenhaa. on the lath Karen, George Ainsworth was fined 3s 6d.</p>
        <p>STEALING A HEN —William Whitehouse, an elderly man, was charged with stealing a hen, of the value oi to G i the property ot Mr James Moore. Fir Grove, Latonford Without. —Prisoner plaid() , not guilty. and intormed the Bench that he' picked the hen off the ,ottd."—P.C. fiennett of War- rington, said he met the prisoner at 3,45 am in Knutstord- road, on the 14th Mama. Prisoner hod something bulky under his arm He went up to the wan and found that he was in possession of a h-n, which had been recently killed.— P.O Tarte said he made inquiries at Latchford, an I found that a hea was missing from Fir Grove. He appr.hended the prisoner and charged him vvi,,h the roboery. His renly was : •• I Sound it, on the road-side. It was dead."—The Bench Inflicted a kiss o, ISs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Frocisham Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Frocisham Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDA T.—Before Messrs C Reynolds (in the chair), A. Thomas, G Crossland Taylor and J Ocklestoa. ILL-TIINA clan a. OAT.—James Summerfield, Frodsham, and Robert Fry, Frodsham. were summoned for ill-tr,ating a cat at Frodsbam on the 2ad inst., the property of William liobinsma.—e.o Fry gave evidence to the effect that the d&amp;endants made a statement respe2ting Emoting at a cat with a catapult and hitting the ani nal with a stick— John Bate, v. terinary surgeoo, Sutton Weaver, said that he had examined the eat and found wounds on thel cat's Ihead,: which could only have been made by a blow. He should say that the cat h.d been stunned first and Ili-used afterwards. The wounds cauld not possibly have been caused by the cat dashing italfaainist the wire. Fined Bland costs or in default one month's hard labour. PITCH AND Toes.—Pour youths named David Whitby, Benjamin Turner. Arthur Ainsworth and Robert Ainsworth, were summoned for playing pitcti and toss in Bates Court on the 16th March at Frodshain.- P.C. Fry deposed that he had received many complaints about gaming and on watching this spot found the defendants gaming. The two Ainsworths led the othe-s into it and were noted gamesters. —The brothers Ainsworth were fiaed lOs including casts and. the other defendants were let off with paymeut of the costs 148 6d.</p>
        <p>CONTRivENING THE SWINE ACT —George Rose. Kingsley, was summoned for removing a Dig without the liecessary certifintte.—P.C. Pry stated that an the 3rd hiarzh he saw a pig being driven along the Northwien-read. He asked the driver whether a swine removal order had been obtained and he replied one h 44 not. Witness went te the owner of the pig and he said he did not know that an order was r, eyes- sary.—Fined 10s including costs or in efault seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
