      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>DECLINE OF ENGLISH AGRIC4JLTURE.</head>
        <p>THE DECLINE OF ENGLISH AGRIC4JLTURE.</p>
        <p>"It in of vital import ,ace tbat we should realise the fact that the last quarter of the nineteenth century hav exhibited a practically unbroken record of decline in this countrs' agriculture. NAwitiistanclng that almost at the beginning of this quarter century a Kcal Cm- i?siep sat upon tile then exis'ing depression in agri- culturo, the position since his seeadily worsened, and shows irit the slightest sign of mending. If the com- plaints of distvaq do not contiau.lly augment, it is for the oinnious,baleful reason that the farmers and tlrir la- bonrers, in 5 early inereasing;:numbers,have given udin despair and disgust the struggle to maintain the cou,-- try's chief industry—there is less and ever less farming now than formerly. This will he apparent if I detail the acreage figures over the period. And I will m tgate the number of the fi.,;:iires by tabulating them in quin- quennial averages. 11-re is the result. THE WHEAT ACREAGE OF THE UNITED KINGD</p>
        <p>AVn.rata A"Pra,,P 1871-1875 1876-1880 1881-1885 GREAT BRITAIN ONLY.</p>
        <p>1900 These wheat acreage figares are those te which your attention should chiefly be drawn ; for tha production 1, of breadstuff is the mast important department of I; agriculture, and it may be taken as typical of arable!, eurivation generally. But it may be well to demon- strate this last point. This will be done by reproduc- ing a similar acreage tail le to the above. but inchiding all kinds of corn crcps—that is to ray, in additon to wheat, barley. oats. rye, bears and peas. THE CORN CROPS ACREAGE OF THE UNITED KINGDOM. • AVeraPn. Averi2e. 1871-1875 1876-1880 1851-1885 BARLEY AND OATS ONLY. IN GREAT BRITAIN ONLY. 1900 Eiere the same talent continuous dectine may be read. The other corn crops have gone the way of wheat. Nor have these lost corn crops bean replaced by green crop., I will not worry you again with tabulated statements ; let it suffice to compare the average at tb. beginning of the quarter-century with ihe average at the end. The green crop; of the United Kingdom, which comprise potatoes, turnips and swedes, reang,-Ids, cabbage, vetches, etc., were from 1871 to 1875 planted over an annual average of 5,073.843 acres. nom 1896 to 1900 they were planted over an average of 4,318,733 aura. So it is with flax, the aver4D acreage 1 which from 1871 to 1875 was 136.005 acres ; from 1898 to noo it was only 47,974 aert s Even hops have declined in the same period from 64,044 acres to 51,600 acres. Thrre is a proverb Down cum, up horn ' ; but the latter his- tory of England poorly ex:mplifies that proverb. Not- withstanding the land which toe diminished arable cultivation set free for stock and sheep raising, the bead of rattle held in the Kingdom has only increased from 9,932,443 to 11,178.976 ; while the number of sheep pastured in the Units.] Kingdom has p-iptively declined duri,g this period, the figures being in the fir.t years 33,192,418, and in the last years 31,051,718. We have failed even to increase the number ef pigs, which have declined from 3,782,134 to 3,663,716. Now thin out the meaning of these fi2UreS in the light of the growth during the period of the p-pulation, which has increased from 31i millions in 1871 to 41i millions in 1901, and which would have increased, it may be remarked, yet more, but for the exteusiye emigration from rural Ireland, and for the substantial emigration from rursl England and Scotland. caused by the decline in agri- cultur,. Consider them in the light of our increased imports of foodstuff 9 In 1875 we imnorted 51.876,517 cwt of wheat ; in 1900, 68,669,490 cwt.; in 1875 we import.d 6,136.028 cwt. of wheat flour ; in 1900, 21,548,131 cwr. ; in 1875 we imported 11,049,476 cwt. of barley. ; in 1900,17,054,990 cwt ; in 1875 we imported 2.955,202 cwt of meat, bacon and hams ; in 1900, 17,911,738 cwt. This is the record for the queen of industries in this country during the past quarter of a century."—From an ;mailing article on " Our National by Ernest E Williams, the trenchant author of "Marching Backwards ;'in the April Wriuson MAGAZINE.</p>
        <p>The " Peep o' Day Thy," a very readable novel by Robert Buchanan has been published at sixpence by Mr John Dicks, and will be found a good investment for those readers who are fond of fiction,</p>
