      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>MINOR' UASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRIDSY.-Before the Deputy-Mayor(in the chairhCouncillora dmethurst and J Boberts and Mr Thornton. NEIGHBoUMS AT VARIANCE.-Ulargaret Miller, 71 Bostock- street, was summoned for,aseaulting Mary Jane Mitchell, 75 Bostock-street. oe the 14th March.-31r Unsworth appeared for the complainant and said the complainant and the defendant had a quarrel about some trivial matter, but at the time it came to nothing more thau words. Two hours later, however, the defendant came to complainant's house and kno3ked at the door. She told Sarah Thomas, the girl who opened the door, that she wanted to speak to Mrs Mitchell. Ca 'complainant going to the door defendant seized her lea the hair, pulled her out ot the house.into the gutter, knocked her , down and kicked her. -Defendant said she was much pro- voked by the complainant's conduct and went to the house for an explanation. Comolainaat refused to give one and she (defendant) thereupon pulled her out of the house. She denied kicking leer.- she magistrates tbought.that consider- ing the provocetion the defendant had received. the justlee of the case would be met by the infliction en a fine of bs in- cluding costs, NOT THE WEIGHT BEPRESENTED.-A WARNING.-Frank Armstrong, if,t, Leonard-street, was summoned for exposing sacks of coat for sale which were of less weight than they 1 wele represented to be.-Inspector Wallington said that on the 15th inn he was in Folly-lane amd saw the defendant in cntrge of a harry which contained fifteen or sixteen bags of ccal. He requested defeadaiat to weigh a bag, and on this being done it was found that the weight was from twelve to , fifteen pounds short of what it Was represented to be. Seven other bags were weighed and were found to be on the average ascot tour pounds underweighieDefeadant said the begs were weighed correctly at the yard and he did not know how they Came to be light. The Bench impased a fine of 40a and costs, the Chairman remareing that if any more cases came betore them they would be very sever-ly dealt With. BREACH OF THE FACTORY REGULATIONS. -Messrs Guest Brothers, Winwick-street, were summoned for having em- ployed Frank Appleton, William id 3ward, Thomas Forrest and James Sixsmith, boys nude, sixteen years of age,without obtaining a certifiitate of physical fitness within seven days of their commencina work. Inspector EaLOCk said Howard had been emp'oyed 13 mouths Appleton is months,Sixsmith 7 mouth and Forrest 7 weeks, and tleir names had not even hose entered in the register.-The defence was that owing to pressure o' work the matter had been overlootted.-.a. fine of 5; and cots in the first case and la and costs in each of the other cases was inflicted. as IFR ASSLULTS.-Mlchael Craighae, 21 Bank-street, was summoned for assaulting his wife Mary on March 13th.- Complainant said she Was in bed early in the morning of the dace named and defendant dr.gged her out by the hair of the head and kicaed her.-Mr Browne appeared for the defendant who denied the assault and alleged drunkenness on the pert o.ihis wife he,Bench considered teat there:were faults on eace side and bound Loth parties o. or to keep the peace.-4raticis Burke, 11, riutton-street, was fined les and costs for assaulting his wife Mary Jane Burke on March 2 th.-The evieenee was to the effect that defendatit so his wife that she was obliged to run out of the house and stay out all night, SATURDAY,-Before Messrs fireball (in the chair), and R T Fairclouah. ARREARsI OF VAINTENANCE. -Julia Hayes was brought up I on a warrant for arreers of maintenance due to hie wife, amounting with the costs to £2 16s 61, and was ordered to pay the money immediately or go o goal for a month. MONDAY.-Before Mr fireball (in the chair), Alderman Suttem, Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs Hayward and Thornton ASSAULT ON A. POLICE CONSTABL.-A HOSTILE CROWD.- Thomas Ellison. hawker, 3, Pierce's Court was summoned for assaulting P.C. Heovict whilst acting in the execu- tion of his duty on March 13th.-- The officer stated that on he date named he had occasion to arrest two men named James Carey end William Smith for fighting in Winwice-ioad. On the way to the Filice Station he was attacked and and violently assaulted by Carey, who received three months' imprisonment, and was also obstructed by a hostile crowd. Whilst he was tussling with Carey the man }Snippy' game up and got hold of Carey, seying to witaess, o Let this man go.- Witness told him he should not and asked him to mini his own buainerea Ellison then Rot hold of him by the rignt aura and the throat and said " If you doe't let him go I'll tbreetle you." He then pushed witness backwards among the crowd. Jillisou escaped and he did not see him again until last Monday when he visifed his house and charged him with obstruction and aeaault, Defendant admitted getting hold of Cleey but denied the assault-William Smith, ironworker. 5, Erwodl-street,who was one of the prisoners in the custody of the Officer, was celled and gave corroborative evidence. He said that he (witness) went quietly to the Police Station -The Chief. Constable said prisoner had been before the court nine times-The Chail man said that obstructing the police was getting a very serious matter and defendant would be flame 2.3 s and costs with the option of a month's imprisa .anent.-William Smith was subsequently charged with cornrnit;ing a breach of the peace by fighting In Win- wick-roast on Meech 13th-The Chief Constable stated that witness received no promise of feel, favour before he gave avid are lathe last case and he had behaved so well that he (Mr Tenant) asked the magistrates' permission to withdraw the case-The Bench expressed their approval of this course and Smith was dis Charged. lgIMMon 01 SRS.-Briv,Ard Broderick, 13. Oliver-street, was tined as tor keeping a dog without is licence.-Sarall Ann Mathe,•, Frlai's-gr,en, was lined Is for allowing a chimney to be on fire.-Ellijah Cunnington, Longford-street, Wf.3 summoned to show cause why an order should not be made upon him to atace a nuisance arising from detective slop- siosse pipes at 5 and 7 Crossley-street.-Inapactor Flood having given evidence, the magistrate's made an order for the abatement of the nuisance within seven days, defendant to pay the c :stet of the esse. TWENTY-FIRST APPE &amp;RANCE .-Bliza Williams, 6, Winwick- street, who made her 21st appearance was. on the evidence of P.C. Clough, sent to gaol for a month for being drunk and disordsrly In Moult-street on Saturday evening.</p>
