      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MottneY.—Before Mesar4 113 Imison (in the chair) and J. Pritchard.</p>
        <p>AN AMICABLS. SETTLEMEsT.—The adjourned case in which Ann Evans and Alice Lythgoe were sued by the letter's husband to return a houseful of furniture abstracted daring his absence was announced to have been amicably settled• A DISGUSTING H•BIT.—For using profane language, Moses White. Loch-str-et, was fined /Os inclu ling costs or in default? days.—For a similar olferice Susannah Peecocke was fined ts and ccrils or 7 days • ssmily Green. Loch-street. Sc and costs or 7 days —P.O. dcholls proved the oases. AFTER THE FoOTBALL MATCH—William Carlyle Market, street. Warrington and Thomas Lister, Wash-lane Islchford, were summoned for using procene language in Church-street ou Wedneeday.—P.C. Wilding stated that he was on duty when he heard the two defendants and another man woo had since been convicted, singing and shouting. Sergeant Gregory told witness to ask them to stop making a noise. Bre did so when they commenced to swear violently at him.—Sergeant Gregory 3orroborated.—Defend- ant s pleaded not guilty. Their characters were too good for them to do anything like the offence alleged. They had been to the football match and were making their way to the traits. They certainly were not drunk,—Fined 55 and costs or 7 daysjn default. AN OLD OFFLNDER.—Maria Evans. married woman. aeainst whom there was a large list of eonvictions, was fined Sc aed costs or 7 days for betug drunk and disorderly in I Priecesa-street.—Defendant : I'll go for 7 days then I l3e.vaue ASSAULT ON A PUBLICAN BY FATHER AND box Thomas Hadley and James Eadley, father and son, were summoned for having assaulted James Bradlee on the 17th Mareh.—Prosecuter stated that the two defendants, together with a nurnter of women came to his house. " The Five Crowns "in Cooper-street and ordered five glasses of beer. His wife took the beer and asked for payment which they refused, on the grounds that they had no money. Witness then ordered the defendants out of the place. when the younger man struekhine without provocation a violent blow in the eye,while the elder scratched his lace and madilit/bleed Louisa Bradley and Mary Jane Stubbs corroborated the asiault while Sergeant Gill deposed that the landlord was quite sober and that his face was bleeding when he com- plained G.) him of the assault shortly afeerweids.—Names Radley (the younger defendant): If he had not struck me I would not have struck hies. He was chunk et the time. I expect it was St Patrick's day with him. —Each defendant was fined 108 and lOs 6d costs ;or 14 clays in default. ASSAULTING A SINGLE Wonex.—A young man named Eerily Critchley, Egerton-street, was summoned at the instance of Lilian Burrows, a stylishly dressed single woman for an assault alleged to have been committed at 10.15 p.m. on the 21. st March at her residence.—Prosecutrix, cashier at the Runcorn Theatre, stated that the defendant came to her house at night and asSed for her father. She replied h 3 was inside and the defend ant then struck her In the mouth and went inside the passege pushing her In a chair.—He used very bad lan:nage towards her.— Hannah Wright, grandmother of the last witness, corroborated and staved that the defendant tad called her "an old cow."—P O. Bratt testified to the mouth of the prosecutrix being badly swollen on the night in question as though she had received a blow.— The detendant, who eleated to give evidence, declared heg was the victim of some ccn-piracy• He denied strikin anyone or using bad language.--Maria Ellen Rose, married woman. Bridgewater-street, swore that defendant stood at the door but a minute and never struck anyone. The only bad languege used was by Miss Burruws. Her house wag only a few yards kway and as she stood at the:door and heard it ail.—Detendant was fined £1 and 12s 6d costs or in default 14 days. DisoRDF.ELY CoNnUCT.—Maria Nesbitt. a Liverpool girl. was fined 7s or 7 days on the evidence at P U Bowyer ior disorderly cenduct in Percival-lane on Sunday night. Pot- cozier elected to go to gaol. ALLEGED DEsEETIoN FRoM THE AlutlY.—Tcyo young fellows uamed Robert Pritchard and William Holland, of Warrington, were coarged with deserting from the Kings Liverpool Regiment stationed at Orford Barracks, Warring- ton on Saturday,—Defeldants pleaded that they bad not deserted. They were simply out of bounds without leave-- gergeant Bennett stated that Ise was stationed at Sutton Weaver and thet he saw the two prisoners coming up the road. He asked where they were going and they replied to Chester—Witness then asked to see their passes but they answered they had none—They were then taken into custody Superintendent lannion said he had co nmunicateci with the authorities at Warrington and an escort would be sent down for them. He asked that they might be discharged. Prisoners whe were in a most frivolous and lively mood, were then formally discharged and handed over to the escort,</p>
        <p>Warrington Technical Institute.—Summer Term. Special arrangements have been made by the Technical Instruction Committee for giving tuition in the following Eubjects during the summer Still life, painting from life, wooCcarving, plant_ drawing, design and colouring (preparatory), ink- ing-in and colouring (machine and building coni, struction),shorttand,French, dressmaking. Furtrie particulerE can be obtained from the secretary(l)lr C. Madeley) at the Museum,</p>
