      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>i Precis of the Measure.</p>
        <p>So far as can be gathered from Mr Balfour'sl speech in the House ot Commons on Mondas even- ing, the new education bill proposes :- 1. That in counties, county boroughs, and, to some extent, in non-county boroughs of 10,030 in- habitants or more and urban districts of 20,000 or more the council in each case shall be the local authority for technical and secondary education. I 2. The council in any of thoBe areas must sub- mit to the Board of Education a scheme for the constitution of a committee through which the council will act for this purpose. Toe committee must consist of a majority of (elected) councillors, the remainder of the committee to be co-opted from among persons skilled in education not being mem- bers of the council.</p>
        <p>3. When the council chooses it may become the local authority for elementary education, acting through the same committee,and where this choice ' is made the council becomes the heir of the powers and duties of the school board, and must alio take over the whole responsibility for the secular instruc- tion in the Voluntary schools within the council area. Toe managers of a Voluntary school cannot refuse to come under the council's control, The option is for towns only. 4. To the council will be paid in a lump sum the Government grants now paid severally to the vari- ous schools, Board and Voluntary, within its area. The council will then consider the budget for each school, and out of the rates make up the sum necessary to keep or render the schools efficient. 5. The quia pro quo for thi.4 rate-aid to a Volun- tary school Is to be as follows :—the secular in- struction must be managed by the managers as the council directs. The committee of managers must consist as to one-third of persons appointed by the , council. Though the religious education in the school is to remain denominational the council are I to have the right of veto on the appointment or dismissal of teachers upon educational grounds. The council are also to have the right to inspect the school and to withhold all financial aid from it un- less satisfied. When a Voluntary school is un- necessary the council may close it as being super- fluous.</p>
        <p>6. Religious prejudices are to be respected in the following manner the parents of thirty or more children in an area wish a denominational scho3l for their children where one suitable does not exist they may provide the building for one and call upon the council to maintain it. When the parents at thirty or more .Nonconformist children, for int,tarice, demand a school that is not Church cf England or Rornfin Catholic the council must pro- vide and maintain an undenominational school. If the denominational sc.lool is de.4erted for the unde- nominational school the former may be closed.</p>
        <p>_ 7. Wales may chose between this sys-em and its present arrangements. S. The bill i. to come into force from the date of its pas,ing into law. 9. The bill does not apply to London. Scotland, or Ireland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FEARED HE HAD LOST IT.</head>
        <p>FEARED HE HAD LOST IT.</p>
        <p>Wiled Wu Ting Fang, the famous Chinese Minister to Washington, missed one day from tbe front ot bis cap the immense diamond he always wears there, he was dreadfully shocked and frightoned. An observing friend pointed on: thailthe statesman had inadvertently donned his turban wrong side before, and that the diamond was si,te in the rear. Profuse th,nks and profound relief. Oh, the difference between things, Had Wu Tin), Fang been wearing a BensoEec; Porous Plaster on his chest or beck to cure a cold, an ache or a strain, he never would hare doubted its Location for an instant-. Be would have felt it doing its work,—warming and making flexible the torpid muscles, extrae,ing (as we may say) the pain and soreness, promoting the free circulation of tt e blood through congested areas, stimulating the skin and tun.: tf to proper setion, and so dissaviivi and baeish- ing the malady tlatt troubled him. Taus we perceive beloved friends, that A BIG DIAMOND ON A HAT while a pretty thieg to look upoo, i. GE no practical use under the canopy. But Benson's Plasters are supremely useful. Thee relieve and cure gout, rheumatism, neuralgia, colds on tie' cheat, lame black, Ste., are quickly Red ccmpletely as ta make you wonder how it can be. Better now—well to moreew; that's the way they work: In the hour of pain and misery a Benson's Plaster on the bad spot is woith a dozen big diamonds tied round he rim of your sx-41.illings derby. Get the genuine, .enson's Plasters are slid by all chemistF. Warrington yH M Ashton, 63, Sankey-,treet ; Boots Limited, and 35, Bridge-street ; Hill and Co., Buttermarket- street ; H Miller, 77, Bridge-itreet ; and J It Young, 40 Sankey-street.</p>
