      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RED ROSE OF MARGARET.</head>
        <p>RED ROSE OF MARGARET.</p>
        <p>Red rose of Margar:3l, liere in my book you lie, Red as her lips and fragrant yet, Dead rose that can never die ; Margaret gathered one red rose, and another red rose I.</p>
        <p>" As long as any rose is red On this little rose-hunt tree, As long as willow leaves are green, Shall I be true to thee." So vowed I unt) Margaret and Margaret unto me. " As long as this little stream shall run Through the quiet willows here, Out of the shadow int ) the sun, Shall Margaret b 3 dear ; Yea, though tho stream should dry and the willows die, and the grass grow kere.</p>
        <p>" Yet should I not fo:get. Though the sun should fail, The holy face of Margaret, Pale as a pearl is pale ; And change shall change all else, but over this it shall not prevail.</p>
        <p>" And as true as I to Margaret To me shall Margaret be, And some day we shall stand again Thy that little rose-hwig tree, And ~ch, with a rose in tbn hand, shall say : was laithful unto thee.' "</p>
        <p>RICUARD LE GALLIENNE, IN Aram SMART SET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Warrington Field Club.</head>
        <p>Warrington Field Club.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles White presided at the annnal meet- ing of this club which was held in the Museum Lec- ture Room, and Mr J Smith, F.L.S., M.R.1.A., read a letter on "The Polyzoa." He endeavoured to indi- cate the relationships of this group of organisms, quoting some of the divergent theories which have been enunciated, and then proceeded to discuss the anatomy of some typical forms, illustrating the sub- ject with a series of beautiful diagrams. Votes of thanks were unanimously accorded to the President and _lecretary for their past ,services, and to the Museum Commitee for the use of the lecture room. —On the proposition of Councillor Greening, Mr F Womersley was elected president for the ensuing year, and Councillor A J Jolley was re-elected hon. secretary.—A short discussion of the arranizements for the summer programme followed. The club concludes the eighteenth year of its existence in a healthy and vigorous et.ate. During that period it has been instrumental in adding very etrtensively Ito collections in the Municipal Museum, and has played a not less useful part in stimulating scientific studies, and the intellectual life of the town and neighbourhood. Anyone interested in natural history and desirous of joining the Field Club can obtain information about it at the Museum or from the officers.</p>
