      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Interesting Experiences on the White</head>
        <p>Interesting Experiences on the White</p>
        <p>Niie.</p>
        <p>Renter's Agency has re-eel-Y;(1 some particulars of the journey up the White Nile recently undertaken by Sir Edmund and Lady Leohmere. The latter is the only Englishwoman that has visited Fashoda except Lady Baker, who accompanied her husband on his famous expedition to the Nile sources. Sir Edmund and L idy Lechmere left Khartoum in Nevember last for Feshoda, the Sobat, and Gondokoro in two large-sailing nuggere. and reached Fashoda early in January. The heat dur- ing the journey was intense, and there was a perfect piague of moiquitoee. Concerning the trip Sir Edmund Lechmere says : The banks of the White Nile for hundreda Of miles are fringed with dense masses of ?cods, and landing is almost impossible except at the very few disembarkation paces. The bats being low in the water it is impos- sible to see over the tops of these reeds, and on landing we have generally to push through half a mile of dense vegetation and swamp bef ere reaching dry land. In the wet season the country is a huge swamp. Fashoda; though an important past, is a dreary and deso- late collection of huts and tents reeking with malaria and mosquitoes. After remaining a few days we praceeded to the mouth of the &amp;bat, where I had a bed attack of fever, and we bad to abandon our idea toi pushing on to Gondokoro. We found the Sitiffiuks and Dinkas on the right bank of the Sobet friendly, but useless as (tamp attendants. The Nuers, on the oppesite hank, were very truculent, and anyone going among them mu,t be prepired for trouble. We had a special te go into t heir district, but It is not advisable for spartsmen to en' er the Nuer country at present. In tact, it is very unlikely that, except under special circumstances, the authorities will allow them to do so. South of Fashoda we found that the natives of bath sexes were quite destitate of clothing, Old "sparkles "are in great demand for necklaces, and even a more valuable as,et for purposes of barter than beads. Oa t‘e journey no to the Sobst we encountered all sorts of birds, chiefle waterfowl, in countless thousands; and for days we sailed through flecks of geese, pelicen, and the like, which scarcely troubled to paddle out of the way of our boats. Rippepetami and crocodiles are numerous and there are several species of antelope, in- cluding the Cebus Maria, of which I got a flee specimen. The fish in the White Niie run up to several hundred pounds in weignt, and 11(14,1 water Wei.; ar3 to be met with. I caught one weighing nearly 100113. During our jeurney we he I sh‘indant evidence of the benefit the eountty is deriving from British influence. ' The Landis beginning to be cultivated, and the natives, , freed from centuries of tyranny, are awaking from their ; apathy. The keynote of &amp;Wan enterprise and in- dustry is British rule, and were this relax the told regime of corrupt and incapable officials would be re- vived, and the c nentry becerne again a happy hunting- ground for the armed tax-gather and the slave-raider. , The great want of the Saudan is population.</p>
