        <p>gained ra-,. world. wide reputation— not .by erstravagazat or se.usztional advertioement, hut by the cvmsnt recommend.ation of those who use it, and of medical men who know its value.</p>
        <p>BENGER'S FOOD is sold in Tins by Chemists, Cac., everywhere.</p>
        <p>PROFESSIONALISM IN FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By J• J. Bentley. Oise of tee features which must appeal to athletes in the April number of the "Windsor Magazine" is an inter- view with Mr J J Bentley. the President of-the Football League, whose vie we on the subject of Professionalism in football will be reed with greet interest. " Not many people,' Field Mr Bentiv, • I fancy, connect the British Aeseciation with football ; yee it is a fact that an organization of that name once played a very im- portant past in the history of the game.' It c ime ahem this war. Profeseionelisra WAS not legalised till 1885 ; but for many years before that it was an open secret that tee players in nearly all the big clubs were receiving payment. The authorities were well aware of the fact, but took no active steps till in 1844 they disqualified Great Lever and Burnley for competing for the English Cup, on the ground that most of the playore of both these clubs were really professionals. As a matter of fact, veiled profeesionalism wee rampant in every Lenceshire Club at the time ; but Lencashire made a great show of virt- uous indignation and withdrew from the Football A iiccietion, and forrneVe combination of all the lead- ing clubs in the county, under the title of the British ! Association. Tee main obj ct was to demand from the Football Association that prefessienalisin should be legalised. Two Lancashire clube, however—Blackburn and Derwen—refus,d to joie the new organisation. Inasmuch as Darwen not nuly paid its players, but was actually the first English Club to import a Sthotsman,one James Love by name.its action was not altogether popu- lar,but it really didd't matter very much, as the British Association only lasted a single year. It was the British Association which really brought to a head the question of professionalism. Previous to this, comrni•sions had been appointed by the Foothill ksSociqtion, to nooks enquiries and examine the account books of the Sus- pected clubs. But complete evidence was most difficult to find, and when the books were promptly proluced by the secretaries they accounted for every penny in the most complete manner. 'Yet it was common knowledge that many, if not all, of the players belonging to these clubs were paid. The popular tale is that the men's wages were placed in their ordinary boots, where they found them when they dressed after the match ; but no such pains to ensure secrecy were taken. They were paid quite openly out of the gate money. The net sum remaining after these disbursements being entered in the books kept for inspection as the gross amounts. Others were found situations, or were established in tobacco and hosiery shone. Tee officials of the Lanca- shire Club simply laughed at the commissions. This . state of things, of course, could not continue, and in 1885 a meeting of the Football Association was held in London, to consider the whale question of professional- ism. • Roughly speaking,' says Me Bentley, • all :the clubs in the North of England were determined to have professionalism legalised, while the clubs in the South and the Midlands were no less strongly opposed to it. Major Marandin was in the chair, and taf rC W A loock, the Secretary of the Oval—although 89 an old Harrovian all hie foothill associations were in favour of amateurism—recognised that professionalism was inevi- table, and urged that it should be legalised. In the end, however. °le hundred and thirteen voted for profes- sionalism and one hundred and eight against it ; but es the requisite two-thirds majority had not been obtained, the law remained as it was. Six months afterwards another meeting of the Football Alsociation was held at Anderton's Hotel, at which the necessary number voted in favour of professionalism, which thus became law.' The Football League was formed in 1888; and its first president was Mr McGregor, who held that office until 1893, when he was succeeded by Mr Bentley, who six years previously had been elected a member of the Council of the Football 11,,sociation."</p>
        <p>A Stornoway correspondent teleyraphs that the church dispute at Ness appears to have been temporarily adjutted. As The result of conferences between the Sheriff and the leaders of the unionists and non-union- ists the special police were withdrawn from the church on Monday and marched to Port Ness, where they em- barked en 11.111 S. Bellona, which at once proceeded to Stornoway early on Tuesday morning for the mahaland. Twelve policemen only have been left at Ness,</p>
