        <head>BRADFORD AVIEGNOHD ONIVVARRINO-TON.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD AVIEGNOHD ONIVVARRINO-TON.</p>
        <p>Immense interest vele manifested b 7 football enthusiasts of Nab Lancashire and Y irkshire, in the meeting of Brad- ford and Warrington at Wilderspooi, princiaally beetirse the same teams met in the fourth round last seasen aad War- rington were successful. &amp; large contingent of their sup- parters accompanied the Bradford team and the Warrington crowd also turning out in full:force there would belfully 8,000 present when hosi hides eianrnencsd. Both teams turned out as advertised and the weather was fine and bright, while the turf was in sane lent condition. Mr P H Farrar of Halifax was the referee ant the teams line I ou'ii as follows : —9larrieglon :—Hellarn, fell back ; Fish, Dickenson, Rat- cliffe and Harris. threequarters ; Hoekentralt and alien lailf backs; Morrison, Boardman. latmonison FALCunningham , Taylor. J Swift and IV Swift. forwarde• Bradford —Mosby full-hack ; Pollard. Bagers, Sinton and Wilson thee. quarters M iredea and !leant no h alt-backs Smedley, Ludlam, Robertson, Feather, Hutt, F Smales, Sharratt and Dean, forwards, Bradfora won the toss, and promptly to time J Swift started for Warrington against a light breeze. There was no return and the immesters by a fierce rush carriedlplay well into the thradfo"t quire er. A fine kick by Dickenson an, a good follow up by Fish prevented Moeby from getting in his kink, and Warrington inhed the Bradford threequarter kfeee some stiff wok followed, and Cunningtam, by' charging (lowa kiek almost got through. Mosby. however, eaved grandly A splendid dribale by Dickenson through the midst of the Yorkshire team to* the ball to the line, and he had the hardest luck in not scoring. Bintan next got away, but Harris was too alert for him. Play now cautinnel in the Warrington territory, and as the result of splendid passing batweeea Marsden, Wagers, and Pollard the latter scored in the corner. Mosby failed at goal. Bradford now played up. and Warriagton hail a diffi- cult task to keen them out Pollard again got the ball, and was making full speed for the line when ndir caught him up an put him into touch. Warrington tried their hardest, but could not get Bradford away from their position cn the field, and Sinton looked all over a scorer when Hallam tickler. him and bowled him in touch. Again the ball came to the Warrington quarters, and Kellam had to make a dive for it in a position or great danger. A series of scrim- msgee followed within a ward train the Warrington line. Ereadley making great efforts to get through, but being well tackled each time. Harris relieved the oressure by ruining off-side and Mosby hit the pasts with his penalty kick. A good spurt was than m ,de by Swift end Ifoaken call, by a judicous kick across the field almost pet Warrington in a better position. Eagers and Pollard, however, again get along. and Hallam having the two to stop, failed to de so, and Pollard gat clear away He was, however, culler at by Boarriman on the line. A passing movement by Bradford followed. Had Wilson received Staten's ease he could hive walked in. Warrington were again penalised and Mosby failed at goal. Half time arrived immediately afterwards, with Bradfoed leading by 3 points to nil. Mosby restarted, and the initial stapes were fought at half- way. Eagers intercepted a pass kicked too strongly, and lost a fine ehance.Paseaw between Allen,Tayloaand Morrison regained lost ground mai the latter ran over oily tolaa brought back by the referee. Marsden igot away from the scrim- mage finely, and was almost free when Hoekenhall tackled him. A. couple of free kicks to Warrington gave them greet relief, ant Ratcliffe also followed up a kick well; tout soon afterwards Mashy, taking the ball at top speed, ran along touch, ant had the hardest of lines in not aearlag Good ex- change passing between Hockenhall and Fish was praductive of great gain to Warrington, and play at this stage was of a most exciting character. Warengton tried their best to get level. Hallam was now hurt but able to resume play and Bradford got the upper hand again. Exciting play followed near the Warrington line, and Harris threw the bail away, Broadley picking up and runnuag easily Irelands the posts. Mosby added a goal. Warrington appeared to lose heart after this, but eta! kept Bradford out until Wilson ran down and passed to Sinton. who scored toe third try. Only five minutes remained for play, when Fish picked up smartly and had a clear nourse, when the whistle soulde t tee the disgust of the crowd. Result :—Bradford 1 goal 3 tries (11 points), Warrington nil</p>
        <p>RUNCORN Y. OLDHAM. After defeating Leigh on Wednesday last in a re-played tie Runcorn met Oldham in the second round. The Runcorn committee had been offered £lOO by Oldham to play the match at Watersheddings, but the offer Was wisely declined, and the match took plaee at Runcorn. About 4,000 Spectators were present, Farmer commenced for Runcorn. The first incident of note was a dribble by the visiting forwards to the Runcorn end, where Houghton touched down. Farmer got well in the Oldham half with a run, but Thomas found relief, and Lawton and Spottiswoode passed in neat style. The home forwar as then broke away. Moore saying near the Old- ham line. From a scrummage Jolley got possession and dodged round the Oldham breks, scoring a try behind the posts. The same player landed a goal. Immediately on the resumption Jolley got possession again, end dropped a goal.</p>
        <p>Owens left the fist) hurt hut resumed after a few minutes • A. round of passing by Jolley, Warder and Flanagan ended, inlthe latter scoring,and Jolley's go il kick struck the croisbar and bounded back. Halt-time score :—Runcorn 2 goals and 2 tries Oldham nil After the interval Frater commenced for Oldham. Runcorn were wien on the attack. and gave the visitors an anxious time. Oldtem had great difficulty In keeping he home men out, the remaining play being in favour of Runsorn, who however, lacked finish, and did not add to their score. Result : —Runcorn 2 goals and 2 tries 00 points), Oldham nil. Teams : Runcoria.—flonghton, back ; Jones. Butterworth, Warder and Flanagan, three-quarter backs ; Jolley and Richardson, half backs ; Wsslger. Langley, Woods, Moulton, Farmer, Reece, Brookes and Higgins, forwards. Oldham.—Thomas, back : Civil, Lees. Moore and Spottiswooie, triree.quarter backs ; A Lees and Lawton,' half backs; Prater, TOPharn, Vowles, Broome. Bonsor, Owens, Grey and D Thomas. forwards. Referee. Mr W Robinson, Mannlisgham. Touch judges, Mr J M Gratiam,St Helens and Mr J A Squires,Brough ton, Rangers.</p>
        <p>SY/DNES V. NYAXEFIELD TRINITY. These teams met at Widnes, Wakefield won the toss, and had the advantage of a strong win". The first incident of note was a magnifieenc piece of work by the visitors' backs, and Greening was pushed into touch at the corner. Widnes pressed, end Hughes following up a kick dropped on the btll jest before Malkin Aspey failing to add the goal points Greening made a praty opening,and worked his way through his opponents the Widnes tackling being very weak at times. Millsom missed a good chance by kno3klng on. The game was very open. and not of the usual cup-tie order. Goodyear placed a goal from a mark, and at half-time Only cne pc.'nt separated the teams —Widnes 1 try, Wakefield 1</p>
        <p>goal. On resuming, Wakefield presse I, and the Widnes defence had an anxious time of It. They got the kall away, and play ruled at the ceatre. Bennett intercepted a pass, but a glorious chance was missed. At limes nlay was very excit- ing both ends being visited in quick succession, The Widnes backs got g,log, and after Lailey, Rispan, and Peacock had handled the bill the last named Scored, but Kinsella failed from a difficult position, Lalley next broke away. and ran completely through his opponents, and scored a brilliant try, from which Rigby placed a goal. On resuming, a lot of exchange kicking was indulged in but eventually Bennett scored a try anti Goodyear kicked a goal for Wakefield. Before the end Aspey obtained another try for Widnes. and Rugby' kicked a goal. Result :—Widnes, 2 goals and 4 tries (16 powts) ; Wakefield Trinity, 2 goals andl try (9 points).</p>
