      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR</head>
        <p>THE CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR</p>
        <p>MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>History of the Warrington and District</p>
        <p>Union.</p>
        <p>An interesting history of the Werrington and Dietrict Union of Young People's Societies of Christian Endea- vourempears in the current number of the "Manchester 1902, Y.P.S.C.E. Coevention Journal," a monthly paper published for the purpose of setting forth the arrange- ments in connection with the great British Convention of: Young Prople'a Societies of Christian Endeavour :'which is to be held in Manchester next Whitsuntide. 'This is the first history of the Union which has ever been written, and will no doubt prove of interest to our readers. It is accompanied by an excellent portrait of the energetic secretary of theWarsington and D striae Melone —Misi V M Taylor and reads as follows: The C. movement in Warrington owes more to the Rev E T Reed than any other Endeavourer in tied town. His time, voice, and willine service are continually being used in its interest,. There is not a society in the Union which has not received some help from him. It was on April sth, 1892, that the first Christian Endeav- our was formed in Warrington. in corenection with his Church—Emmanuel Reformed Church of England. At that meeting twenty young people joined as active and four as associa' e member.. The vvehly meetings of this soliety have been regularly held ever since. On Sat- urday, September 24th, 1892, twelve Christian Endeavourers went from Warrington to attend the first (?) Manchester Christian Endeavour Con- vention held in Oldham-road Congregational Chapel, having been most cordially invited by the Rev W Ei Towers, the pioneer or C.E :in Man- che, ter. Warrington in this way became associated with Mancirester in Christian Endeavour work,and when , dissolution became necessary because of growth, it was ' deckled that Warrington should be one of the centres at which a District Convention should be held. Arrange- meats were accerdingly made for the first Christian ' Endeavour Convention in Warrington to be held on - February Bth, 1891, in the Wesleyan schoolroom, Bold- street. As there were no f unds in hand to pay the ex- penses of this Convention, the preliminary steps were taken in faith. Much prayer was offered, and in a truly remarkable way, God honoured the faith of His servants, for both the afternoon and evening meetings were not only well-attended, but they were also full of spiritual power, and more money than was actually needed was given to defray eaPensee. At this pre- liminary Convention welled the efficient and valued help of the Revs J E Roberts, MA, B.D, and W H Towers. of Manchester, W Bainbridge then of Chester, J J Muir, F.L.S., at that time Preaene of the Liverpool and Dis- trict Union Special provision as made at this meet- ing fer the convenience of thh newerpaper reporters, who gave excellent reports in the local press.—At the afternoon meeting the Chairman (Rev E T Reed) empha- sised the united character of the movement, by quoting the following stanza " Presbyterians, Baptists. or Methodists we, Congreeationalists, Churchmen, whatever we be. We are all Christians in oar welcome of thee, Christian Endeavour 1"</p>
        <p>On the day of the Convention a meeting of repre. sentatry a was held at which it was decided to form a local Union for Warrington and Distrie. A constitution was subsequsratly drawn up and approved, and the first meeting of the Council was held on April 2713, 1894, when the following officers were elected :—President, Rev E T Reed ; Vic-President, Miss E M Allatt ; Trea- surer, Mr A Mounfield ; Secretary, Rev D W Roberts. The Union commenced with ten societies, the number now a ffiliated being twelve Young Pamle's Societies, and seven Junior Societies. Recently the societies in Widnes have, with the hearty consent of the Warring- ton and District Union, been formed into a separate union, which led to the withdrawal of ten SoCiPtiCe. It has been usual in this district to hold two Conventions annually, one in Warrington, the other in some outly- ing place. Accordingly, the first Autumnal Convention was held at Newton-le-Willows, on °cue!), r llth, 1894, when excellent local arrangements were made by our Vice-President, Miss E M Allatt, and by Rev J Allatt who presided at the evening meeting. The second Annual Convention was held in Wycliffe Csngregational Church, on February 7th, 1895, when Rev W Knight Cnaplin, Hon. Sec. of the British National Council, rendered ex-elient service in the aftei- noon and evening. The Rev John Yong., pastor of the Church, pi esided at the annual meet- ing. The next Autumnal Convention was held in Emmanuel Reformed Church of England, on October 12th, 1895, when the following contributed towarda a most interesting and successful gathering, Mr Henry Hutchinson, of the Children's Special Service Mission, Mr John Crumblehelme, of Bolton, Mrs Higgs, of Old- ham, Rev W H Towers, Rev James Mursell, who spoke on "American Christian Endeavour as seen at Beaton, '95," and Mr WII Bops, of Liverpool. Other Conven- tions have been held at Widnes (Fi-bruary 26th, 1896), Earlestown (November 19th, 1896), Warrington, Gel- borne-street Baptist Church (February 25;h, 1897). Latchford (October, 1897), Widnes (1898), LYinrn (May, 1901), Newton-I—Willows (October, 1901). On three different occasions a street collection has been taken up on behalf of Dr Bernardo's Home, the total amount collected being £lOB. The Christian E ideavour- era of Wartingten also ' co-operated in carry- ing through a street colleclen on behalf of the Warrington District Nursing Association. The sum obtained on this occasion Was £47. The Union has twice assisted the Manchester C.E. Cycling Corps in holding open-air meetings at Lyme). The f Alowing have held office dui ing the time the Union has been in existence :--President : Rev E T Reed, 1894-5.6 aad 1899 ; Miss E tI Allatt, 1897; Mr A Mounfieed, 1898 ; Rev J F Matthews, 1900-1. Vice- Presidents : Miss E M Atka, Rev T E Miller, Mr A Mounfield. Rev J F Matthews, Rev John Yonge. Secre- taries : Rev D W Roberts, Mr A Mounfield, Miss Beatrice Lees, Mr W R Beswick, Miss Venda M Taylor. Treasurers Mr A Mounfield, Mr J S Green, Mr R Atkinson, Mies V M Taylor, Mr W Barton. The Rev J F Matthews has beers very energetic during his term of office. fie has delivered about sixty addresses at E meetings, and visited every Young People's and Junior Society in the Union. As much of the success of a local union depends upon the effi3iency of the Secretary, it is only right to say that most ME- cient service has been rendered to the Warrington and District Union by several of those who have held that important office. Our first Secretary, the Rev D W Roberts, was indefatigable during the early days of the movement. Miss Beatrice Lses, during a secretariat extend:ng from 1E97 to 1901, rendered most efficient and devoted service. It may be truthfully said that no more loyal-hearted Chris ian Endea- vourer could be Mund. Mr Beswick's tenure of office was but short, but while he held it his duties were faithfully performed. Allusion might have been made to the united evangelistic efforts in which the Union has taken part, and t 3 the Evangelistic Mission held by Mr Henry Thorne, which was entirely carried through by the Christian Endeavourers. The growing work among the juniors calls for notice. A most successful Junior Rally was held on November 23rd, 1901, and it is truly a delightful thing that among the boys and gins a new generation of Christian Endeavourers is growing up, who will, it is hopod, carry forwaed into " fresh fields and pastures new" the blocd-3taitied banner of the , Cross.</p>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING CONVEN PION AT MANCHESTER. Rapid progress im being made in the arranvments for .the groat IN itional Christian Endeavour Convention which wil! be held in Manotiester on May 17th, 18th, 19,:h and 20h. Additional interest will be lent to the</p>
