        <p>HOLDROYD'S MEDICAL RALL, CLEOKHEATON YORKS.</p>
        <p>Conventioa by the tact that this year the C.E. movemet celebrate its zioming 9f age. The convention aiil be, so gigantic a character that for its accommodation it expected that the Free Trade Elan, St James' Ball, an Hengler's Circu4 will he engaged. There are now near' eight thousand Christian Endeavour Societies in ti United Kingdom and ills expected that some tweil thoutiand delegates, from all parts, will attend the Col vention, the preparations for which have been in pr( gress for nearly a year. The first draft of the Conventic programme has been Issued from which it appears ill' the Convention will open with a " domonstration I favour of civic righteousness sad international broth(' hood" which will be held in St James's Hal An interesting series of meetings and services has bet arranged for each day of the Convention and addresia on various topics will be delivered by well know speakers. The Convention will close on May 20th an for the following, day a number of excursions an ram bles have been organized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>GROCERS' AND PROVISION DEA LERS'DINNERATWARRINGTJI</head>
        <p>GROCERS' AND PROVISION DEA LERS'DINNERATWARRINGTJI</p>
        <p>The Association and its Work.</p>
        <p>There was an interesting function at the Wrarleigl Cafe, Warrington, on Thursday last week when the nn annual dinner lu connection with the newly-fennel Warrington and District Grocers' and Provision Deeleft Association was held. The difficulty of fixing a datl suitable all the members of the aseociaticn someggbn milit a ted against a large attendance. Nevertheless representative company sat down to dinner. Mr J' Richardson, the president of the Association, sat at ttn bead of the table and there were also present Mr a ' Giles (secretary of the Grocers' Federation), Mr J &amp;Hitt J.P. (chairman of the General Purposes Committee ° the Federation), Mr W W Walmsley (Willer and RHO', Manchester), Mr a E Worthington (W and R Jacob an' Company Limited), Messrs John Gandy, W PeeeN Jackson, 3D Leigh, E Gandy jun.,J Reid,J W Kay. S , Davey, r Cowdell, A Griffiths, W Monks, J P Go 0; and W H Owen. Letters of apology for non-attendan,n' owing to unforseen circumstances were read from nal Alfred Bawtlen (the hon secretary) and Mr Rotherfory of Liverpool. The tables were artietically decorated with chein spring flowers which had been provided by Mr Ogve,l who also presented each guest with a button-hole. excellent dinner was served under the superintendenv of Mrs J W Hayward. The loyal toasts having been proposed and herioute,.l with enthusiasm, Mr Kay proposed the health of Grocers' Federation and coupled with it the names: Mr Kellitt and Mr Gilee. In doing so he expressed hope that the Warrington Association would have 1, bright future Mare it and that its members won have a period of great prosperity. be Mr DAVEY, in seconding the proposition, said be lieved that the grocers and provision (tattlers ol ricgton were on the eve of far better things. felt the need of unity and mutual goodwill. He belien" it would be possible for the grocers of Warrington ci meet one another with a gracious smile. (Laggbtor)•l Mr KELLITT, in respondine,congratulfeed the gront? and provision dealers upon the formation of an assonr'd tion, and said he believed it would benefit tbe trg from every point of view. The association was not ai organization for the purpose of regulating prices it, was intended to bring them together socially, to zin„ them feel more and more interested in the trade interested in each others success. They were among,; the largest bodies of ratepayers and there were n 1); subjects of special importance affecting taxation, t,e, they ouvht to exercise their united influence in te`,.a mace thereto. In order to save them from €l,°'-i disadvantages. Then there were subjects raised Parliament upon vghich their voice as a body should heard. Mr Kellitt proceeded to mention seve:e instances in which the Grocers, Federation had 13;„`ii able to save the grocers of the country from disadvantages by influencing Parliament to take nn'' of their grievances.</p>
        <p>Mr GILES also responded, and pokted out that it I not the object of the Grocers Federation to Milne °ricers to form lo2al associations, because it was lot, that the associations were much stro-iger when the,' ' been established spontaneously in order to supply 5 long felt reed in the trade. The Federation weloos the formation of such associations, and . he hoped</p>
        <p>- Warrington Association would be affiliated with Grocers' Federation at an early date. (sear, hear). tt Giles proceeded to speak of the work of Federation, and referred to the succe 33 had attended its efforts in obtaining advantll; for the trade. Several insta• ces were given aff=et'il sugar, tea and biscuits. In the past, be the trade bad been going on in a " bappy-go-lue'; fashion, but in these days of keen competition it ,s; necessary kr them to be united, and to give clog', sistent, and intelligent attention to their busineS'i order to succeed. When the grocers were united ti these associations he had not the slightest doubt that ) future of the trade would be brighter and everyone 9i, I was a grocer would find that it was not a nan3e,i ivnomy but a name of honour of which he waS proud. (A Pplause.) SO Mr POVET, in proposing the health of the guests. there was room for further advancement. He eb° Ido his utmost to make the association a success 0 Mr GANDY seconded and agreed with toe reol,tl with regard to the inadvisability of regulating prie-°0 means of the association. It would nct 3 wise, be t to fix arbitrary prices. Their object was not to get, much out of their customers as possible, but to ^tip a profit which would provide them with a decent (Hear, hear.) Ic was for them to work on srosiDle friendly lines, e.ch trader to cousider his neigbbeli much as himself. The toast was rssponded to by Mr INALMSLEY SVC WORTHINGTON. erti Mr MONKS then proposed the teas,. of the Warrie . t.o</p>
        <p>Association. He thoaght they had every le rem, tO gratulate ttemselves on having formed the agEoCO''' Lie was certain it would be of good to the trade' the public. Mr RICHARDEON responded and said he had a deal to do with the initial stages of forming the ciation and he took it that the members reCognr' that when they elected him as president. HIS O: was to raise the standard of the trade. There ; many burning questions affe,iting the trid,i win they as individuals cluld do nothing, but if tbeY together and put forward their representations right quarter he believed they woilld be able to 'Y themselves heard, and many difficulties which fronted them would be removed. The remainder ot the eyeniok was snent in a so- manlier, progressive whist being a feature, of tbe ceeding,e.</p>
