        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB. --- Smoking Concert.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB. --- Smoking Concert.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cycling Club season was successfully inaugurated on Thursday last week when as moking con- cert was held at the Patten Arms Hotel. Members assembled in good force and the evening was very pleasantly spent. The veteran "Pa "Davies wee voted to the chair and presided over the gathering with his accustomed geniality. The officials responsible for the arrangement of the even- ing's programme were placed at some dis- advantage owing to the fact that several artistes who had promised to give their services were unable to be present. Notwithstanding this, the proceedings passed off as enjoyably as could be desired. The opening item was a pianoforte duet which was skilfully rendered by Mr Frank Booth and Dr Kisby. Later in the evening the same gentlemen contributed a second duet, and the company were not stinting in their expressions of appreciation. Mr E Morris com- menced the vocal part of the concert with a pleasing interpretation of the popular song " The storm fiend." Mr Jones sang "Star of my soul" with good effect and was cordially applauded. The humorous element was then introduced by Mr J Emson, who was as successful as ever in "Heigh up, its com- ing I " The beautiful song "In the shade of the sheltering palms " (Florodora) wag admirably sung by Mr W A Clay. Messrs E and F Morris gave an amus- ing duet "Nuggets" in whieh the choruses of many well known comic songs were introduced. Mr E Morris sang "The Boys of Eogland "—a song with a swinging chorus which was joined in by the company. Late in the evening Mr W H Ireland and Mr Poole, who had been singing at another concert, arrived and Mr Ireland delighted the assembly with a highly creditable rendition of " The sound of the drum" in Which his pure-tenet] voice was heard to great advantage. Mr Poole sang " Blew, blow thou wintry wind" so effectively that an eneore was demanded and Mr Poole responded with "The raft." Another pleasing item was the duet " Excelsior " by Messrs Ireland and Poole. whose voices blended very sweetly. Other items on the programme were seines by Mr Jones and Mr Eamon which were well received. Mr Frank Booth ably discharged the duties of accompanist. During an interval the Chairman reminded the mem- bers that the opening run would take place on Saturday and expressed the hope that there would be a good I muster to support the Captain and Sub-captain. By attending the runs the members could show that they I appreciated the services rendered by the officials. The Secretary (Mr E Pickavance junior), announced that he had received,telegrams from Mr Verco,St Helens and Mr 13 Houghton, one of the vice-presidents, regret- ting their inability to be present. He added that on 1 Saturday he had a visit from another of their worthy vice-Presidects, Mr R Pierpoint, MP, who said he I would most certairly be present at the concert. On , Wednesday, however, he received a letter from Mr , Premolar, regretting that he would not be able to come as he had to attend a committee of the House of Corn- mons that day (Thursday). Continuing, Mr Piekavance expressed the hope that there would be a good turn-out I at the opening run, and referring to the programme for I the season, said he thought it was very comprehensive, and no one could find very much fault with it. ,If the members discharged their duties faithfully 1 and helped the committee and their young and I promising officials they would no doubt have a very successful season. Be was very pleased that they had the presence that night of Mr Stonier, of Roby. Much of the popularity of the club was no doubt due to tba gentleman's able articles in the " Cycler's News" and it was very kind of him to favour them with his presence. Mr STONIER said it was a great pleasure for him to be present as he always enjoyed his visits to Warrington. Last year be bad occasion to congratulate the club on the honour which had been conferred up it by the elec- tion of the Secretary for the eighth time to the position of secretary to the Roads Committee of the N.C.U. this year be had been elected to the highest possible Office in the Liverpool centre—that of Chairman. (Applause). The election was by ballot, several other names being proposed,but Mr Pickavance came out a long way ahead of the others. Be (the speaker) was sure the club was proud of the distinction conferred upon the secre- tary, and they in Liverpool were proud to have Mr Pickavance as chairman of the centre. (Applause.) A vote of thanks having been accorded to the C hair- man the proceedings were brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
