      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Chasing Delarey.</p>
        <p>Big Combined Movement.</p>
        <p>Guns Captured.</p>
        <p>135 Prisoners Taken.</p>
        <p>The following despatches were issaed by the War Office on Wednesday _</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener to the Secretary of State for War PRETORIA, TUESDAY, NOON.</p>
        <p>At dusk on the evening of the 23rd of March a combined movement against Delarey was under- taken by columns of mounted troops only, without guns or impediments of any sort. The columns started from Commando Drift,on the Vaal, Klerksdorp,and Vaal Bauk,on the Lichtenburg blockhouse line.</p>
        <p>_ Kekewich's columns, after the start of the action, captured three 15-pounders, two pom-poms, nine prisoners, 100 mules, and some carts and wagons. General Walter Kitchener's columns captured 89 prisoners; 25 carts, 20 wagons, and 1,000 cattle. There are a few more prisoners and surrenders on the blockhouse lines.</p>
        <p>The troops covered 80 miles in 24 hours. Lo rd Kitchener to the Secretary of State for War :</p>
        <p>PRETORIA, Tuesday, 3 p.m</p>
        <p>In continuation of my telegram of noon to-day, all reports are now in. The total number of pris- oners amounts to 135.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Liverpool Scottish Volunteers.</head>
        <p>The Liverpool Scottish Volunteers.</p>
        <p>A Week-end in Liverpool. Departure for the Front.</p>
        <p>The special service section of the Liverpool Scottish Volunteers spent last week-End in Liverpool. The men, who bad got special leave of absence from Warrington, arrived at the Central Station, Liverpool, at about a quarter-past seven on Saturday evening, and marched direct to the Town Hall, wkere they were the guests of the Lord Mayor at a banquet. Their personal equip- ment had been completed at Warrington, and, whilst retaining the kilts, thay now wore the khaki service tunic of the Gordon Highlanders, to whom they will be attached, and the latest style of service hat, of the wide- awake pattern. They also carried their bandoliers, and their smart and soldier-like appearance attracted con- siderable attention during their passage through the streets. As they gathered inside the Town piper of the Bth V.B. discoursed a selection of stirring airs,and this certainly lent additional animation to the scene. After the banquet, a number of toasts were submitted, and in the course of his speech, Captain Blair, who had had had the section under his charge at Warrington during the week, paid a high tribute to the manner in which the men 'had conducted themselves whilst in the barracks. No offence ot any kind had been recorded against them.—During the evening enjoyable selections of music were given by the Euterpean Orchestra, direct- ed by the Misses Emilie and Annie Scott. Miss Londini was the vocalist.—On Sunday the Volunteers, in com- pany with their comrades of the 3th Battalion, marched to Seftnn Park Presbyterian Church, where the Rev Dr Watson (chaplain of the battalion, and whose son com- mands the section) delivered a touching valedictory discrurse. Great crowds of people witnessed the parade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Departure of tile sth Irish and</head>
        <p>The Departure of tile sth Irish and</p>
        <p>Liverpool Scottish from Warrington.</p>
        <p>• The sth Irish and Liverpool Scottish contingent ha a splendid send-off at midnight on Tuesday when they left the Central Station, Warrington for Southampton, en route for South Africa. A large number of relatives and friends bad come from Liverpool, and, in addition, the townspeople had gathered in great force, with the result that when they left Orford Barracks, shortly before eleven o'clock, the streets were lined with people the whole length of the route. The detachments were beaded by the depot band, and on their arrival at the station the platform was s.on crowded. During the time the Volunteers waited for the tram the band played a number of selections, and the pipers of the Liver- pool Scrttish, who bad COMA from Liverpaol for the occasion, also played a number of martial Scottish airs on the bagpipes. The crowd was very enthusiastic during the long wait. The Midland train by which the Volunteers left Warrington arrived in the station at 11.53 and a minute or two after midnight, amid tre- mendous cheering, the train steamed away, the band and bagpipes playing together "Auld Lang Syne." The sth Irish will join the Royal Irish Regiment, and the Liverpool Scottish joined the Gordon Highlanders on their arrival at Southampton. The sth Irish number thirty-seven men, under Lieutenant Grindley. The Liverpool Scottish number twenty-two men, and are under Lieutenant Watson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>AT DEATH'S DOOR. -- CHRONIC STOMACH TROUBLE CURED BY VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC.</head>
        <p>AT DEATH'S DOOR. -- CHRONIC STOMACH TROUBLE CURED BY VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. WILSHAW, RIBBLE BANK, SETTLE. Yorks, writes :—You will be glad to know that Veno's Sea weed Tonic has worked wonder in =cease. For the last two years I suffered very much with pain after eating, and wind. ay stomach was so bad that I could not eat solid food, I lost a great deal of strength and ft,sh and was not able to work. I tried all kinds of medicine but without any benefit, and the doctors gave up trying to Cure me. It was a happy day for me when I started taking Veno's Seaweed Tonic. After the first week the pains were relieved, also the wind. I began to relish my food and gradually got stronger, and now I feel myself cured and able to work. It is a godsend to me. I stall never forget your Seaweed Tonic. VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC is the great medium that restores health. It is used in nearly every British home because of its great tonic and strengthening rower, and its specific action in all cages of stomach, LIVER, KIDNEY, and BLOOD DISEASES. For WEAK HEART and HABITUAL CONSTIPATION it has no equal. A bank cheque for 24. 6. pay- able through the London Trading Bank, Ltd., is enclosed with every 2. 9i. bottle. You are entitled to collect 21. 61. for every 2s. 9J. bettle you purchase if it fails to cure you completely. It is a genuine guaran- tee and means a cure or no pay. Ask at chemists and medicine vendors ter Veno's Seaweed Tonic. Price it. I,ld. and 24. 93. Sold by Boots Ltd., Warrington ; Peake. Earlestown and Newton ; Weston and Co.. Runcorn ; Wallbridge, chemist, J. W. Jackson, chemist and druggist, Sr. Helene.</p>
        <p>Joe Corbett's Mission. The mission to the people held in Parr Hall was again a decided success- On Sunday last Joe Cor- bett addressed a large congregztion, and was sup- ported by Mr James Dodds, Mr A B Windle. Mr R Scarisbrick, Mr Johnson and several others. During the service, Miss Polly Houghton and Miss Nellie Anderton sang the duet "I waited for the Lord (Mendelssohn),which was a rich musical treat. Miss Polly Eloughton also sang a FOlO "Meditation " (Chaminade), with great taste. The Bewsey-road Choir also sang two anthems, under the conductor- ship of Mr B Wadsworth, and Mr Joe Corbett song a solo out of Sankey's accompanied by Mr Scans- brick on his one stringed fiddle. Mr Albert Spruce conducted the massed choir, and Mr Fred Pod more,. M.I.U.M. presided a': the organ and piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
