      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS. Death of MT. Cecil Rhodes.</p>
        <p>CAPETOWII, THURSDAY. Mr Cecil Rhodes died at six o'clock last night. The end was quite peaceful, and Mr Rhodes was able to recognise some of those round the bedside. It is understood that he will be buried, in accord- ance with his own request, in Rhodesia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Well, there is still the West Lancashire Cup to strive for! Whatever pretensions Warrington had to the Northern Union cup this season were promptly squelched on Saturday by Bradford, and I cannot say that I was surprised. Warrington would have had to play a long way above recent form to again defeat the Bradfordians, who are quite at their best just now, and will take a lot of licking in the cup tourney.</p>
        <p>There was some hope in Warrington that the locals might rise to the occasion, especially as they had never failed to do so in a home cup-tie since they were a club, but though they made a gallant effort they were quite overpowered. It was the hope of some- thing better that drew such a hrge crowd, about 8,000 being present, and the gate receipts totalling £193. The hope was summarily extinguished though not until the match was three parts over, and Warrington's proud record has gone by the board with a crash. Bradford s win by 11 points to nil was mortifying but there is the small consolation that Warrington were not so bad as the score indicates. They played a very plucky game, but the weight, strength and science of Bradford, combined with the weight and strength of the referee's apparently biassed opinions, were too much for Warrington. A couple of referees were mobbed last Saturday, and Mr Farrar was lucky to escape such a fate at Warrington, for it is a long time since an official's decisions on the play were received with such strong disapproval by the Warrington crowd. Bradford appeared unable to do wrong in the referee's eyes and Warrington were unable to do right, with the consequence that the whistle scarcely ever sounded when Bradford were on the move, but it never failed to shrill out when the home team made progress. It was aggravating and disheartening, and the unpleasant reflection could not fail to cross the mind of the most impartial observer that the referee, like the visiting team, was from Yorkshire !</p>
        <p>It is just as well to admit now that the best team won for there can be no doubting the fact, notwithstanding that luck was dead against the homesters. Bradford had an immense superiority forward and eventually they overplayed Warrington in every department, though this sequence of events was due to the fact that the visitors were almost always in possession of the ball. The Bradford forwards came with the avowed intention of smashing up the Warrington combination, and they succeeded admirably,at the same time giving their own backs endless opportunities for attack which they made good use of, though I think the Warrington three-quarters would have been more I dangerous had the same chances come their way. Chief honours must undoubtedly go the Bradford vanguard, who are as fine a set of forwards as anyone needs wish to see, while they are capitally led by Tom Broadley. It is a long time since the Warrington scrummagers were made to look so small, both metaphorically and I actually, on their own ground. They scarcely ever got the ball and in the loose were neither as speedy nor as ' capable as the Bradford men. Hutt, the biggest of the lot, was particularly active and ran, fielded, punted and tackled in really fine style, while his skipper was, as usual, übiquitous.</p>
        <p>The commencement of the game was rather exciting, for Warrington went off with a fierce rush which actually bore the visitors back on their own line. Dickenson came near scoring with a dashing dribble, but that was about as near as Warrington ever got to annexiag points. The Bradfordians soon recovered from the effect of the homesters' sudden onslaught, and were early operating in the vicinity of the Warring- ton quarter. Marsden got away,and though tackled by Hockenhall he managed to pass, with the result that Pollard received from Eagers, and dashed over at the corner. For the rest of this half the Bradford men kept on pegging away in the home territory, but met with an excellent defence. There was one spell of several minutes when excitement ran high, for the visitors scrimmaged on the Warrington line, and Broadley hovered round like a live battering-ram looking for a weak spot in the chain opposed to him. That weak spot was not found however, and the burly international was downed several times before Harris put an end to the suspense by deliberately running off-side. The move gave nradford a penalty, which was fruitless, and probably saved a try. At the interval the visitors were only one try ahead. •</p>
        <p>What advantage there was in the wind fell to War- rington after half time, and for a short spell they flattered their supporters by keeping Bradford on the defensive. Two or three dead balls were registered and Morrison had hard luck in getting over when the referee was against him. Several times the autocrat of the whistle intervened when Warrington appeared to have a chance, and eventually the visitors worked their way to the other end A hot attack was main- tained, and at last Broadley scored easily after Harris had lost the ball on the goal line. Mosby kicked a goal and all was practically over'. By and by Sinton added the third and last point by a fine effort, while to com- plete Warrington's mortification Fish was brought up by the whistle when he had cleverly secured possession and appeared to have a clear course. A series of (minions hootings concluded the game,for the crowd was none too well pleased by the general run of things, though they admitted being beaten by a better team.</p>
        <p>Taken right through the game was not a bad one of the typical cup tie order. The keenness which is always associated with such contests was a ,feature, and the tackling at times was quite an object lesson in the art which would have appealed strongly to such a man as J. L. Daniell, the English captain, whose hints on forward play were published in the " Examiner " on Saturday last. There was still a fair amount of open I play, principally by Bradford, for the Warrington ' halves kicked frequently when a pass would probably have ibeen more serviceable. It was really astonishing how much neglected the Warrington wings were, both of them standing idle except when defence was called for on their side. I don't think Fish had a pass from Dickenson all the afternoon and this was a serious blemish on the otherwise vigorous and skilful game played by the centre. Both Fish and Harris would have made better use of toe ball than some of their fellow-players had they only been able to get hold of it. Fish did make a couple of characteristic runs, but he was too far from the line to be really dangerous. Mars- den and Eagers were the best men on the field, both of them playing finely, while in the rear Mosby was very safe. Hallam also merits praise, for he had a tremend- ous amount of work to get through and generally ac- quitted himself well. Ratcliffe was the one failure on the home side, and he never did himself justice all the afternoon either on the attack or defence. Perhaps the broken finger he sustained had something to do with it. 'Boardman was easily the best of the for- wards, and was ever to the fore. One tackle he made prevented Pollard from scoring when. he had safely ne- gotiated Hallam.</p>
        <p>Though the Bradford backs are not usually con- sidered a class combination, they are a good lot individually and on Saturday they got through some very effective work. In Eagers they have a centre of similar build to Bob Wilson, and little inferior in point of skill. His punting was really superb at times,while he never neglected his wing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>At a General Council meeting of the National Cyclists' Union held at the Inns of Court Hotel, on Saturday, the Right Hon. A J Balfour was unani- mously re-elected president of the Union, and all the Vice•presidents re-elected en bloc, whilst Mr T NY J Britten was re-appointed treasurer. It was decided to form a sub-committee to consider the question of in- come. The old rule dealing with first year licences was revived, and ;the propositon by the Manchester Centre, that in view of the establishment of the Boards of Control the rule requiring the names of handicappers to be stated on prospectuses be rescinded, was passed. It was also decided that the minimum fees for entries at village sports shall be ss,lialf of which amount shall be returnable to headquarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PZNKETH.</head>
        <p>PZNKETH.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT PEICSETR.—The annual meeting of lk.iankey and Penkatti polling district of the Newton Parliamentary Division tcok place at Periketh Conservative Club on Tuesday night. Mr J Fulton Smith presidcd. The following officers for the ensuing sear were;elected :—Chairman, Mr J Fulton Smith; vicc- chairmen, Mr Baker (Bold) and Mr Gorse (Sankey) ; hon secretary, Mr J Davies ; delegates,MessrS J Brockle- ' burst (Bold), J Wright (Cuerdley), and P Pownall (Penketh). On the motion of Mr King,seconded by Mr Smith, and supported by Mr T W Deacon, the following resolution was passed That this meeting desires to express its strong approval of the general policy of his M:ijesty's Ministers, and has every confidence in their efforts to bring the vast to a successtul termination, and that a prosperous and contqated Colonial federation will be established in South Africa, and that in due course Lord Salisbury and his ,:olleagues will tee able to carry out beneficial legislation for au i classes of his Majesty's subjects both at home and abroad."</p>
        <p>NITRE GEE'S SPEY DISTILLERY, situate in the noes:, N't hisky-producing District of Scotland, is tho property of W and A Giibey. This Whisky is made entirely from Home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in His Majesty's Bonded Warehouses ts misture,and sold by W and A Gilbey's agenls in every town at 3s fid per bottle.</p>
        <p>i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER. THURSDAY MARCH 27, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. THURSDAY MARCH 27, 1902</p>
        <p>ongregatinn like that. He thanked those who had come from other churches to hear what they expected would be a farewell sermon, and ho hoped they would net think he had been unmindful of what they name for. He did not desire to harrow their feelings ia the ordinary way, but he would say what be had to say when he wrote his letter to the parish magazine that week. They would see then what would, perhaps, ex- press hid own innermost thoughts. They were all differ- ent, and there was not one the same as another, and one could not, therefore, in a general congregation, say just the right thing for each. They must go on, and as they met and told each other their own experiences there might be a continuation of the help they had been to each other during the past three years. The departure of Mr Tarbuck is deeply regretted in the parish. During his three years' connection with Warrington he has not only worked zealously in his parish:but has also taken a deep interest in the public institutions of the town. The Easter offertL ry will take the form of a testimonial to tbellev E ft Tarbuck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A Society. There was a well-attended meeting in connection with this society on Sunday afternoon. Mr Joseph Owen occupied the chair and briefly addressed the meeting. The speaker was Mr Jago, of Manchester, and be delivered an interesting address. Mr W H Wallington gave an excellent interpretation of the solo " The Palms." Tbe organist was Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bac. Mr Farrar, the hon secretary,also took part in the meet- ing and Mr Josephaarner read the lesson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Late Captain Savage. Memorial Service at Bewsey-road Wesleyan</head>
        <p>The Late Captain Savage. Memorial Service at Bewsey-road Wesleyan</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>An impreesive memorial service of the late Captain Savage was held in Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening. The Rev John Gunnell of Penketh, conducted the service. There was a large congregation The pulpit was draped in black as a mark of respect. An ,anthemi "Thou shalt keep him in perfect peace whose mind is stayed on Thee" was sting by. the choir under the conductorship of Mr B Wadsworth. The r reacher paid a fitting tribute to the memory of the deceased Captain. Referring to his birth at Runcorn in 1829, the preacher stated that Captain Savage was of bumble origin. He had but few and very meagre educational advantages and had to go to work—and very hard work too—when be was quite a lad. He never forgot those somewhat rough experiences of his early days. On more than one occasion he had referred to the fact that he had walked beside a horse on the towing path for twenty miles in one day and received as his remuneration one shilling. His father was a good Christian man, and although one of the poorest of men, so marked was his Christian character, so deeply had that character im- pressed the people of the town in which he lived that they came in crowds to his funeral to pay their respect to his memory. Not long before his death he said to his son (the late Captain Savage) " Now my lad I have no wordly goods to leave you, but heave you my character, my example and my prayers "—a priceless heritage which he need scarcely say Captain Savage turned to very good account. The preacher proceeded to give a brief outline of the life of Captain Savage, alluding to his removal to Sankey Bridges when 18 years of age. Some years afterwards he married, and found in his partner a help- meet indeed. Who of those who were present at the celebration of their golden wedding a•short time ago could forget that happy gathering ? Reference having been made to his removal to Widnes and his subsequent return to Warrington to take the pastoral charge of the Workingmen's Mission at Bank Quay, the preacher observed that the three years which Captain Savage gave to the work in con- nection with the Workingmen's Mission made a deep impression on his Christian character and life. His heart went out to all Christian missions and he was ever ready to give a helping hand to those engaged in work of thie kind. The end came with startling sudden- ness—not too sal Ii a for himielt, for he (the preacher) was told that Captain Savage bad a desire, if the Lord willed it so, that he might not have a lingering illness and so be a trouble to anybody. There were several traits in the character of Captain Savage which he would like to allude to. First of all he was a man of more than average intelligence. Ile did not mean to say that Captain Swage had much book learaing,though be read carefully and read a good deal. His knowledge was rather that obtained by close observation, by shrewd intelligence and by a good memory. He not only read much but he thought a great deal. In these dap; there was a great deal more reading than thinking. Captain Savage gave himself time to think of what he had read and observed and took the liveliest interest ia public affairs. Ile was well grounded in the theology of his own church. To the young men of that congregation he was a fine example. He was a man of fine Christian spirit and Godly habits, a man of prayer and a man of faith. Not only was he a good man but he was essentially a kind man. All good people were not always kind. There were some in whom kind- ness took a rather harsh and repellent form, but that was not so with Captian Savage. Anyone farthee removed from selfishness it would be difficult to imagine. A man of kind nature, kind manner and a iman with a kind word for everybody. He was kind to the poor. He loved to do good by stealth. They should bear in mind that his means were never very large. He</p>
        <p>was not sure whether the impression abroad was not the reverse of that. Let him correct the impression. The captain's means were never on a large sale. He might have spent every penny about himself and his home and yet have lived in a very modest style. There were some people who thought that because they could not give very much therefore they had a very good reason for not giving at all. That was not the csptain's idea of duty. When he had little he gave of that little to his needy neighbour, and when his income increased his generosity inoreased, and there were on record many examples of his generosity to those in nevi. The children loved him for he always had bright smile and cheery word for them whenever they met him. They looked up with confidence to that face beaming with kindliness, and they loved him. An old man who was loved by children must be a good man or he must have some very excellent qualities in him. Might they copy the good Christian :ample set before them by their beloved and now d eceased friend. Baptist Church Goliaorne-street.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday a great simultaneous collection was held in considerably more than 1,200 Baptist churches on behalf of the uonominational Twentieth Century Fund. The amount aimed at for the day was £50,000. The I otject of the fund is to raise £250,000 for toe extension of the Kingdom of Christ in our own land. Of this sum £155,000 has actually been paid and nearly £50.000 more has teen promised. It is hoped that by this great final effort the £250,000 aimed at will be completed. On Sunday at the antra church, services had been arranged to join in this last supreme effort to complete , the Fund. The Rev Herbert Dunnico was the preacher for the day, ant his discourses were listened to with pleasure by good congregations. In the morning the preacher preceded his sermon by an address to the I young on "Salt "and gave some very beautiful lessons from this ever useful article. The subject of the sermon was, " The Kingdom of God " taken from Revelations, chap:7,verse 9. The Kingdom of God said the preacher, is not ta be estimated by outward appearances,what the world counts nothing Christ counts gain. Of John in Patinas the world would say, it was death, degradation, and shame, heaven said it was victory, peace a d glory. In the afternoon a scholars' service was held in. the</p>
        <p>_ chapel, when Mr Dunnico gave an addreis on the object of the" Baptist Twentieth Century Fund,"and explained its purpose and, use vary clearly and plainly to the</p>
        <p>yeung people. The evening's discourse was based upon the subject of "Amusements," the text being taken from 22nd chapter Matthew verso 21. " Render unto Csssar the things that era Ctestr's." At the commencement of the dis- course the preacher said he did not intend to apologise for taking this subject, for religion was to make our , lives better, it was to help us. If religion is part of our lives.it will show us whet amusements we are to indulge in. Amusement and recreation is.essential,but it is our duty to see that our pleasure is not in wrong amuse- ments. After speaking about where to draw the line in amusements the preacher spoke in a most earnest and pleading manner of man's duty to himself, his neigh- bour, and his God. God sent us not simply to amuse ourselves, but to be useful and of service in the world. Seeing the way life is being frittered away and the craving that people have for amusements, one has to be bold and speak out against ir. Respect for self brings respect for others. We have no right to go to any place which lowers Ourselves. Amusement is legitimace, but, when we find it is gaining mastery over us, we ought to give it up. Every man OWC3 a duty to his neighbour, exercises some influence over others, therefore we have no right to mix no with anything which might 0611913 another to stumble, we have no right to go and see anything that degrades others. Our duty and purpose is to glorify God. The amusements we engage in ought to help us to glorify Rim. What will be joy when this life ceases ? Will it be that we have made money, or a great name? No, said the preacher, joy to me will bs to know I have " led some lost one home." —.At each service collec- tions were taken on behalf of the Century Fund, and resulted in the spiendid sum of £l6 12s 6d being con- tributed, £l9 lOs 61 briing promised. As £5O has already been given by this Church, the grend total amount raised for thus fund is £76 3s, which is con- sidered very satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Vicar of St. Paul's Farewell Sermon.</head>
        <p>The Vicar of St. Paul's Farewell Sermon.</p>
        <p>There was a large congregation in St Paul's Church, Warrington, on Sunday evening, when the Vicar' the Rev E R Tarbuck, who baS been appointed Vicar of St. George's, Altrincham, preached his farewell sermon. The rev gentleman selected for his text the words '1 thirst," being the last words of the 28th verse Of the 1911 chapter of St John. At the close of his discourse, Mr :Tarbuck said be would not preach a farewell ser- mon in the ordinary secs. He was not going far away and would be within reach of those who had appreci- ated his work in Warrington. What seemed to him to be unneeeassry WAS that he should appeal to their per- sonal feelings, feelings that would quickly evaporate, emotional feelings that would be of no use beyond Mt. day. If his work had not gone deeper than that he would not insult them or degrade himself by appealing to superficialities of that kind, but he thirsted tor their welfare. It was that thirst that made him take orders in the Church and become a commissiened officer in it. And they must bear in mind the thought that the man who had worked amongst them for three years' and , worked so imperfectly as to make him ashamed of the deficiencies of his work, would always thirst far infor- mation of them, individually or as a body. Be had been their parish priest, their vicar, for three years,and he should th rst for their improvement in every way. They would grant that to be an honest statement of the case. The rest was, perhaps, too sacred to be told in a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>FRANCE ANO RELIGION.</head>
        <p>FRANCE ANO RELIGION.</p>
        <p>Interesting Lecture by the Rev. D. A. de Mouilpied. "The Most Religious Conntry on the Continent."</p>
        <p>An extremely interesting lecture on France was delivered by the -Rev D A de Mouilpied in the Bold Street Wesleyan Church, Warrington, on Monday evening,—Mr J G Withinsha we presided over a moder- ate attendance, amongst those present being Revs T Bishop,C 11 Hocken,lW C J Neville, and J F Matthews In the Course of his remarks tee CHAIRMAN said he held that in France the Protestant cause was awakening, 1 but they must mind that as Protestants awakened in France they must not go to sleep in this country. The Lecturer said he looked upon his lecture as a direct contribution to the missionary enterprise. They had had missionaries at Bold-street °march who had served Christ in China, India, Fiji, Africa, and other parts of the world, but, he questioned whether anyene had spoken to them of Christianity in France. While thinking as every missionary thought of his mission field, and appreciating his enthusiasm, when be Con- sidered things all round he felt there was no field so interesting, that had such traditions, and such glorious memories and which at present was exercising an influ- ence to formation of thought and action in other lands as that great, glorious, and enigmatical country ! about which he was to speak to them. He wished to direct their attention to France as a relig- ious country. He was surprised at the imperfect infor- mation of many of their friends concerning France. Nowhere, perhaps, was that want of information more manifested than in their appreciation of the moral life of France, and certainly of the religious life of France. The common opinion was that there was no sucu thing asenerality in France, and, consequeatly no religious life. He was quite prepared to admit that everything they had heard concerning:the irreligioa and tile ant 3.. religious feeling of French people could be easily proved and that it was possible to find in France to-day SO:110 of ithe most violent and passionate agitators against clericalism—against the Church. He had read in many religious pagers in this country that there were eight million Atheists in F ance. At the last census there was a column in the paper set apart in which persons were requested to say to what religion they belonged.' The French Government were very liberal in their patronage and clergy of the Roman Catholic Church, Reformed Church, and the Lutheran Church,the Jewish Rabbis, and the Mohammedan and Mullahs were all paid by the Government. Tne Methodists received nothing, and they were in France, as England a free church. (Applause) There was another tree church, what they might call an offshoot of the Church of Scotland, which had a splendid record in France, but apart from these two churches,the others were recognised by the Govern- ment, and the object ot the Government in making that request at the census was to know definitely how many persons belonged to each of these various con- fessions. Strange to say eight million people were re- turned as having no religion, that was, really, that the leight million people did not belong to the State churches. That, was what led people to . think they were justified in saying that there were eight million Atheist; in France. He had lived eighteen years under the French flag, nine years in Paris, and bad come into contact with people who called themselves Atheists, but after a few mo- ments' convereation he found they were no mere Atheists than he was. They were very far from being good Methodists, but they were not Atheists at all and ' he had never met with an Atheist in France. He was prepared to say that considering the genius of the Fr:sum people there was not a nation upon the face of the earth so opposed to absolute Atheism. The French were intensely idealistic and in no country in the world had theories such a hold upon the imegination and upon the life of the people as in France. No nation was so thorough in its appiicetion of greet principles to social or political life. France had been the ecclesiastical political, and socisl laboratory of Europe and of the whole world. It was Said that French life was made up of frivolity and passion, but if they read the tragic history of the country they would think differently. In 1 100 years she had sacrificed more than two millions of her sons for the realization of her ideals—" Liberty, equality, and fraternity." France was not an atheiatic country, and it was not a profoundles irreligious country. Should he surprise them when he told taem that it was the most religious country on the conti- nent of Europe ? French people raised far more for foreign missions than they (the Wesleyans) did, Last:yeerithe Roman Catholic Church in France raised a good deal over £400,000 tor forego misaione and the Protestant church raised something like £50,000. The Roman Catholic Church in France _raised more money</p>
        <p>for the propagation of Roman Catholicism in Eogland than was ever raised in England for the propagation of Protestantism in France. Yet he thought Fran cc te-day was from the religious point of view :.the moat agitated and storm-toseed nation in Eurore. Toe greates t trouble was the trouble of toe sou', and Prance Wa !profoundly soul troubled, and that not only for the reason that France was such an idealistic nation but because she had had such a 'marvellous religious history. France was the country where that great mar- vellous movement, the world-shaking and transforming movement—the Reformation ef the 16to Century—wee the most fiercely debated and foul,tit. That question was settled in England by God's blessing for all time. It was settled in the triumph of Evangelical truth and the liberation of the conscience and intellect of the Angle-Saxon race from the superstitious, thraldom, and I tyrannies of Rome. The question, however, was never settled finally in France, though it was discussed there I with a vehemence and a passion unknown in any other country. Referring to the reign of Queen Mary, and of ! the dark yeara ol persecution, he said there were 218 people put to death in England for the faith. All honour to them: Let them keep their memories green and ever remember that they owed much Co the constancy, forti- tude, and faithfulness of those holy martyrs, who kept the faith in days of yore. But that number was not a day's work in France. in 03q massacre alone the lowest anthority put it that 30,000 Protect ants were massa- s cred whilst others put the number at 100 000. All _</p>
        <p>those Protostants lost -their lives in that one massacre— that of St. Bartholomew. The religious war in France lasted for more than 200 years, and in one year alone over 32,000 Pre'estant churches were levelled to the ground. There was not a single ministei's house or a sinele Protestant left in France after that. The nableet page in the history of the Church of G )cl—a page written in blood—wits written by those men who held not their lives dear. They were all familiar with that period of French histoi y called the revolution, and the position of the Boman Catholic Church at that time. It was the mightiest force in France, and owned ome -third of the territory area. When the revolotien broke out, all that, property—cathedrals. abbeys, monaeteries, conveet3 churches, and everything that constituted eeclesiasticia property—was swept into ,the National Exchequer , Napoleon the First, tho wever, restored the church and Put back a church and a Priest in every parish. Then followed a succession of reigns, and the Church regained hcr power. After the Franco-German war there was a tremendous re-action against the Church in the matter of education, which had been practically in the hands of the church. Whenever the educational interests of any country were subordinate to ecclesiastical interests that country was bound to decay or to fall. The French netple felt that they had been defeated not by the German auns, hut by the German schoolmaster. Whilst. 50 per cent of the French army could neither read nor write, the Germans and Prussians were educated and knew the situation and position of the places they wished to go to. Then France immediately set about to educate her people, and the Republic had made marvellous advances in that direction. That step brought on a conflict between Clericalism and the State If there was one thing that the clericals prayed for it was that they might overthrow "this ever to be accursed ever to be damned Republic." The struggle was still going on between the lay spirit and the clerical spirit. But religious freedom obtained as perfectly in France as in England, and he (the speaker) was as free to go to any town or hamlet in France as he was able to do in England. The question of Divine right of kings had been settled in England, and the question of Divine riglv s of clericalism would be settled by the civilization of the 19th century. The French Government had decided that every church should give an account of its dolags,and should he under the common laws of the land There was a marvellous movement going on in the French Catholic Church, and ;during the past few years no fewer than 375 priests had gone out of the cherch. France was tired of supersti:ions ; and was hungry for Christ. If they took Christ as their leader they would never reach a goal of darkness of horror, and of blood, and in taking Christ by the band they would realize that Be was indeed a leader of men and the leader of Feance in the way of faith in which she had trod so magaifmently. In conclusion the speaker said he wished to leave them with the thought, that in doing mission work they were doing the grandest, noblest and most° abiding work that could b e done by teen en this side Heaven. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EARLESTOWN _CRICKET CLUB. Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN _CRICKET CLUB. Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of this club was held at the Viaduct Institute on Monday even- ing. Mr T Ormand presided, but there was only a poor attendance of members. Mr J H Alderson (secre- tary) read the minutes of the last general meeting which were duly confirmed. He then read the annual report, which was as follows :—ln presenting my annual report for the past season, I may say that the result of the cricket was as follows :—Firet eleven played 17, won 6, lost 6, drawn 5, as against 7 won and 4 lost in the pre- ceding year. The number of runs scored was 1858 for 146 wickets, averaging 12106 per wicket ; our oppon- ents compiling 1903 runs for 153 wickets, giving an average of 12.67. In the year 1900 our per nentage in batting was 1528 whilst the wickets we obtained cost 13.143 each, These figures show a slightly worse re- sult in the games won and lost, and also a drop of 3 runs per wicket in our batting. The bowling though showed up rather more tavourably, as we got our oppo- nents out a little cheaper than in the previous year. In batting, J Miller again heaas the averages with the excellent figures of 337, the rest ef the team showing a fairly level rank in batting. With the ball, H Davies showed a great improvement, having taken 43 wickets at a cost of 11 runs each, as against 20 wickets averaging 2017. in the previous yet,. The second eleven played 15 matches, won 8, lost 5, drawn 2, as against 6 won and tour lost for the season previous showing a very creditable improvement. The income for the year amounted to £49, includine a balance of 4s 5d and the expenditure was £47 5s lecl, leaving a balance in the treasurer's hands of £1 143 2d. Sub- scriptions realised £3O 15s .„6d. as comoaeed with £29 lie. 61. and the gate receipts £ll 93 4d as against £l2 123 id. The four bate generously presented by the Institute Committee, have been awarded to the following: W H Crowther, J H Alderson, J W Burrell and W Harrison, and the grant of £2, also kindly given by the same committee, hes been divided between R A Robinson, H Davies, W Morse and J Marsh. A new feature this year has been the prizes awarded for fielding. There seems to have been a fairly strong competition as to who should come out on top, and tne committee ill awardwg the oree,,s to W H Cro wther for the first eleven, and W Morse for the second eleven, believe they have done so to the satisfaction of the players in general, as few will deny that both the players named have got through some good week in that department of the game. One of our old olayer3 W Jenkinsen, who had completed 10 reasons with the club and who had worked very hard, and ,lene all in his powerlfer the benefit of the club, was granted the home meteh against St Helens on August 31st as a benefit match. The result was:highly gratifying, as the treasurer had the pleasure of handing to Iva) the sum of £l5. Matches have been erranged with Wigan, Bol- ton, Warrington, Liverpool. St Helens,Leigh. Tycilesley, Mend:teeter Clifford, Great Harwood and St Helens, Rena We have the services of all last season'e players with an addition'or two.which it:is hoped will strengthen one or two weak places in the team. Lam pleased to say that the ground looks very promising for the time of the year and there is every prospect of MIS good crickets being forthcoming, which reflects great credit upon Mr Josephs, the groundsman. Mr J Owen moved the adoption of the report and the statement of accounts and said he did not think they had much to be enthusiastic about in the playing of matohee. The play might be better, but it could be a lot worse. The general public did not, he thought, take into account the fact that they were playing much better matches now than formerly, and they were relying rnos.tly upon their own young players. The public were also not as generous as they might be. If the club relied upon the outside public they would have ceased to exist long ago. There also seemed a tendecey upon the part of the honorary members to withdraw their subscriptions, and the club could do wite many more members. The gate receipts, unfortunateis were down, although the club played much better matches, and he hoped there would be a much larger attendance at matches in the future. (Hear, hear.) Mr Rylance asked if it was not possible to have one day on which all spectators should pay, whether mem- bers of the Institute or not.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman said it was one of the conditions of the wernberihip of the Institute that the grounds should be epen at all times to the members, consequently they could not close the grounds against them, without alter- ing the rules of tne Institute. Four-fifths of the spec- tators were undoubtedly workmen and that went to show that the general public were not t•upportittg the club as they formerly did. The gate money wis handed over practically in bulk to the club, or in other words the Institute Comrnit.:ee gave then* two-thirds of the gate receipts, and if they aided to that, the £2 given for prizes, and artier donations which came from the Institute Committee, in other ways, he thought they . .</p>
        <p>would see that they got more than full gate money dur- ing the season. It was gratifying to note the progresi which had been made by a young member, Mr Davie,. They were glad to see he had been awarded the prize for the first team bowling. The averages, considering the status of the clubs they had played against, were very good indeed, and ought ta encourage to do bttter. They must keep up to the status of the clubs that they asked to make matches with. It was gratifying that the prizes offered for fielding had been taken up with mo e enthusiasm. He should have very much pleasure jo continuing his prize for another year on the same con- ditions. (Applause.) The election of officers was then proceeded with, Mr J W Emmett was re-elected priaident ; Mr F Harwood, vice-nresident ; Mr T Orrnands treasurer; Mr J H Alderson, hen secretary, to whom a vote of thanks was accorded for his past services. Captain, let team, Mr C W Emmett; vice-captain, Mr G Husband. Captain, 2nd team, MrT A Limond ; vice-captain, Mr F J Beade. Auditors, Mr 3 Gooding and Mr H Tarbuelt. The fol- lowing were appointed the committee :—Messrs J Owen, James Seddon, T F Roberts, J F Heade, J W Burrell, Jame; Sanders, G Husband, R A Roninma, G The usual prestntations were then made for profici- ency in batting and tincvlin in the respective elevens. Mr T A Limond announced his intentioa of giving a prize again this season for fielding. I It; was decided to have a dinner in connection with the club, at the opeuiv of the season, the date to be</p>
        <p>decidel by the Committee. A vote a thanks to the Chairman, moved by Mr J. Owen, vended by Mr T F Roberts, was heartily aeoor- ded, and thie terminated the meeting.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Among those the kin-g,-o-n-the recommendation of the Lord Chancellor has appointe I as his new King's Counsel is Az. Arthur Powell, eldest brother of the Rev F E Powell, M,A., Vicar of St James' Warrington.</p>
        <p>BLIND PEOPLE havelhad their sight 30,000 restored by MELLoR, 168. Oxford-street Manchester. The most successful Eye Specialist In England. He has cured after Hospitals, Doctors, and, Oculists have faller Book post free on men coning this paper.—Advt. 'A Message from Mars." " A Message from Mars," the delightful Way that is now enjoying a longer and more successful run than any English comedy of the present time, is announced for an engagement of one week at the Court Theatre, commencing on Easter Monday night. The plot of the story in this deeply inter- esting play, is one of most exceptional originality and charm. The way too in which the Memsenger's appearance upon the scene is brought about, is said to he one of the best bits of stage management invented by a modern play-writer. The sense of mystery is wonderfully well conveyed, and the illusion made absolutely perfect. Mr iMouillot's now celebrated company, including as it does several very well known London actors, should make a very welcome attraction at Warrington during the coming week.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATURS.—Ad". Mr. A. H. Crosfield at Cannes. -- •</p>
        <p>We have received n copy of the European edition of the" New York Herald," published in Paris, and from it we see that Mr A 1-ICrosfield has again been distinguishing himself at golf. A great many cele- bride are now at Cannes. and considerable interest appears to have been taken in the competitions in connection with the Cannes Golf Club. Mr Cross field, who is to be warmly congratulated upon his success, carried off the captain's prize, namely a large silver cup, his aggregate being as follows : Total 79, handicap 3, net 76. The report of the competition winds up with the following amusing descrip'ion of a quarrel between two automobilist: —About mid -afternoon two automobiles—there were 16 at the Ilub—going in opposite directions got their wheels locked just inside the mimosa avenue. The machinists forth with used language and called each other names. Then the big one shoved the little one into the ditch,and the outlook was stormy for a moment. But the latter got up without other signs of fight than tempestuous remarks, and the sky cleared happily</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday Field Marshal the Duke el Cambridge, the only surviving grandson of George 111., entered upon his eighty-fourth year. He was born at Hanover on the Hal March, 1819.</p>
        <p>'MOTOR CAR FATALITY.—On Tuesday afternoon an elderly man was knocked down by a motor car in High Oueegate, York, and rt,cveri injuries which resulted in his death an hour later. The rnor.or car i 3 the prop`Nrty of the British Power, Lighting, and Traction Company, whose works are on Hull-road, and was beino driven by a man named John Sooter. The deceased appaared to have been unaware of the near approach of the car, and when the driver sounded his horn he seealed to become bewildered, and, being unable to regain the footpath, was knocked down by the car and dragged a few yards. The deceased Was conveyed tc the Councy Flospital, where he died as stated. One of his arms was broken, and there was a scalp wound, but beyond this there were no external marks of injury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Recognition of Mrs. Ward's Services. The Chaplain Vacancy.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Recognition of Mrs. Ward's Services. The Chaplain Vacancy.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Board Of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednes- day. Mr A J Jolley prssided, and there were also present—Dr Patterson, Mrs Clay, Revs Father Wil- son, J H Riddette, and T R Matthews; Messrs G Pilling, A Jolley, J Billinge, T II Asbcroft W Hick- man, A Brooks, J Clarke, it Forshaw, T Halton, W Houghton, G Mather, J Vayton, T Ritchie, J Robin- son, J W Knowles, J C Sutton (clerks, F Gate (the newly appciated master),A Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools.)</p>
        <p>AN INSOLENT INMATE. The proceedings of the House Committee included a minute referring to a complaint made by an inmate named Charles Groves to the Local Govern- ment Board. The Clerk was instructed to reply that Groves was very insolent and insubordinate when in the house, and quite able to earn his own living outside. It was decided that the tender of Mr George Hoult for the repairs to the male lunatic bath at £5 l7s 61 be accepted. The minutes were adopted on the motion of Mr BROOKS, seconded by Mr PILLING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RELIGIOUS "BREEZE."</head>
        <p>A RELIGIOUS "BREEZE."</p>
        <p>The CLERK read the following letter from the Rev E it Tarbuck Dear Mr Sutton I shall be resign- ing the incumbency of St Paul's in about a mouth's time. I beg to send you formal notice chat I shall resign my position as chaplain to the Union on the 15th April. I should like through you to acknow- ledge with the deepest feeling of gratitude, the uniform cßirtesy and assistance of the guardians and staff, from yourself downwards, whicm has ten- dered my work much easier and pleasanter than it might otherwise have been. My relations with the officials have been of the pleasmtest and I have deeply appreciated their constant kindness. May I suggest that in the appointment of my successor the Guardians might possibly see their way to add £5 to the stipend in order to enable the new chap- lain to appoint and pay for a regular organist. I heartily recommend Mr Bracecamp to the trustees 1 of St Paul's as most suitable for appoint'inent to the ' living, and as he has been appointed I have great pleasure in recommending him for the chaplaincy. The CHAIRMAN gave notice that at the next meeting of the Bosrd he would incoe that the Rev Bracecamp be appointed chaplain. The Rev J El RIDETTR said be thought the HOMO Committee ought to take into consideration the possibility of securing the spiritual miniscrations of some clergyman in Warrirgton gratuitously. The Nonconformist sinmates were attended to gratuitously, and he thought there should be some equalization—some re-arrangement—of the amounts paid to the Episcopal chaplain and the Roman Catholic chaplain, It seemed to him a scandal— _ .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN (interposing) said the rev gentle- man had no right to make a speech at that stage. The Rev Jfl RIDDRTTR Then I give notice that I shall propose an increase at the next meeting in the salary paid to the Roman Catholic Instructor and will also ask for the number of inmates in the workhouse who belong to the Roman Catholic Church. as well as for the number to whom the newly-elected chaplain will have to give his minis- trations. It seems to me unfair that the Rev E R Tarbuck should nominate his successor. NIL. A JOLLEW : He does not nominate him. Get off with that soft talk. (Laughter.) W KNOWLAIS : It; seems to .ne that we are going to have a Christian warfare before long. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RATING OF MACHINERY.</head>
        <p>RATING OF MACHINERY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK intimated that he had received a communication in reference to a Bill, now before the House of Commons, which had for its object the relief to some extent of the rating of mathinerv. It was called the Rating of Hachinery Bql, 1902, It was an old imeaiure,having been brought forward for many years, and the Guardians had always petitioned against it. The CHAIRMAN expressed the opinion that there was no chance of the Bill passing. . . .</p>
        <p>, It was decided to take no action in the matter. WARRINGTON'S TJNR/sIPLOYED.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC said he had received a letter from the Secretary of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council forwarding a resolution " That this Council demands that the Town Council and the Boar r' of Guardians do take into consideration the question of the unemployed in Warrington." lie had written acknowledging the letter' The CHAIRMAN : There is nothing else to be done, I think. The matter was not further discussed, SCHOOL ATTENDANCE OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr.tIALTox moved the re-appointment of Messrs</p>
        <p>F Wright and I` Ef Ankland as school attendanes officers until March 25th, 1903, at a salary of £25 each per annum, as heretofore. Mr BROOKS seconded and the motion was agreed to. EtALF-YEARLY ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>The CLERK submitted the statement on the esti- mates for the half year ending Michmlnaas, 1902, amounting to £14,537 54. "The estimated expendi- ture,"he said,"ehowi continuous increase under cer- tain heads, as those of vaccination fees mainten- ance of lunatics, &amp;c. The qvulual rise from Si 2d per head per week in 1899, to Os lid seriously affects all Lancashire Union accounts. I. propose to continue, however, in the next half-year, the usual Gd poor rate. The last call upon rural parishrs, without School Boards, was one of id in the made at Alichailmas, 1899, to meet expenditure by the School Attendance Committee. A penny is now</p>
        <p>again a necessary call upon the same parishes, it is estimated that this penny will cover the outlay for at least the next two years, It is desirable to explain the items appearing under tha head of superannuation allowances. Until the passing of the last Superannuation Act, whereby reductions frow officers' salaries for the creation of a superannuation fund are enforced, all such allow- ances to retiring officers were solely charged upon the poor rate, ye: in no case was such allowance ever withheld in this Union if the Gurdians were empowered to make the grant. The operation of the more recent Act shows that whereas a total sum of ..£5Bl has been deducted from salaries and ' has passed to the credit of common fund the actual payments of superannuation allowance to retired officers, entitled under the Act, total £195 149 sd. Hence the common fund has been.to this date,bene- fited to the extent of £385 Se 7d."</p>
        <p>On the motion of the CHAinliaN, it was resolved that the estimate v of expenditure be approver), and that the Clerk he instructed to prepare contribution orders upon the several townships in accordance chArewith after conclusion of revision of valuation li4s and supplemental lists and approval thereof by the As.esetnent Committee. MRS. WARD'S SERVICES RECOOKISED.</p>
        <p>The following appaared on the agenda: Pursuant to notice sent to each Guardian on February 19th the following motion will be moved, seconded and resolutions passed thereon:—" The office of Work- house Master having become, under the Superan- nuation Act. vacant. Mr Ward being incapable of discharging the duties of his office with efficiency. owing to permanent infirmity of body, and the office of Workhouse Matron having also be- come vacant owing to their joint appointment, and the Guardians having, as recorded on their minutes of December 4th last, decided upon favourable consideration being given to any request by the matron for a reasonable addition to the number of years of her service under the Poor Laws, such consideration will be given to same and, pursuant to notice given at the Board Meeting held on January 29th, the Chairman of the Board will move, and Mira Clay will second al motion—" That the office of Matron of the Unionl Workhouse being vacant by reason of a vacancy in the office of Master, that a number of years, not exceeding tea, he added to the length of service of ' Mrs Ward. under the Poor Laws, for the purposes of superannuation allowance and that the Clerk be instructed to apply to the Local lrovernment Board for their sanction of such increase." The CHAIRMAN moved, and Mrs CLAY seconded, that the number of years to he added to Mrs Ward's period of service should be ten. Mr ASHCROFT moved, Mr CLARKE Seconded, and the Rev J H RMDETTE supported ala arsendment that the number of years to be added should be only five, the latter pointing our, that the joint pen- sion would, if the amendment was passed, amount to £143 per annum. The resolution that the period of service should be increased by 10 years was eventually carried. MASTER'S CLERK. The CLERK said he had received 30 applications for the position of master's clerk, and most of the candidates seemed to be able and experienced men. The salary offered was £4O a year, with the usual privileges. , It was decided to refer the applications to the Master for his consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>TENDERS.</head>
        <p>TENDERS.</p>
        <p>The following tendt-4-s-s-v;i7e-accepted El Singleton ; bread. Mr James Hallwood ; leatner, E Pickavance and Son; Hour and oatmeal, Carter and Son ; coffins, Mr J Massey ; atone, Warrington Slate Company ; straw and potatoes, Mr J Cliffs ; shoes, Mr A Taylor; coal, R Evans and Co. ; clogs, Mr William Jones; glazing, Mr Hazledine. The tenders for drapery, grocery, brushes and drugs were referred to the House Committee, This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PARISH MEETING.</p>
        <p>Should Stockton Heath be an Urban District.</p>
        <p>The Coronation Celebration. There was a fair attendance at tem annual parish meeting of Stockton Efeathaieki in the National Schools. on Thursday evening. Mr J H Clarke presided and there were present the members of the Parish Council and a number of ratepayers. The Chairman said titre were no resolutions to be put • forward at that mcieting,but they were obliged to call it. in accordance with Act of Parliament. Any ratepayer was at liberty to bring forward matters affecting the parish or to ask a question relating to parish affairs. Previous t 3 this eyear, the election of the Parish Council had taken place at this meeting, but by a recent Act of Parliament the election was fixed to take place triennially. He proposed to refer to several matters which had eneaged the attention of the Parochial Come mittee and the Parish Council. Alluding to the making of private streets, he said the District Council had been strongly urged to take in hand the making of Bostock- street and Gaskalastreet. Tnisy dealt with tie matter and carried it to the extent of letting the contract. Application was made to the Local Government Board to borrow moeey for the making or the streets, but they replied that the parish of Stockton Heath had never had rowan granted to it to make these st mete. It was originally granted to the Parish Council of Apple- ton. That was some }ears ago, and since then Appleton had been separated trom Stockton Heath. It. was there- fore necessary to make a fresh application to the Board. That application was made and granted, and the whole routine had again been gone through. He hoped that before long to making of the streets would be com- menced. Besides these streets, there were other newly. formed streete which Were in a disgraceful state. These had not been overlooked. The Parochial Committee had the question under consideration and they would decide what streets should be made. They were determined that there should be no delay, because when the streets were better the health of the township would be better. (Hear, hear). Much attention had been given by the Parish Council to the proposed light railways, more especially to the pre- posal of the Warrington Corporation. The Parish Cmncil discussed the whole of the draft order, and sent to the County Council 40 proposals of alterations and amend- meals. Most of those were adopted. The Parish Council sent nine or ten separate objections to the Corporation, and they were adopted with the exception of three. Those were the width of the roads, the fare from Victoria-sqaare to the centre of Warrington and the question of luggage traffic. They considerea there would be very little chance of succeeding with the third objection, as the Northwico railway had already been agreed to by the Commissioners. They. however, persisted with the other two objections, and he was glad to say they succeeded wholly with reeard to the penny fare, and partly with regard to the width of the roads. The Commissioners agreed that tee proposal of penny fares was a reasonable one and raid it would be granted. With regard to the width of the roads the Corporation were obliged to purchaie property for that purpose. The width suggested by the Parish Coun- cil was 50 feet and the: Commissioners adopted that width. There were, however, certain points where that width could not be obtained, but the Corporation were bound to provide a roadway of 32 feet and also a rea- sonable footpath. The Pedal Council thought the Corporatiors might easily make the footpaths very much wider than they proposed doing wed suggestions hail been submitted Co the Board of Trade. Another import- ant question which bstore long would affect that district was the Education Bill which was brought into the House oa Comneona on the pr( vious night. It seemed that they would be:in the hands of the Cheshire County Council for their educational facilities under the new Bill. It was important that they should see that their interests were safeguarded, and must they got their share of the benefits of the new all. Unfor- tunately they in Stockton Heath were governed by a rural population, wallet to all intents and purposes they were teemselves an urban population. From his ex- perience of the County Council and the Rural Dittrict Council they would have some difficulty to getting all the benefits of the new Education Bill, not only as re- gards elementary education hoe also with reference to secondary ecincatice. They would find that the War- rington children would be educated to a bigli standard and it was absolutely necessary that their schools at Stockton Heath should give An education equal to the standard at Warrington. There was no reason why the education of the children in that part of the district should be neglected. Tho question had been raised whether it was not policy for them in Stockton Heath to apply for powers to become an urban district. It deserved grave and serious consideration. They had now a population of about 3,000, and under the govern- ment of the County Council and the Rural District Council they were not receiving what they should for the rates they paid.. They could not get any improve- . ment. If they applied to the Cheshire County Council to buy a bit of land or improve footpath they seemed to begrudge • it. Recently they applied to the Runcorn Rural Couecil to purchase a piece of land value £4. A numbers ef farmers gct up and said they were not going to buy land to please the Stockton Heath piopie. Nevertheless they were willing to take tbeir ratesialtbough they were rot willing trrimprove their streets to the extent of £4.alt made the inhabitants of Stockton /lea, h feel that they ought to go in for powers to govern themselves. (lear, hear.) The other side of the question was the expense. If it would mean an increase of rates and consequent increase of rents, they might with advantage wait until their ratable value had increased. He hopes to have the assistance of other members of the Parish Council:to go into the whole question and another year the Parish Council would be able to report to the ratepayers what proposals they suggested. Mr Isherwood t Couldn't you give Us some figures now ? The Chairman: We haven't gone into the question. Mr Whitley : lam informed we cannot get urban rowers untd we have a population of 4,000. The popu- iatien in 1892 was 2 543. Mr Jenkins thought it behoved the residents of Stock- ton Heath to be thinking of the well-being and future development of the village. He suggested that a com- mittee of ratepayers might consider the matter in con- junction with the Parieh Council. Mr Hallowes said he was quite prepared to leave it to the Parish Council, He proposed a vote of thanks to the Council for their past services. Mr England seconded and thought the question of seeking urban powers a most important one.</p>
        <p>arrington.—Printed and Published by JOHN Watkaa &amp; Co. at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewesy- street, and published by thesis at the same place and also at The Examiner Office Victoria-road Mink ks 32. George-street. Sr. J1M.E203 and ."5. Market-street EARLESTOWN." in the County of Lencaeterand Oen qt et. RUNCORN,ID the, County of Chester. MAR. 2 7 194</p>
      </div>
